      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, October 7, 1905. s AND REALM CIENCE NOTES AN I GLEANINGS. DTI FqS-FASFIION-HOME.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 7, 1905. s AND REALM CIENCE NOTES AN I GLEANINGS. DTI FqS-FASFIION-HOME.</p>
        <p>2 GROCER McDOUGALL'S Pategt SELF-RAISING FLOUR</p>
        <p>I say. ing household work may be seen at the Electrical Exhibition at Olympia. When we can obtain all our house-warming and do all our cooking by electricity, the prospect of being left servantless will lose half its terrors, and it would seem that the day when this will be possible is not far distant. There is at the Exhibi- tion an exeetric kitchen, presided over by a lady cook, ' who demonstrates what a lut. of d:rt, time, and trouble may be saved by. this system of cooking, and there is also an electric " washer-up "which performs that most unpleasant part of household work. There is again, an electrical equipment, by which a brea'sfast of tea, coffee, toast, boiled eggs, or a grilled rasher or chop, may be provided, and partaken of at one and the same table, without dirt or trouble. A metal jug, to which the current may be switched on whilst in bed, will provide the hot water for getting up, and there are electric footwarmers and 'various other appliances by which comforts, that once mant a great deal of trouble and labour, are now readily obtained. There is no doubt that as electricity advances, and greater use is made of it. for domestic purposes, household work will not be half the trouble and labour that it is now. -</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
        <p>is to</p>
        <p>This intimate that you can Furnish for Cash or on Easy Terms of Payment. at the Lowest Prices in the Kingdoms—That all Goods are marked in Plain Figures.—That the Stock is second to none out of London.</p>
        <p>rATLANTIC WAVES.,</p>
        <p>Careful measurements of Atlantic waves have been made for the Hydrographic Bureau of Washington. It has been found that the ordinary big Atlantic wave averages about 30ft. in height, while those due to severe storms attain a height of from 401 t. to 48ft. The largest wates are often 500 ft. to 600 ft. in length, with a period of ten or eleven sedonds, while the longest recorded had a length of half a mile, and lasted twenty- three seconds. It has been noticed that in general the longer the waves the less is the pro- portion of height to length; in other words, the longest waves have the easiest slopes, and hence in some respects are less to be feared than tl.:e shorter, steeper waves of a sudden storm. • MEASURING BY MILLIONTHS.</p>
        <p>It is many years since the late Sir Joseph i Whitworth, n his millionth-of-an-inch measur- ing machine, found it possible to detect a move- ment of one-millionth of an inch. This result has now been much improved on by an electric micrometer described by Dr. P. E. Shaw in a communication to the Royal Society, with which he has found it possible to detect a motion of one-fifty-millionth of an inch. The device consists essevkally of a series of six compound levers giv- ing Ir magnification of 2,000 to 1, which are operated in combination with a micrometer screw. A telephone and an electric circuit are used to determine when the contact pieces are in touch with each other. Dr. Shaw has used his instrument to determine the sparking distance of one volt in air, which appears to be one-two-mil- lion-five-hundred-thousandth part of an inch.</p>
        <p>' FISH-CATCHING BY ELECTRICITY. A device for catching fish by electrical ap- paratus is a novel application of electricity. An electrical battery is catried on a suitable boat, and placed in circuit with the battery is a net- work of vertical and cross strands composed mostly of electrical conductors which are sus- ppnded in the water from a spar Dr boom that extends out laterally from /each side of the boat. The working of the. invention is simple, but the wiliness of fish may make it non-effectual. , The boat is brought to a narrow portion of a stream or body of water where fish is to be caught, and the nets rre spread out transversely in order to cut off a considerable space of water on each side of the boat. The fish will be imprisoned in the narrow space, and in endeavouring to escape will come in contact with the net. An electrical shock, • stunning them, will result, sending them to the surface, where they may be gathered up by - hand nets. The invention does not seem to admit of extensive practical adoption.</p>
