        <p>THE SICKLY WORKER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>Does Half the Work, Earns Half the Wagcs, And is only Half a Man.</head>
        <p>Does Half the Work, Earns Half the Wagcs, And is only Half a Man.</p>
        <p>Dr_ Williams' Pink Pills Restora</p>
        <p>Full Strength.</p>
        <p>An energetic young man. Mr. Thomas Burling, resid- ing at 7, Old Mill street, Dudley, does not hesitate to tell how much Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People have done for him.</p>
        <p>"I began to suffer," he states. "from severe head- aches and pains in the stomach, which followed the constsnt taking of weakening purgatives. Then a bad attack of influenza so shattered my nerves that I had to give up work for a time. My health became worse; I went off food; my strength seemed broken, and I really feared I might have to give up work altogether—l was such a physical wreck. The prospect was gloomy, when I was induced to try Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. The relief obtained from the first few doses was astonishing. My headaches vanished, I could eat with relish, and new blood began to course through my veins. I can truly say Dr. Williams' Pink Pills cured •me speedily, and that ctire remains permanent. I walk long distances now and tackle work with energy." Every worker and employee, however lowly his position, is a wheel in the machinery that works a house of business. If he is ill, work suffers, and employers finally say," Very sorry, but you must make way for another." All workers must remain in good health. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are a true help to workers. They maintain strength, stimulate appetite, tone the nervous system, and disperse sick heahaches and neuralgia. They cure antemia, general debility, indigestion, bile, rheumatism, sciatica,paralysis, and the effects of overwork or excess. All dealers sell Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People; in Case of doubt I send direct to Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., Holborn- viaduct, enclosing 2s. 9d. for one box, or 13s. Od. for six boxes.</p>
        <p>Ti.tomas By— photograph) Cured by Dr. Williams' Pink, Pills.</p>
        <p>. , 4 . - I .. -------_:_-., . 4., ,\ . 4k, • ,isisoo. 'BUNG (from a plot )</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>CIP.LB AS PUBLIC CHAP.ACTEBS.</head>
        <p>CIP.LB AS PUBLIC CHAP.ACTEBS.</p>
        <p>It is still—ancient as the controversy would seem to be—frequently argt4ed that girls in these days have no heart, because they do not happen to wear them on their sleeves; and are not domesticated, 'since their sole interests do not lie inside the walls of their own houses. These, declares "Lady Phyllis" in the Bystander, are silly assertions; every age has its faults and its follies; but I think we may say, that if we have lost occasionally in one direction, we have gained substantially in othors. We have gained dis- tinctly by coming so much more out into the world, by' being, so to speak, public instead of private characters; . and what is just as im- portant, the world at large has gained also. The idea that girls of the upper classes were to be shut up •in their own homes with so much rigidity lest a breath of air from that wicked place, the world, should poison them, was a narrow precaution, on the part of their guardians and a distinctly selfish one on their own. Besides —though, of course, I ought not to say so—the world cannot do without us; and men, now they have become used to the new state of affairs, find it exceedingly handy to have us released from so much formality. ' NovEL WOOLLEN BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>Already we are beginning to feel the first nip of the cooler autumn days, and to realise that a woollen blouse is a desirable and necessary addition to our wardrobe. .Pictured in our sketch is a really novel and charming blouse of this description suitable for smarter morning occasions, or for simple everyday afternoon wear at home. The sleeves and bodice part of the blouse are of heliotrope delaine, while the inner vest is of pale ivory embroidered net. The bodice is quite plain and fitting, with the excep-</p>
        <p>tion of the front, where a little fulness is drawn up in graceful folds, that curve in lines suggest. ing the figure. A stitched strap fastened with a buckle hides the joining between vest and blouse, while similar straps draw in the fulness of tlte puffed sleeves. A touch of stronger colour is given by the deep purple velvet ribbon passed round the throat an tied in a careless bow in front.</p>
