      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>OUTDOOR RAIMENT.</head>
        <p>OUTDOOR RAIMENT.</p>
        <p>Coats Lave become indispensable, from the travelling ulster or carrick and the dust cloak, in whose folds our dresses keep fresh and crisp throughout the longest journey, to the innu- merable, useful, and beautiful three-quarter paletots, says the Woman at Home. What a woman has to study is , not so much the coat as her own figure, but happily the new styles are legion. We have some very coquettish mantlets, cloaks, and cape collars, which will tido us over the next few months. For late autumnal wear, some of the best houses are making sets which consist of u very large muff in velvet, corded at intervals and edged with fur, and a scarf, which i-3 shaped over the shoulders, made on the same lines, and also edged with fur. This scarf has lung stole ends which fall nearly to the hem of the gown. Other scarves are made with hoods, and the muff affects a heart shape. Many smart mantles in 'beige or sand-colour cloth have yokes of interlaced , cut straps of cloth. Velvet mantles are puckered and befrilled out of all reason, and the consequence is that they look somewhat heavy. Many dark-blue coats show a moss- green cloth under the interwoven straps with which they are ornamented, whilst to others the coloured cloth serves as a background for hand- some black braiding! Silk and velvet are being ruthlessly braided, so that not even, the most regal materials command respect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>BOLERO COAT.</head>
        <p>A BOLERO COAT.</p>
        <p>Some very pretty little models in boleros aro being worn this autumn. At present these are carried out in cloth and velvet, but later we are likely to see much the same styles realised in various kinds of furs. A glance at the sketch will give the reader an idea of the kind of coat under discussion. The one here portrayed is a i dainty little specimen carried out n dark-blue velvet or cloth, having double box-pleats down</p>
        <p>the back, which continue below the I:indicted waistbantl in the form of tabs. The belt dis- appears undernwth the coat at the side seams, so that the froirts hang loosely at will. If cloth is the chosen fabric, the collar, cuffs, buttons, and belt will be of velvet; but if the coat itself is velvet, the trimmings should be of cloth or of very tine suede. Velvet coats are tvorn chiefly with cloth skirts matching in tone; rarely are coat and skirt seen in velvet.</p>
