      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>EXTRAVAGANT PRICES FOR PARIS HATS.</head>
        <p>EXTRAVAGANT PRICES FOR PARIS HATS.</p>
        <p>The prices asked for hats in the streets near the Avenue de l'Opora are higher than those of Bond-street. writes a correspondent from Paris. My companion admired a graceful small black hat, which was decked with rich white feathers. The price was about three guineas without the feathers, and five guineas with them. Very few of the high-crowned shapes were shewn. Toques and hats of mauve felt, heaver, and velvet', are selling freely for the autumn. In August all the chief London shops displayed' new plumage mil- linery, but I saw Halo of this in Paris. At the Elyse° Palace Hotel, where we had tea one afternoon, we saw sonic very beautiful and somo very extravagant headgear.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>FILLETS Or SOLE A LA REINE.—Take sonic flllets of sole. spread them on one side with a layer of stufling, made of hreadcrumbs, chopped parsley, pepper, salt, and cayenne, and a little beaten egg. Roll up the fillets of sole, and tie each one round with tape. Boil them in water with a little salt till tender, drain them, and put them aside. Make a white sauce with a stock, thickened with flour mixed to a smooth paste with cold milk, add a small lump of butter, two cloves, 'Pepper: and salt. Jet this • simmer till flavoured; strain, put ' back the saucepan, add a spoonful of cream and the beaten yolk of rut the cookett ntiecs sole 1011 sauce 'to get hot, but it must not boil after the cream and egg are added. Remove the tape oarefully, and dish the fillets upright, with the sauce round them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>PLANTS THAT SHOOT ARROWS.</head>
        <p>PLANTS THAT SHOOT ARROWS.</p>
        <p>The arrows are crystal needles of oxalate of limo of microscopic dimensions, and are shot from minute capsule.Aaped bodies, found in the tissues of such plants as the Indian turnip and the Polynesian taro.: Dr. Wiley, chief of the Washington bureau of Chemistry, describes the extraordinary spectacle he beheld in the field of his microscope when the " bombs " contained in a drop of taro pulp began to discharge their arrows. Sometimes only one or two needles, and sometimes groups of four to ten were discharged at once, the bomb recoiling as the projectiles left it. Dr. Wiley suggests that the intense burn- ing and pricking experienced in ,chewing such plants as those described are due to the release and discharge of these crystal arrows when the plant tissues arc crushed ill the mouth.</p>
        <p>ABOUT 'ANTISEPTICS.</p>
        <p>The belief that antiseptics can prevent decay is erroneous. Decay may be arrested withoat killing the microbe which would produce it. A few drops of a solution of carbolic acid added to flour paste will keep it sweet for a certain time, not because the germs in the paste are de- stroyed, but because these germs are hindered for the time being in their growth and multipli- cation and because the paste is rendered temporarily an unsuitable soil for the growth of whatever microbes may fall from the air into it. When the effect of the acid is worn off and dis- appeared we see the paste going to the bad as rapidly as if it had not been treated antisepti- cally at all. Moulds will grow in it, and it will 'decay in the 'fashion familiar to everybody. It is in this way cold may act as an antiseptic when it is used to preserve meat and fish. There is no destruction, but only arrest of germ life.,</p>
        <p>COPPER AS WATER yURIFIER. Copper has a marked toxic effect on fiecal bacteria, including the typhoid bacillus, and these organisms are rapidly killed by introducing into the water containing them small quantities of copper sulphate or even by leaving the water for a short time in-contact with copper foil. The minute quantities of copper required to effect this purification, says a New York pharmaceutical journal, would not affect; man, and the higher animals and plants, and moreover, since there are many factors (organic and inorganic precipi- tants) tending to eliminate copper from solution, it is scarcely likely that water, which had been thus treated in a large reservoir, would contain any appreciable amount of this element by the time it reached the consumer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>VALUE OF RESEARCH WORK.</head>
        <p>VALUE OF RESEARCH WORK.</p>
        <p>Under, modern conditions of industry, says the Bngineering .11ecord, it is doubtful if any com- pany whose product represents the combined work of many specialists and the united proper- ties of many variable materials can afford to overlook the possibilities of the research labora- tory in improving the economy of production. No greater mistake can be made than to assume that the end of progress in any particular field is in sight, and • that research is a needless luxury. The possible discovery of new combinations of materials which will result in largely increased profits is never absent; and the ability to in- vestigate at first bawl schemes for, meeting competition is worth -much in these days of systematic analysis of everything frOm the office boy's use of the telephone to the transportation charges upon the finished product. Once a re- search laboratory is established in an industrial plant. however, it should be run on business principles if the maximum good is to accrue from its maintenance; and, although the department may work for months. without appearing to pro- duce valuable results from its researches, the phenomena and materials investigated and the negative points ascertained should be kept in such shape that the company can, at least, see that some return for its money is being made. In the great majority of cases, from the railway system to the factory, results of positive value will be derived from a properly equipped and i conducted laboratory, and there s no question that the future will witness a much more ex- tended development, along the lines of reseafeh for the sake of commercial ends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>MEASUREMENT OF COLOUR.</head>
        <p>THE MEASUREMENT OF COLOUR.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. J. W. Lovibond's theory of colour, which ' has been applied with success in • various scien- tific and industrial investigations, owes its origin , to researches which its author made as a result ! of the inconvenience he felt in being unable to recover a given colour sensation: Mr. Lovibond accordingly invented a series of glass standard scMes, which were co-related to physical colour I constants, and ,also" an apparatus by which a set. of normal conditions may, be realised for mak- ing colour comparisons with a series Of charts in which any measured colour sensation .occupies a position that can be occupied by no other colour sensation; the variation of colour in given substances is their densities increase be- ing recorded by means of curves, thus supplying a means of identifying similar substances. All this led to. the formulation of a new colour ! theory and a code of laws ,deduced from the experimental •facts. Normal white light is found to be made up of • red, orange; yellow, blur, and . violet in equal 'pro- portions: if the proportions are unequal the light is abnormal and coloured. The ; colour of an abnormal beam is that of the one. preponderating ray if the colour is simple, or of the two preponderating rays if the colour is ' complex. The vision is not sensitive at one and the same time to more than two colour rays in the same beam of light, the colour of any other abnormal rays' being either-Merged in the general lumi- nosity of the beam or possessing too low a luminous •insensity to arouse definite colour son. satiothi: Orange,. green. violet, .red, yellow, and blue rays can be separated owing to the normal vision possessing a six-colour perceiving appa- ratus, to which these primaries are equally sensi- tive a ivual ittotiocl; 51thott,g11 structurally reel, yelloW, and blue me trichromes, whereas the remaining thr.m. are "monochromes. Thus the colour elements of a 'beam of White light may be separated. hitt) three triad groups, and the groups into three rays. As red, yellow, and blue mask thbir two associated rays they are called the dominant colours of the system to dis- tinguish them from the subordinates—orange, green. and violet. Each triad group consists of one dominant and two subordinates, making up one-half of the chromatic circle. The comple- mentary half contain two dominants and one subordinate. One subordinate ray in each triad is held in common by the adjacent triads on either side. The colour sensation aroused by a triad may be named at once by normal vision. To :describe differences in colour depth we are forced to have recourse to purely arbitrary ex- pressions such as light, medium, &amp;c. This ;is. .vbere the author's ohm for co-relatinx these sensations to some pnysicai colour constatus comes in. His' glass standard colour scales ars numbered according to colour depth and be. come colour constants on co-relation to percent. age solutions of chemicals such as copper aid.phate, potas iunl permanganate, and the like.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Royal Tour.—The Princess of Wales has been much occupied with preparatious for the Indian tour, for which she 14 taking a wardrobe of some seventy or eighty dresses, arid -consequently fitters and dress- makers have been in constant attendance at Marlborough House. Her Royal Highness also went down to Ports- mouth the other day, to inspect the quarters prepared fer her on board the" Renoun." These consist or four rooms, which have bece beautifully fitted up, and decorated, the bedroom being provided with a swing bedstead. The Princess is never a good sailor at the ' best of times, and will travel overland as far as Genoa,l where she is to embark on October 21st for the voyage to India. The Prince and Princess are due to arrive at Bombay on November 9th, where King Edward landed just thirty years ago, on November Bth, 1875, at the beginning of his Indian tour. Their Royal Highnesses will be received by Lord and Lady Curzon, who will not be leaving India until November 18th, and they will spend about four months in the East, returning the beginning of April next. , The Royal Outfit.—For two -months modistes and their assistants have been workinr, diligently at the magnificent outfit the Princess of Vales is taking with her to India, and now skilled packers are busy, stowing it all away in the special air-tight tin boxes, encased in wood or leather. Considerable forethoneht has been necessary, in the packing of this Royal outfit, owing to the great changes of climate Her Royal Highness will frequently be called upon to encounter. What each place demands must be forthcoming, and to hand, when that place is reached. Save in the matter of full dress toilettes, simplicity reigns supreme, in this wonderful outfit. There are no very full skirts, and no huge sleeves, indeed exaggeration in every form is conspicu- ous by its absence. Walking skirts are made to clear the ground, and every day dress is built with sleev:is that reach to the wrists. The Princess of Wales</p>
        <p>possesses a, boauti ful neck and arms, but she. holds .a</p>
        <p>very strong opinion that neither the armsor neck should be shewn daring the daytime. As far as possible only home-made fabrics and trimmings, or those hailing _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>from the Colonies, have been seledieil by the Princess of Wales, and in the matter of lace, the claims of Ireland have been specially considered by her. The beautiful Notts. and Honiton laces, also play an import- ant part in thescheme of decoration. More toques than hats have been selected by Iler Royal Highness, all being rather small, and close fitting, with high up-standing trimmings. School Girls Fro:ki.—The days when we had to have weight, if we wanted warmth, are happily past and gone, a fact of more importance to the growing girl than anyone. Heavy garments should be carefully avoided for the young people, especially for outdoor wear, otherwise exercise is rendered more fatiguing than it ought to be. The new soft cashmeres and serges, made entirely of wool,and exceptionally light in weight will therefore be welcomed with delight by those mothers who make a study of their dripghter's health sad comfort. It is true these retried' are somewhat highly priced, but then it never pays to buy cheap materials, for the school girl. Cheap serges and zhastimeres cockle up, andspot with rain, quickly wear shabby, and rub through in a marvelously short space of time. Provided a useful colour such as dirk navy blue is selected, a really good serge or cashmere frock will— in the case of the average girl—stand considerable weariand tear, and many renovations, ere it shows con- clusively the ravages of time. Leather Ornamentations.—Leather is seen on onl v one or two of the dresses comprising the Princess of Vales' outfit for India, but these costumes have been snecial ly designed for the East, and will not greatly affect the modes of the coming winter at home. For purposes of ornamentation leather is being largely used, and many of the autumn models are piped and banded with this material. It is also being employed for reveres, belts. straps, pocket flaps, and cuffs, on the new coatees end waistcoats. For motoring, smart-looking boleros are built entirely of soft-dressed leather, which material has some of the warmth, without the ungainly appear- ance of the leathers usually employed for cover-all ' cloaks. Suede-covered buttons, end leather ornamenta- tions are being largely used for tweed costumes, and many women now have all their walking skirts hemmed with hard-wearing leather. Glove.—The smartly-dressed woman almost invari- ably confines her choice to long white kid gloves which are being worn in the twelve and sixteen button lengths, but coloured suedes, if of the lighter shades, look equally as well, and are more economical. It is true that the white kid gloves of good quality, viht clean better, but then they soil so qnickly, especially in the dark and foggy days that they soon arrive at the stage that they will not stand another visit to the cleaners. Many who buy the white washing kid, try the home cleaning process, but this is usually more trying to the stability of the glove than the treatment of the pro- fessional cleaner. Tri choosing suedes, regard should be paid to the colour of one's toilettes, and as a rule it is a mistake to buy the darker shades, such as " choco- late " browns and chestrints. TOOSE) never look well when worn with a costume of equally sombre colour. whilst they are quite oat of place with a gown of lighter hue.</p>
        <p>Women as House Judges.—Mrs. George Cadbury, who ecently presided at a Wemen's Conference on Housing at Letchworth, claimed that women were better judges of house accommodation than men. This is perfectly true, for the wife spends much more of her time in the house than does her husband, and it is only natural that she should have a keen perception of the small conveni- ences of a house, such as the arrangements for washing cooking, storeago of coal, linen, and soon. This in fact is often admitted by the husband, who when it is a question of taking a new house, is prepared as a rule,to acknowledge that his wife is the best judge, in such matters. The point, however, is that in dealing with the housing pioblem, women should be called in to give their views upon architectural construction, and there is every reason to believe that their assistance would be useful. In illustration of this it might be said that if men—including builders, had always had to do the cooking, they would have discovered the advantage of elevating the kitchen range, a foot or so from the door so as to save much of the back-breaking and stooping that is always necessary in attending to it. Electricity in the Home.—Many mos- appliances for</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>THE...</head>
        <p>THE...</p>
        <p>WHITE ROSE.,</p>
        <p>By GERALD BISS.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XV.--f Contsnued.)</p>
        <p>"I wish you'd stop that infernal drumming, -my good Otto," exclaimed Rudolph, sharply. "You can't image how' irritAing it is for -other people.' "So you seem to find it,", said Otto, smil- 'ing gOod- humouredly, as he laid down the offending penholder. Rudolph laughed. " Good old Otto," he said, " it's impossible to ruffle that imperturbability of yours. But • what do you make of this cock-and-bull conspiracy ? " '</p>
        <p>"Candidly, my dear Rudolph," responded his adviser, "I have not had time to come to a mature decision in sO short a period; but my first impression is that we must not allow ourselves to do or say anything rash."</p>
        <p>" Surely you don't take it seriously ? " asked the Prince quickly. "My pretentious to the throne of Great Britain are merely fly-blown theories, which require three cen-, furies of genealogical meandering to sub- stantiate them ! " _</p>
        <p>Otto ignored this outburst and began drum- ming with his fingers in lieu of a penholder. "The Duke of Belsize is a man of great 'influence and standing in England," he went on: "and the list of this committee—the Head Centre. as he calls it—is a very impor- tant one. Why, it contains, amongst others, two Cabinet, Ministers, and one member of ,the late Administration! From this outline, they also appear to have a very good organ- isation—or the nucleus of an organisation •on the ' centre ' system, which is the safest and best. I don't say that at the moment it is by any means sure of success: but there are possibilities in the idea, if it is not unduly -rushed. Certainly, my dear Rudolph, it is not a thing to dismiss too lightly or without • -consideration."</p>
        <p>"But surely, Otto," broke in the Prince, "you do not advocate my lending myself to such a plot ? The thing is utterly preposter- ous, especially in the twentieth century.' "The possibility of the greatest position in the whole world is never preposterous," said Otto sententiously, "if it is attainable." "1 don't think it is attainable," argued 'Rudolph. "I don't say for an instant that it is : but there is no harm in temperisiag, if you do not commit yourself to paper. They run' all the risk: you don't. Davarienburg is very small beer beside Great Britain," he concluded, meditatively. "That is treason, rank treason," objected Rudolph, laughing merrily,' "but I won't dispute your proposition. However, I shall not press my paper claim to a theoretical right. I shall content myself with the small •beer, which is not so inclined to get into one's 'head." '</p>
        <p>" That is the easier and, perhaps, the wiser, tourse," said Otto, smoothly: "but it must be managed diplomatically. As a matter of fact, if I may offer my hnmble advice-" "My dear old chap," interjected Rudolph -deprecatingly. "I should say that no harm can be done by further examining this business-like, though astounding, proposal, so long as you do not -commit yourself. It would be as well to look into the real reasons for this plot awl to see ia carical Ev,er gpse tja_ heat ning L:Tf the world people ILL L.., .</p>
        <p>.love-cl to play at -kit 7-makini-: and some- times their plotting has offered an oppor- tunity worth taking." Rudolph frowned ominously. , . . .</p>
        <p>"Mind you, my dear .Rudolph," Went on. Otto hastily, "I am not advocating that you should take this extraordinary letter too seriously; but, on the other, band, I do not wish you to distuiss it too lightly. By -doing so, you -would only snub a body of influential people without , benefitting yourself in• the slightest. The least you can do is to let them down lightly by showing , an intelligent in- terest in their plans and by refining in such a way as not to give offence. They seem serious about it : and, whatever their reasons for desiring a Charge of dyna,sty or a reversion to the Stuarts, they are running no small risk by their action. This in itself demands courteous treatment. Remember, it is for your sake." Rudolph .continued 'to. frown.</p>
        <p>" It's srubbith, crack-brained rubbish, my dear Otto," he said, in a petuiant voice: "and I have no patience with it." "I daresay you • haven't," said the other, a trifle more sharply. "you never were conspic- uous for that quality." -</p>
        <p>This -sally brought. 3. smile to the Prince's face, and Otto went on : -- "Of course, it .is . too early to discuss the question of your going to-London to meet the Members of this Head Centre, if such a step should ever become advisable. That I would not even suggest as a remote possibility. All I say is that it is necessary to be courteous and conciliatory." The response was a grunt of dissent. "The thing which principally impresses me is that their organisation and methods are businesslike: and they are, obviously sincere, whether deluded or not."</p>
        <p>"Probably each of ~them is paddling his own little canoe," objected Rudolph ungra- ciously.</p>
        <p>" Perhaps so but that remain .4 to be seen. Anyhow, their letter demands an answer, and it will be easier and more satisfactory—not to say more diplomatic—to give it verbally to their messenger, this Mr. Frazer, who will be here in less than an hour's time, than to put even a strong refusal on paper. Remember, that wou!d be tantamount to a formal receipt for this letter." •</p>
        <p>"Well, I shall not see him," said the Prince, decisively. " Then I shall have to," said Otto, with *anal decision.</p>
        <p>—"See :um, it you insist," agreed the other, "and tell,him to go to the devil or back to England, or anywhere else he likes." All right," said Otto smiling, having gained his point. "I will give him your truly courteous and kingly message: but I shall take the liberty of paraphrasing it." . . . . . .</p>
        <p>Rudolph laughed his boyish laguhr , "What a holy terror you are, my dear old Otto You might be the heir to the throne and I your humblest dependent by the way you boss me and insist on having your own way."</p>
        <p>a That's only because of your real goodness, my Prince, and because of my love for you," broke out the usually undemonstrative Otto, impetuously, speaking seriously. "You know I would give, my life for you, or to further your interests."</p>
        <p>It was not often that his friend openly burst into sentiment: and Rudolph always felt shy and awkward at such times. "Don't talk bosh, old man," was his char- acteristic answer: but it was toned down by an affectionate grip of the hand. "This damned business is getting on my nerves. I'm off for a gallop." "All right; I'll look after things while you are out."</p>
        <p>Rudolph opened the door, and stood with his hand on the handle.</p>
        <p>My kind regards to Mr. Frazer," he said whimsically. The door banged behind him in a way sug.' gestive of a boy let out of school. "Dear old boy," murmured Otto, as he resumed his seat at the table, but not his writing. . .</p>
        <p>He put his elbows on the blotting-pad and rested his forehead in his hands. Letters seemed too trivial at the moment; and he wanted to think. Thoughts, visions, hopes, ideas were pounding through his brain in a medley; and he was waiting for them to settle themselves.</p>
        <p>After a few moments of deep thought he sat up in his high-backed chair and lit a cigar- ette from force of habit and a feeling of tobacco-hunger. Whet n TrOCTI.,t ." T,P ril,rmPred to him- self. "My God, what a prospect! King of Great. Britain and Ireland after the small beet of Bavarienburg, a mere principality! It's too big a thing, it's stupendous: but I must see what can be done. 1 would do anything to see old Rudy "—here he reverteil to the boyish pet name—" in such a glbrious position ; but, if done at all, it will have to be done without his knowledge, if not actually against his will. But it's worth a lot of bother and trouble and—and risk,"—hls eyes glowed—" and I won't let it drop toe easily, if—if only there's a real prospect. 1 must sound this Frazer and see how far it's really serious, and if it's worth while, I'll go Dyer. myself, and spy out the land. Rudy's too impatient to investigate things properly." He rang a bell on the table and a man en- tered.</p>
        <p>"An English gentleman is coming at noon to see the Prince," he said to the groom of the chamber : " and his Royal Highness has de- . . .</p>
        <p>puted me to sce him on his behalf. Show him into the little audience-chamber, and tell me: but do not, on any account, let him know 'that Prince Rudolph is out. Do you understand ? "</p>
        <p>"Yes, my lord," said the man, bowing as he left the room.</p>
        <p>Otto lit a fresh cigarette and began to work out his plan of campaign. "'lt's a, difficult game to play," he muttered: "but I'll see it through, for old Rudy's sake."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>CHAPTER XVI.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XVI.</p>
        <p>HALF-AN-HOUR later the servant entered the room and announced the arrival of Mr Frazer.</p>
        <p>Otto rose from his chair and drew a deep breath.</p>
        <p>" Now for it," he said to himself, , as he passed through the ante-room to the audience- chamber.</p>
        <p>- He paused on the threshold and drew an- otNer breath deep.' , Then he entered boldly. lan Frazer was standing at the window, hat in hand. As the door opened he turned round.</p>
        <p>Otto walked up to him, holding out his hand.</p>
        <p>"Mr. lan Frazer ? " he asked, by way of introduction. • Frazer bowed. "I am Prince Rudolph," he said, taking the plunge. .</p>
        <p>Fiazer- bent low over the hand extended him.</p>
        <p>":1 must thank your Majesty for so gra- ciously granting me an audience," he mur- mured.</p>
        <p>Otto flushed as he felt Frazees lips touch his hand.</p>
        <p>"To start with, Mr. Frazer," he began, "let Us fully understand each other. At present I an not Your Majesty' : and it is imprudent to make use of such words. Let us, rather. talk the, matter over frankly, with- out the use of unnecessary formalities, as this is a strictly business interview between, friends. You_ understand ? ".</p>
        <p>Frazer bowed. " Come over to the window and be seated,.' said the pseudo-prince graciously,. • He took a, seat beside the Scotsman in the big. windo*.</p>
        <p>-" Nbw we can talk freely," he 'continued, bracing himself up to his part. " I received the letter of your committee this 'Morning: and, needless to say, it 'came as a complete surprise to me. I hardly, .even now, know what to make bf it, -and I thought it better to see you entirely alone, so that I might ques- tion you as to details. In the first . place, how far is your scheme advanced ? " • "Comparatively speaking, it is in its in- fancy, sir," replied Frazer, speaking in hie most respectful and dutiful manner: but; so far as we have gone, everything has been most successful. We have now, been work- ing for several months quietly, but actively, organising centres throughout the country. As you will see from the list of names, our Head Centre in London could hardly be more influential than it is; and in °the! - in Edinburgh, and, I ma-r,</p>
        <p>As far as possible that, too, (s or now desire,. sir," .he agreed :evasively.</p>
        <p>"I hope." began Otto, "1 mean, I trust, that so far nothing of the sort has taken place ? "</p>
        <p>Frazer felt himself cornered.</p>
        <p>"Sir," he replied, speaking 'with dignity, " to our regret in one or two cases it has been necessary'to silence traitors for the sake of the sacred cause of the White Rose."</p>
        <p>Otto's mouth felt dry, and he grew sud- denly pale. "The White Rose ?" he exclaimed. "DO you mean—no, it can't be I" Frazer bowed in silence. A sudden revulsion of feeling came over Itto.</p>
        <p>Go" he said in a strained voice; "go at once I will have nothing to do with the damnable business."</p>
        <p>Frazer paused for the fraction of a second. "It was forced on us to our regret. It was for your Majesty's sake." With these words, uttering no further protest, Frazer began to move slowly towards the door with his eyes on Otto's face. He thought he saw a man struggling be- tween principle and a great prospect a repetition of the treat temptation, when the say, throughout the whole or scotrand, we are equally strongly represented. In addition, many of the ltadin:,-; families throughout the country have been sounded indirectly, with- out their even being aware of the fact ; and reports have been filed in every instance. Naturally our movements have all along been extremely hampered by the necessity of the utmost caution,: but I may fairly say, on behalf of the Head Centre, that we are en- couraged, greatly encouraged, by results up to the present."</p>
        <p>Otto —listened to Frazer's statement in silence. "Then I am to take it," he asked, as the other paused, " that you have a fairly ex- tensive organisation on a perfectly safe basis ? That is to say, that no one is in a position ,to betray the Head Centre, despito the, fact that the plot is comparatively wide- spread.? , "That I can assure you, sir," answered Frazer promptly. "Outside the Head Centre nobody knows exactly how or where they stand, though within a few hours the whole of those interested in the scheme could be informed of any plan of action, and supplied with full details. Nothing hithertc has been made too del)nite, as we have been awaiting your consent and co-operation tc advance our plans further. What we have so far done is to honeycomb various depart- ments, regiments, and sections of society throughout , the country with supporters ; and we only await yout word to take more active measures. We could not, ,under present circumstances, go further: and up to the present we are delighted with results. The Catholics, for instance, are with us in great strength ; and they are no inconsider- able section." '</p>
        <p>"What is it you want me to do ? " asked Otto thoughtfully. "Firstly, we want your endorsement and ycur personal assent to our plans," replied the mainspring of the conspiracy:. "and, secondly, we . want your immediate presence at our deliberations for the development of the scheme. I would, if I may presume to sug- gest, propose that you should come over to London strictly incognito for a few days as the guest of the Duke of Belsize. Then you can go into all the documents and details, and preside at one or more of our councils, thus meeting 'personally and getting into direct touch with the members of the Head Centre. After that it will be some months, if not longer, before we can take final as4im, unless the whole affair can be arranged by a grand coup." "The last suggestion seems impossible, but the other is at least practical and sensible. To the question of my consent I can give no immediate answer until I have discussed the details more fully and thoroughly examined the prospects of success. Therefore, such a visit as you suggest seems to me wholly advis- able ; indeed, the sooner the better. I can come and go freely without exciting suspicion or fear of being recognised, as I am entirely unknown in England."</p>
        <p>" Th&amp; Head :Centre will be delighted by your gracious decision," said Frazer warmly. When may I advise them that your—you will arrive ? '</p>
        <p>"Let me see," said Otto thoughtfully. "To-day's Friday. I will travel over on Sunday night and arrive on Monday. Please tell the Duke of Belsize that I wish to be re- ceived quietly and unostentatiously for fear of exciting the least curiosity or suspicion. I shall travel as 'Mr. Stuart,' which will be sufficient to identify me without advertising me too openly." " I will deliver your message to the Duke of Belsize," replied Frazer ; "and every care shall be taken that your instructions are care- fully carried out. I myself will meet you upon your arrival,, as the duke is so well known that it might cause remark. As you so wisely say, the less ostentation the better at present." Thank you, Mr. Frazer," said Otto "that will be the 'best plan. It will give me two or three, days to think the matter over and weigh up the pro's and con's. One word of caution—be scrupulous not to mention your visit or this. interview with me in this country either inside the castle or out of it." " Your may rely my cliscrr- tion," responaed Frazer, deftly -slipping in the forbidden title, as though by force of habit. A slight frown passed over Otto's face. "Another point in closing our interview," fie went on, speaking nervously, " I trust and hope that everything will be carried through ; without —without bloodshed" Such a scruple stnick Frazer as strong..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, October 7, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 7, 1905.</p>
        <p>Devil led Lariat up to a /ugh place. T. Rim it was a study in psychology, and in his own materialism he did not doubt which would win. He did not understand. "For Rudy's sake " murmured Otto to himself. "Those men did it for a mere idea —not even, for an ideal. I must go through with it—for Rudy's sake." "Stay," lie,said aloud in a hoarse voice.</p>
        <p>Frazer paused, concealing a smile of cyni.:..ism. He made sure that the devil had won. -</p>
        <p>" plans hold good." said Otto, curtly. " Exp.:cf. 'me on Monday. Till then, au terotr, r. Fran-."</p>
        <p>• With a certain feeling of repugnance he held out his han'l to the envoy of the White Rose. Fr'lz,:x• bent over it again, bowing low and</p>
        <p>kissin-; it</p>
        <p>Voor Majesty's most humble and obedient "h said with a touch of triumph in</p>
        <p>serval', t ,</p>
        <p>his v.ce,</p>
        <p>Otto rzng the bell without another- word: and :Frazer 'Nvaa shown out..</p>
        <p>As he rctrard his Steps to the library he that.ll- had indeed taken the plunge, and upon his owe resDansibility. He did not feel entirely comfortable about it, but he realised that he %would have to go through with it—at any rate, with the preliminary spying out of the land.</p>
        <p>He welked up and down the room restlessly waiting for Rua°lph's return. Half an hour later, when Otto had had time to compose himself and his plans, the Prince burst into the room in the.best of spirits. The annoyance of the 'morning had. been com- pletely driven away—driven away by a gallop i through the keen air: and he was n exuberant spirits..... _ _</p>
        <p>ft \Yea, he exclaimed, as soon as he got into the room, "have you got rid of that infernal Scotsman with his plots and mys- teries ? "</p>
        <p>Otto forced a smile which he did not feel.</p>
        <p>"Yes,' he said slowly, "he has gone." "What had the beggar got to say for him- self ? " went on Rudolph with an eager curiosity...</p>
        <p>"Lois," replied Otto with a tantalising breyity. _</p>
        <p>I" GO oo," said the other laughingly: "you axe only trying to tease me.':</p>
        <p>" The tliing- is more serious and has gone further than we thought,!' continued Otto. drumming on the table to his hearts content; " and I had more difficulty in getting rid of him than I had anticipated. And nowl in- deed, it's only a tentative dismissal."</p>
        <p>"You don't mean to say that he is coming back ? " exclaimed Rudolph in dismay. "What a pertinacious brute I" "Well, not exactly," said his companion; smiling at the other's chagrin; f; but /some- thing more will have to be done." " What ? " asked Rudolph curtly. Otto proceeded glued: to report the details of his conversation with Frazer, emphasising certain points and glossing over others. Of the "removal of the traitors," to which his mind was becoming more reconciled, however, he made no mention.</p>
        <p>Rudolph listened intently to the end, offer- ing no comment. I'll have nothing to do with it," he said decisively, as Otto concluded. "1 didn't think you would," acquieseed Otto soothingly • "but the fact remains that something must be done." "You mean that this Frazer won't take a plain 'no ' for his answer ? " asked Rudolph. "To a certain extent, yes; but you must remember that he is only the mouthpiece and representative of far more important persons. A blunt refusal would put him in a very awkward position with them, besides being decidedly., discourteous to all concerned; and, my dear Rudolph, you must bear in mind that this is all being done for your benefit." "I wish people would leave me and my affairs alone," burst out Rupert petulantly. "I'm perfectly capable of looking after -my- self ; and, if I wanted to be King of England, I should say so ! " Otto smiled indulgently, having by this time entirely recovered ,his sang /rod. His mind was fully made up. "When you've done grinning at me," went on the Prince, "perhaps you will be good enough to inform me what you propose to do ? "</p>
        <p>"I propose," replied the other, flicking the ash off his cigarette with the utmost care, as though the fate of an empire depended upon it, " with your gracious permission to take a few days' holiday." Rudolph stared. " Why ? " " It is so many years since I visited London that I think I ought to renew my acquaint- anceship with such an important commercial centre. Besides, it's too beastly healthy over here, and my lungs simply crave for fog—real, undiluted London pea-soup fog." "Do be serious, Otto, and a trifle more explicit," said the Prince. "Certainly I will if you wish it. What I mean is that, if you will make shift without me for a few days, I will go to London as your representative, taking with me, as it were, your power of attorney. There I will go into the whole matter on your behalf. I will find out all the weak points, demonstrate them up with imperturbable suavity and squelch the whole business with pure politeness. Everyone will be pleased, no offence will b• given, and the plot will fizzle out amidst the utmost cordiality and good feeling on every side and die a natural death. Do you com- prehend, my Prince ? • Rudolph broke out Into one of his peals of. laughter._</p>
        <p>41.0 h, Otto, my Otto, you area trump and a brick—to say nothing of a born diplomat I I 'don't want to lose your pleasant company. but I'll give you indefinite leave, and you can start as soon as you like, on condition that you square this business up for me decently Goodness only knows how I should get along without you,' he concluded. "Then thg.' is settled satisfactorily," said Otto, smiling inscrutably; "and I hope that it is not far off lunch-time. I'm beginning tc. feel decidelly peckish 1"</p>
        <p>tTa be confinsono</p>
      </div>
