        <p>tion Act that work had become less necessary than it was thirty, forty, or fifty years ago. He would, how- ever, like to point out the value of education to the working people. He was quite satisfied that so long as people were content to remain in ignorance they could never reap the advantage of their own labour. Educa- tion lay at the root of all success. He would impress upon that audience—because one did not get many I opportunities of saying it—how much he had always valued education because he had found out what benefit 1 _</p>
        <p>it had been to him in his business career..He ;Zia</p>
