        <p>PROTECTIONIST The political world FALLACIES AND THE is once more waking IRON TRADE, up into activity. We have already had speeches from members of the Government, like Mr. BONAR Law, and from former members of the Government, like Mr. GEORGE WYNDHAM. The</p>
        <p>Parliamentary Secretary of the Board of Trade, it is true, did not appear as much in his political as in his official character. He was speaking at Sheffield, and to the annual meeting of th 3 Iron and Steel Institute. But, being a devoted follower of Mr. CuI:ABERLan., he could not help making allusion to the sort of " facts" on which the policy of so-called Fiscal reform is based. He endeavoured to convince the manufacturers that lif their industry has not yet "gone " it is at least "going." Twenty-five years ago, he said, the country was producing as mach pig-iron as it pro- duces now, whereas the rest of the world has in that period at least doubled its production. This is a typicallexample of the Chamberlain style of argumeot. It is the method of ratiocination of the man who can only sea one thing at a time,and whose power of general survey is either lost or dormant. Pig-iron is the humblest of all indus- trial products. It is, indeed, scarcely entitled to be described as more than raw material. But Mr. BONAR Law,Parliamentary Secretary to the Board lof Trade, is content to regard our production of I pig-iron as a test of our national prosperity. His argument is that we are standing still because we are not making more pig-iron than we made twenty-five years since. He should have chosen his audience better. The Iron and Steel Institute knew what nonsense he was talking. Among his auditors there were those who were fully qualified to point out to him that it pays as well to buy pig-iron abroad, and then to turn it, say, into knives and forks. The prosperity of Sheffield, said une of the subsequent speakers, and the very high quality of Sheffield goods is founded upon material which is brought to us from abroad. "The more material we get from Sweden," he added, meaning pig-iron, "the better pleased</p>
        <p>Sheffield will be." If Mr. Bomen Lew intended</p>
        <p>anything by his remarks, he surely meant that the production of pig-iron having been at a standstill for a quarter of a century, the characteristic industries of the great Yorkshire town are in a similar state of stagnation. But what are the facts—the sort of facts ignored by the Tariff Reform League? The Lord Mayor of Sheffield gives us the reply. Sheffield is producing more crucible steel than has been produced heretofore in any part of the globe. A wall of tariffs, as Mr. CnAmbnutArn. never tires of insisting, has been built up against us almost all the world over; yet the trade of Sheffield advances year by year. No protective duties are high enough to be prohibi- tive. As the production of pig-iron is now what it was twenty-five years since, Mr. BONAR LAW would have us believe that our industries are being taken away from us by the foreigner. But he should not have gone to Sheffield to say so. Sheffield has a population of 4430,000. If he will inquire what it is living upon he will probably, in future, take care to talk no more rubbish about pig-iron. The old saying is that statistics can be made to prove anything. Of course they can, if they are employed as Mr. BONAR LAW, Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, and the Tariff Reform League employ them. If, as a nation, we were dependent upon our produc- tion of pig-iron we should be now, beyond doubt, in much the same position in regard to population and national prosperity that we were in twenty- five years ago. But, of course, everybody knows that this is not the case. We are not stagnating because the people of 'Other countries I are making more pig-iron. We buy their pig-iron from them, and turn it into ships, into engines, into bridges, and into knives and forks. Mr. BONAR LAW is supposed to be one of the most brilliant members of the Government. What, then, must be the intellectual condition of his colleagues when he—representing the Board of Trade—is capable of believing that our national advance is to be measured by our production of pig-iron? A schoolboy, only half as clever as the famous schoolboy of Lord Macauley, ought to know better, and surely it is time that members of the Government ceased to make themselves ridiculous, and to lower the national credit by going about the country "crying stale fish."</p>
        <p>There were days VETERAN when the outlook CO•OPERATOR. of the Co-operative</p>
        <p>movement Was any- thing but bright in 'Warrington. The bold spirits who had undertaken the venture did not falter, and it was at that somewhat critical period that Mr. A. H. Rathbone—himself one of the founders of the Society—was appointed to the managership. For forty-one years he has fulfilled the duties of the important office with conspicuous ability and zeal and during the whole of that time the Society has never looked back. To-day it occupies a proud position among the Co-operative organizations of the kingdom and:has nearly 8,000 members on its books. How much the Society owes to Mr. Rathbone's sovnd management it would be diffi- cult to estimate, rind the handsome gift of 300 guineas and an enlarged photograph which Mr. Rathbone received from the members on Monday was a fitting recognition of strenuous services. Co-operators are, not unnatur- ally, proud of the success of their enterprise, and the note of self-congratulation was dominant at Mondays gathering. Mr. F. Monks, however, took the opportunity of impressing upon working- people the advantages of education and regretted that the Society had not done more in this direc- tion. If his remarks stimulate the Society and the general body of members to greater efforts in the educational cause, the results should be far- reaching.</p>
        <p>,</p>
      </div>
