      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <p>There are three weeks MUNICIPAL CONTESTS. ye t before the municipal elections, so it is not to be wondered at that the number of candidates now in the field is not great. The Liberals are desirous of contesting as many seats as possible in:order to get their fighting machinery in good order, but so far desirable candidates have I not come to the front with any eagerness. In Howley Ward, as we mentioned last week, Mr, E. Pickavance is a prospective contestant, and he has already received great encouragement by irimerous offers of assistance. There can be no doubt that he will do well when the time comes, if he is called upon to make a fight. In the Whitecross Ward, the retiring candidate Councillor James Evans will again appeal to the electorate with every hope of success. Whitecross is a Liberal stronghold and as a Liberal Mr. Evans has taken a prominent position, while he has also done good work muni- cipally. Bewsey Ward is the only one, so far,with two prospective candidates. It ic reported that the retiring Councillor, Mr. Peter Peacock, will be opposed by Mr. E. Plinston in the Labour interest. As Mr. Plinston is a member of the Liberal executive, and of the Liberal Ward Com- mittee for Bewsey, his attitude is one which will require some explanation. Mr. Peacock has only had two yeas in the Council but during that time he has contrived to fulfil his election promises by attending conscientiously to such matters affecting the masses as cheaper gas, free admittance to the baths for scholars, half-penny tram fares, and a recreation ground for the Winwick-road side of the Ward. Probably in the course of another week or two there will be more signs of political activity On kith sides.</p>
        <p>The Town Council</p>
        <p>LAWSON'S CONTRACT, would probably have escaped, on Tuesday evening, any such acrimonious discussion as en- sued on Lawson's contract with the Water Com- mittee but for Alderman Francomb's ill-advised action. It was within his province to have allowcd the minutes including the resolution passed at the Parr Hall meeting, to pass unquestioned and this would certainly have been the wisest course to pursue seeing that the work done under the con- tract is now being investigated by an expert. There would have been no need for such an investigation had not the contract and its contingencies swollen from £24,000 to £57,000, and it is little use the Alderman re iteratieg the statement that £24,000 was not the actual amount of the contract price. Though c,mitingencies were an understood thing it was not anticipated that they would result in the contractor's claim reaching the huge sum mentioned above,and people must be excused if they persist in considering the excess to be over and above the contract price. If it is not, then the committee and the Council went into the matter with their eyes cloaked. They had no idea that they were committing themselves to any such expenditure, and an investigation is none the 13ss necessary because Mr. Lawson's claim may admit of very con- siderable reductions. So far as can be learnt to- day the bulk of the money has gone in pumping waste water down the ditches—a procedure which has some similarity to ploughing the sands. As an expedient to ensure an ultimately ample water supply it may possibly be justified, bat we shall have to wait Mr. Garside's verdict on the point before it can be viewed with anything approaching equanimity.</p>
        <p>Apparently, from THE STATEMENT OF the nature of the THE CASE, discussion at the Town Council meet- ing on Tuesday, and the attitude of the Town Clark, neither the Council nor the Investigation Committee is going to be allowed to see the state- ment of the case for the prosecution against two members of the Council for alleg3d corrupt prac- tices. This may be a perfectly correct modus operancli in point of law, but we venture to assert without any hesitation or doubt, that it is entirely at variance with the understanding arrived at a month ago between the Mayor and the Town clerk on the one hand, and Alderman P,erpoint and the Town Council on the other. In the course of the discussion on Tuesday evening the Town Clerk created some consternation, and upset all preconceived notions by the following assertion: The Town Clerk: I promised to submit to the Investigation Committee my application to the Attorney General for leave to proceed. That is the only document I intend to lay before you.</p>
        <p>This compels us to revert to our verbatim report of the discussion, relative to the same point, at the Council meeting a month ago, and we find that the Town Clerk then agreed categorically to the state- ment ii)f the case—and not merely the application to the Attorney General—being submitted to the Investigation Committee. -We quote the foll ow ing from our issue of September 9th. The TOWN CLEcx : I understand what you want is that when I have prepared the application to the Attorney-General the committee shall see it. Alderman PIERPOINT : Yes, that is it. Alderman PIERPODTT : I suggest the Investiga- tion Committee should go through the statement of the case with you and if they can make any suggestions, subject to agreement between our- selves and yourself, the whole thing can bo amicably done. The TOWN CLERK: I have not the slightest objection to that, but I should object to any interference from the committee with my conduct of the case.</p>
        <p>In the course ot some remarks immediately following, the Mayor said :--" And now the Town Clerk says he has no objection to the Investigation Committee seeing his statement of the case." Possibly, the Town Clerk intended to agree only to the " application " being seen by the Com- mittee, but he certainly, as the above quotations prove, consented to the statement ot the case itself being submitted to the Committee, and the whole Council understood this to be so. On Tuesday there was a complete volte face to the extreme dissatisfaction of those mem- bers who had pressed for the statement of the case being shown to them. The suggestion that the Investigation Committee, having reported, ceases to be, is at this stage absurd. That Committee was entrusted with a mandate to take steps for clearing the honour of the Council, and is still rightly and definitely concerned with that object. In our Opinion the Committee is entitled to ask that the statement of the case as prepared by the Town Clerk from evidence heard by them should be submitted for their consideration before being forwarded to the Attorney-General. We do not suggest for a moment thAt they have a right to dictate to the Town Clerk how the prosecution should be conducted, but they are entitled to see the case as prepared and were given a definite promise that they should. This eleventh hour refusal is calculated to engender suspicion—a thing which it is the duty of the Council at all times to avoid creating.</p>
