      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>Music In the Park.</head>
        <p>Music In the Park.</p>
        <p>The Legli-street Brass Band will occupy the stand in the Park on Sunday at 3.n and will render the follow- ing progranime, Mr. Fred Podmore conducting : Hymn, " Nativity" ; march, "President," F. Podmore (in memory of the late Mr. Job &amp;gales) ; Moreean, " Lizilietta," Von Blon ; selection, " Beauties of Wagner," Newton ; cornet solo, " Star of Bethlehem," Adams ; selection, " The celobrated Largo," Handel selection, " War March of the Priests)" Mendelssohn ; "God save fae King."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Tho Emperor Nicholas, the Muscovite marve " Dead frost that war turn. I'll give 'em The Hague business again. Hurry up with the dove and the olivo branch !' —" Punch."</p>
        <p>"Mr. Winston Churchill, M.P. for Oldham, has grown a moustache. He has nearly finished the important work upon which he has been engaged for the last three years. . . . '0 —Yorkshire evening paper quoted in "Punch."</p>
        <p>* * * Mr. S. Norbury Williams and Mr. F. W. Cooper were on Monday re-elected as elective auditors to the Manchester Corporation. Mr. Williams had a majority of more than a thousand over either of the other two candidates. * Congratulations to Crtsfield's band ! Although they did not carry elf the grand shiekl at Crystal Palace they certainly brought honour to the town ia securing the second award, and no doubt they will do greater things next year.</p>
        <p>** The National Service Leaguers have approached the Committee of the Appleton Rifle Club for the pur- pose of securing facilities for the use of the rifle range at Appleton during the next season. This is practical politics. It is incumbent on the Leaguers to set a good example and very necessary that they should learn to shoot. As possible conscripts their value Will be enhanced.</p>
        <p>* * The official in charge of the stoneyard at Bootle experienced something like a shock on Saturday last when a deputation of "the unemployed," who had been found temporary work by the Guardians at is. per day, waited upon him with the request that they should be allowed half a day's holiday in order that they might attend the Liverpool y. -EYerton football match.—" Liverpool Post."</p>
        <p>The curse of party and its blighting effect upon our national aficieny is the theme of an important preface by Lord Ilosebery to Mr. Alfred Stead's new book on "Greater Japan: A study in National Effaciengr He says :—" We regard our parties as interesting groups of gladiators. Our firmest faith appears to .be that one will do worse than the other; so we main- tain the other, whichever that may be."</p>
        <p>* * * 'The strength of the British Navy and the incom- parable valour of the Japanese Army represent a combination which no Power will tempt fate by defying."—" Englishman" (India).—Apparently the pre-eminence as fighters which the British soldiers have held for so many centuries has gone to the new nation in the East. We ought to be grateful that our Navy is still considered in the first flight.</p>
        <p>* * * Alderman Bennett has come back considerably and favourably impressed with his visit to Belgium to attend the Associated Chambers of Commerce meet- ings at Liege. At the close of his report he says- with characteristic fervour, that " Liege, the centre of the iron trade, was an object lesson as to how the two ideals of beauty and utility may be combined, a lessor; emphasized by nearly every continental town 1 saw.</p>
        <p>* ; * When proposing the minutes of the Fire Eniee Committee on Tuesday Alderman Smethurst said that on the IGth September " Councillor Wright 'Faye 3 false alarm, which was very satisfactory. The Council laughed, as it usually does when the Alderman is relating things in his own peculiar way. It wag found from the" false alarm ' that the turn-out wag I very good, but the water supply in Mill-lane was verf bad, and the latter is to be remedied. By the way we wonder how the firemen like "false alarms " by inquiring members of Committee? * * * Children are taught such a variety of subjects at school nowadays, that most people will agree with Mr. J. C. Bate, the Cheshire Coroner, that it is extra- ordinary that they receive no instruction as to the measures which should be taken in the event of anyone's clothes catching fire. In the case which called forth this comment, it was proved that the deceased's life might have been saved, had one of the witnesses known how to act promptly. Here is a field in which modern education might well be extended with beneficial rssults to the community.</p>
        <p>* * In Holland (Lincolnshire)the thirsty- soul can get drunk with impunity so long as he does it quietly. The Chief Constable of the district has intructed his officers not to arrest a man on the technical charge of drunkenness if he remains inoffensive in any shape or form. We fail to see how drunkenness can be other than offensive and we hope the police elsewhere will continue to offensive, charge of the drunks. They all require a lesson of some sort and it is to the public interest that the police should exercise a corrective iufluence.</p>
        <p>* * * Mr. D. Lloyd-George, M.P., whose political activity in Wales just now almost rivals the intensity of Mr. Gladstone's famous Midlothian campaign, is earning that doubtful asset for a Liberal the goodwill of " The Times." 'Mr. Lloyd-George' said " The Times "lass week, 'is very much more than a Welsh leader. Re- volts may come and revolts may go, but as long as he commands the support of the Nonconformist con- science he can never be regarded as a negligible quantity in politics, and, so long as this conscience speaks throigh a Welsh trumpet, so long must Wale* be present in politics.' *** Lines suggested by the appearance of the damp but enthusiastic crowd which surged round the door of * well-known musical establishment from an extremell early hour on Wednesday morning. "You may think I'm a Mik— For making such—Ado But, then I want to pick Out a good seat or two. And though I've stood by this 'ere door Throughout the bally night, And got wet through and stiff and sore, I've got my seats all right. HANON HYMOUS. Diplomacy has gained the day and the plenipotea taries of the Education Committee and the Gymnasium' Committee have at last agreed 'upon a scheme for the physical instruction of the scholars in the elementarf schools. For twelve months at least in all schools but two the instruction is to be imparted in the schools by the elementary teachers, with the assist- ; once and advice of the Gymnasium instructors. The . Gymnasium Committee will also provide a class for 1 the training of those teachers who do not ahead,' • possess the necessary qualification, including the purl teachers at the secondary school. For these servlce:4 the Gymnasium Committee is to receive 100 as a year's remuneration. Physical instruction will not, therefore, be a very expensive addition to the school curriculum, and should prove well worth the money We have been asked to remind those of our readers who are on the Committees of Literary or Political Associations of the work of the Eighty Club. The Club consists of a number of gentlemen willing tr) assist the Liberal and Free Trade cause by addressing local clubs, debating societies, or public meetings, awl I also by lecturing on special subjects of current political interest. No fee or expenses will be elnival by the speakers, and in view of the forthcomins General Election, the Club is preparing to sole, speakers not only to large meetings, but also to Wart'. meetings and to series of village meetings. So; Edward Grey, M.P., is the president of the club, anu all enquiries should be directed to the secretary, R. C. Hawkin at 3, Hare Court, Temple, L0nc1ee.....0,„,</p>
        <p>* * The Town Council meeting on Tuesday was a sort of one-act drama—that is it lasted only a little ever one hour, but grew very dramatic before the close. When Councillor Pemberton was thumping the table and making his indictment against the Water Cora- mittee, Alderman Francomb looked on with an ' air of righteous indignation, while the was full of such interjections as " November: "Cowardly," "Lies,' and the like. There were touches of humour, of course. Without them 110 drama would be a success. It relieved the tension somewhat when the Mayor and Alderman Smethar4 plaintively asked Councillor Pemberton to "t5ll Wit something we don't know." He did before he had finished—to the effect that the contract should have been finished in 52 weeks instead of five years. nut these are matters for Mr. Garside, the expert appointed to investigate the works.</p>
        <p>The fires of a new glass bottle making works at Burton-on-Trent were formally lighted on 'Wednesday. The works have been fitted up with special labour-saving appliances, and it is hoped to be able to produce gl!iss bottles and jars at cheaper rates than now exist. Between 80 and 100 workers will be employed at first.</p>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>'Notice. The Warrington " Examiner " is now published on Friday afternoon, price one penny, and is on sale at the various newsagents at about 4 o'clock. Should any difficulty be experienced in getting the paper the proprietors will undertake to deliver a copy at any address in Warrington by 5 o'clock on Friday after noon without extra charge. Address all communica- tions to "Examiner Office," Bewsey-street Warrington Coming Events. A sale of work in aid of the extension fund con- nected with St. George's Mission will be held in the 'Mission Room, Brick-street, on Friday and Saturday, October 13th and 14th. It will be opened on Friday by Mrs. Parr, and on Saturday by Mrs. Macdonald The first of a series of University Extension lectures by Mr. Lawrence Schroeder, M.A., will be given in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, to-night (Friday): I The lectures will be on" A trio of Essayists: Lamb, Hazlitt, and De QuitleeY." Personal. Mr. R. E. Kenyon, M.Sc., F.C.S.; an old boy of the Warrington Grammar Sohool has just been appointed Science Master at the Grammar School, Lecturer at the Technical School, and Secretary to the Technical Instruction Committe at Sutton Coldfields, Birming- ham. Mayoral Dinner. The Mayor (Alderman Bolton) will entertain the members of the Council, Corporation officials, and a number of invited guests to dinner at the Town Hall on Wednesday evening next at 6.30 o'clock. Mr. I. T. Williams. A complimentary dinner is being given this (Satur- day) evening at the Lion Hotel to Mr. I. T. Williams, London and North-Western District goods manager, by his personal staff, in celebration of the opening of new offices at Arpley Station. Meeting of the Progressive Labour League. The first meeting for the winter session of the War- rington Progressive Labour League was held at the Liberal Club on Tuesday evening. Mr. T. Barnes Presided over a large attendance and delivered a brief address on the future work of the League. An excel- lent discussion followed. The subject for discussion at the meeting next week will be the coming November elections. Presentation to Mrs. Dewhurst.</p>
        <p>- _ The members of the Howley Sewing Class in connection with the Parish Church, were entertained to tea by Mrs. James Fairclough,Bailyfield, Latchford, on Thursday Afternoon. The gathering was a particu- larly interesting one, as during the afternoon Mrs. Clifford, on behalf of the class, presented Mrs. John Dewhurst with a small handbag as a token of their esteem. Mrs. Dewhurst has attended the cliQs for some years and her departure from the town will be Minch regretted. Sale of Passive Resisters' Goods, The goods of the Warrington Passive Resisters, seized in default of payment of the education rate, 'were sold by Mr. Robert Lowe, Bank-street on Tues- day morning. The goods included various articles such as bronzes, clocks, &amp;c., and beloNjed to the following: Revs. W. J Roberts and Charles Leonard Stowe, and Messrs. W. Clatworthy, Edward Howard, Thomas bale, Evan E. Hughes and Miss Fieldhouse. All the articles were bought in, the sale being over in a few minutes. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—IINDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST 000T5 IN LANCASHIRIL—AdVt. Bankruptcy Court. At the Bankruptcy Court, on Friday, John Norbury, joiner, &amp;c., 78, Wilderspool-road, appeared for his Public examination. The statement of affairs showed a deficiency of £179 Bs. 11.—Mr. Steel appeared for the bankrupt who attributed his failure to bad trade and heavy business expenses.—The examination ,was olosed.—Frederick Bancroft, baker, 37, St. Mary-st, also appeared for his examination.—The deficiency amounted to £53 10s. Bd.—Mr.Steel having questioned the debtor on behalf of a creditor, the examination was closed. Emmanuel Church Men's Union. The members of the above gave a social evening on Monday in the Church Institute, to which the congre- gation were invited. The Incumbent occupied the chair. A very pleasing programme had been arranged by the committee, consisting of songs, choruses, &amp;c., rendered by the members of the Union. Mr. Arthur bitchfield delighted the audience with some choice selections on his first-class gramaphone. A most interesting item was the transformation of the room into a miniature Bisley. An air-gun shooting corn- Petition had been arranged for the Young Women's Bible Class, which afforded much real amusement The Rev. F. Vaughan handed the prizes to the winners at the conclusion of the programme amidst rounds of applause. The Local Free Trade League. A meeting was held on Thursday evening at No. 5 iluttermarket street, of the local branch of the Free, Trade League,to elect officers and executive committee., ~11.1% Rigby Armitage was appointed chairman and Mr.l '4 Greening vice-chairman. Messrs. H Roberts, N. Greening, Captain G. Crosfield,J. J. Crosfield and Dr.l /C. E. Markel were elected vice-presidents, power being reserved to add to their number • Mr. F. R. Roberts, ann. treasurer, and Messrs. A. and A. H. Rathbone, ifune hon. secretaries. A finance committee was also orrned consisting of Messrs. J. Renworthy,L. Greening, Wright,F. R. Roberts.R. Armitage and A. Rathbone, and all gentlemen attending with a number of others Who were proposed, were appointed on the executive nommittee. John's Literary and Social Society. 9n Tuesday evening the opening social meeting of uns society was held in St. John's Church schoolroom. Th -ere was a very satisfactory attendance of members and their friends. The chair was occupied by the President, Mr. P. 11. Brown, who announced that a rrrY attractive list of lectures, debates, &amp;c., had been ranged for the session then opening, which, he said, Irotnised to be a very successful one. The Rev. J. L. ilne, and Mr. W. E. Bindloss also delivered short addresses during the evening, emphasizing the value 'lf such a society to the young, and impressing upon • .;:ae Young men and women present the importance of ea Young and study. A good programme had been pre- ted, and songs were given by Miss Ditchfield and ssrs. J. W. Jewett and J. Hunter, mandoline selec- ;re by Miss Stanley and readings by Miss Dewar. ,eeea Was handed round during an interval in the pro- The,„gs meeting concluded with the usual "‘ns Of thanks. lea,chers' meeting. The quarterly meeting of the Warrington and Dia- Teachers' Association, was held in the Wycliffe orial Hall on Friday evening of last week, and irk the form of a social gathering. There was a /Fig? attendance of teachers. The President (Mr. S. '_,effery) presided. The first portion of the evening u s evoted to music, the following artistes coutribut- q.ll to an excellent programme :—Misses D. M. Mold, adY, Woodland, and Messrs. Dewhurst, E. Pearce iati(Ltyhitney. During an interval Mr. Jeffery spoke guest terms of the character and the influence vesi 3,earah Mr. Dewhurst had exercised during the last 40 Lon 8 in his profession as a schoolmaster in Warring- and expressed for himself and fellow teachers tt be eere hopes that Mr. and Mrs. Dewhurst would longl ea BPared in health and strength to enjoy their well- retirement Mr. Dewhnrst in replying to a Of thanks to the artistes, thanked Mr. Jeffery and I.elspresent for their kind expressions and wishes. The o a„t the evening was devoted to a whist drive iill,g!d by Mr. I. Bowen. The catering, which was hands of Mrs. Atkinson, gave every satisfac-</p>
        <p>Lantern Lecture. The first entertainment of the season in connection with Ebenezer Church was held on Monday evening, when a limelight lantern lecture was given by Mr. Henry Taylor. Police Court Missionary. Views of England, Ireland and Scotland were shown, as well as some very fine flower, statuary, and animal studies, the whole concluding with comic and mechanical slides. The Lecturer pleased the audience with humorous songs and two recitations. Mr. Henry Thornton pre- sided. A very hearty vote of thanks was given to Mr. Taylor. There was a large attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>KITCHEN RANGES, Fire Grates, Tiles &amp;C., large stook.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—ADvT.</head>
        <p>KITCHEN RANGES, Fire Grates, Tiles &amp;C., large stook.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—ADvT.</p>
        <p>Road Redden's.</p>
        <p>On Monday a bread van belonging to Messrs. J. Toft and Son was being driven down the road from Hill Oliffe to Warrington when the horse, a spirited animal, got out of hand and ,swerved into the gate- way of Mr. Henry Green all's grounds. The van was completely wrecked and the goods strewn over the road, whilst the horse also sustained some injury.— A somewhat serious accident befel Mr. W. Taylor, Brow Farm,Sankey,on Wednesday when driving along the Bold-road to Warrington. By some means his trap came into collison with a motor-van and the horse had a foot taken off.' Consequently it had, to be destroyed at once.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—AdVt. The Motor Tragedy.</p>
        <p>The funeral of Sarah Ann Lawless (13) who resided with her parents at 49, Orford-street, and whose tragic death we recorded last week, took place at the War- rington Cemetery, on Saturday. The school children from Fairfield School and the Sunday school children from Ellesmere street school, which institutions the girl had attended walked in procession and sang hymns at the graveside. There was a large assembly of people along the route and also at the graveside, general sympathy being expressed with the parents in their bereavement. The officiating minister was the Rev. Sidney Pitt. MAIL CARTSAnd Bassinettes, the latest designs. J. S. GARLAND. 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington. Musical Success.</p>
        <p>As a result of the Manchester local Examination (Trinity College, London) of July last, Miss Marie Houghton, of Daresbuty, has been awarded an Exhibition Scholarship, value 9 guineas, she having obtained 06 marks out of a possible 10Y, which is the highest number given to any candidate for several examinations in senior singing. As " Exhibitioner " Miss Houghton stands first in order of merit in singing for the United Kingdom, out of a large number of Ex- hibitions given by the College. Miss Houghton will continue to study under the tuition of Mrs. Hubi- Newcombe, who has already trained her vocal powers to such good effect, and we can safely predict for her a brilliant future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>British Women's Temperance Association.</head>
        <p>British Women's Temperance Association.</p>
        <p>The first meeting of the above for the season was held in the Bold-street schoolroom on Tuesday after- noon. Mrs. Garnett presided and in her opening remarks spoke very earnestly to the workers, saying she was impressed more and more with the evil the drink was doing amongst the people of our town. The Secretary Mrs. Simpson gave a retrospect of the past year's work. New members were welcomed and offices elected, which are as follows :—Mrs. Garnett, president; Miss Bessie Dutton and Mrs. Norman, vice-presidents ; Mrs. Harvey Cook, treasurer ; Mrs. Simpson, secretary; Mrs. Norman, recording secretary; Mrs. Pitt, convener of cottage meetings, and Mrs. Milne, superintendent of literary department,</p>
        <p>ASH for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINIMAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>Exportation of Walton Pigs.</head>
        <p>Exportation of Walton Pigs.</p>
        <p>Three grand Middle White Pigs, a boar and two sows, the property of Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., were on the 23rd of September, shipped from Tilbury dock to New York to the order of a gentleman in the United States, The fine young sow Walton Rose, 37th,16364, winner of the first prize and reserve for the breed medal in the under twelve months old class at the recent Yorkshire show was included in the consign- ment. On Saturday a large breed boar and an in-pig sow were shipped from Parkeston Quay, to Esbjerg, to the order of two gentlemen in Denmark, The res- pective new owners recently paid a visit to the stock of Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., and selected the pigs themselves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS.—Advt.</head>
        <p>UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Oddfellows Progress.</p>
        <p>The financial statement for the year ending 1904, for the Prince of Wales Lodge of Oddfeliows shows that the steady progress which has characterized the work of the Lodge for many years has been fully maintained. There has been distributed in sick pay 'during the past year £326 lls. 6d. The increase to Ithis fund during the same period amount to £175. making a total of £2,908 is. 10d. The Management Fund receipts amount to £l3l 18s. 6d., and the expenditure £124 19s. 70., leaving a balance of £39 19s. 5d., after transferring £2O to the sick and funeral fund. The Benevolent Fund receipts amount to £l2 3s. Gd. and the sum of £6 17s. has been expended in benevolent and other gifts: £lO trans- ferred to the sick fund, leaving a balance of 17g. Old. The total increase of lodge funds during the past year amounts to £157 ss. 41d., thus showing the total worth of the 10dge—£2,964 18s. 4d. The number of members on January Ist., 1905, was 404. H. M. ASHTON'S Lin TONIC takes away that tired feeling.—(Advt.) Exciting Scene at Howley.</p>
        <p>An exciting scene was witnessed at Howley on Monday. Early in thet morning William Hewitt, 11, Albert-place, met a man named Napoleon Bayliss and after bidding him an affectionate farewell proceeded to Howley and threw himself in the river. He was rescued, and arrested. On Taehday he was charged at the Police Court with attempting to commit suicide. The Chief Constable said that for some time the prisoner, when sober as well as drunk, had talked about suicide. On Sunday he went home and said he had just walked round to the river, but he thought he would not drown himself just yet. On Monday morning, at about nine o'clock,his eldest son gave him 2s. 6d. to go to Liverpool and look for work. He spent the money in drink, and then went home, evidently to make a dramatic scene to cover his dissipation. He met Bayliss, and then went and threw himself into the river. A flatman was close by—Tas no doubt the prisoner noticed—and fished him out with a boat- hook. He would suggest that it would be the best course for the Bench to remand him in custody to give him a chance of realizing his folly, and in he mean- time observation would be taken of his demeanour. The Bench remanded prisoner until October 10. H. M. AsTrros's LIFE Toxic takes away that tired feeling.—(Advt.) Infirmary Concert.</p>
        <p>The annual concert in aid of the fends of the Warrington infirmary was held in the Parr Hall on Saturday evening, when a large attendance testified to the growing popularity of the event. An excellent musical programme had been arranged, to which con- tributions were made by well-known and capable artistes, and the concert was in every way a success. The opening item was the Grand March from" Tann- hauser ' (Wagner) which was effectively rendered by Mr. T. J. Down's orchestra. The orchestra, which I was under the conductorship of Mr. A. Down, later in the evening contributed a number of items, perhaps the most successful of which was the concluding gallop "The Post Horn" (Koenig). Mr. W. D. Holgate, a tenor of more than average ability, treated " The sailor's grave' (Sullivan) in a sympathetic manner. His most pleasing item was however the popular song " Come into the garden Maud." Mr. G. H. Ditchburn is well known to Warringtonians, who thoroughly appreciate his powerful bass voice. On Saturday the audience re-called him for both of his contributions, "Oldi happy-go-lucky" and "The Skipper." One of the most pleasing items of the evening was the duet "Tenor and Baritone" given by Messrs. Holgate and Ditchbum, who were re-called for their effort. Miss Bessie Blackburn is the possessor of a sweet and flexible soprano voice which on Saturday was heard to the best possible advantage in "The Glee Maiden " (Solomon) and in "Summer Rain" and " The Cuckoo." The three vocalists were heartily encored for a com- bined effort in " The Gipsies' laughing trio," which was splendidly treated. The humorist, Mr. Victor G. Roberts, succeeded in his endeavour to amuse and in addition displayed considerable dexterity in con- juring with cards. Mr. W. H. Payton Mus. Bac. ably discharged the duties of accompanist.</p>
        <p>I Grammar School Swimming Championship. I The annual swimming contest for the championship belt in connection with the Warrington Grammar School took place at the Corporation Baths on Wed- nesday. There was a large gathering of scholars,and Mr. Young, one of the masters, was also present. There were eight competitors for the race of 150 yards and H. Adams proved the champion. He covered the distance in 2mins. 12secs., and won by about 6 yards. This is the second time in succession that Adams has carried off the trophy and he is to be complimented upon his performance. Mr. Cr. A. Barlow, the baths keeper, acted as time-keeper. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER' TIDIULOCK.BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. Buckley-street P.S:A. Society.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Buckley-street P.S.A. was held on Sunday at which there was a very good attendance. The chair was occupied by Mr. Healey who urged the necessity for more prayer, and by so doing they would 'derive much benefit. Mr. Wynne-Jones gave a brief address. He based his remarks on the benefits of the P.S.A.'s and the good such "societies were doing throughout the country. Mrs. Taylor gave excellent renderings of " 0 rest in the Lord" and "He shall feed His flock," while Mrs. Highley sang "Come unto Me." Miss M. Plinston was the accompanist. Mr. J. J. Fairhurst read the lesson..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>Exarainer, Saturday, October 7, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Exarainer, Saturday, October 7, 1905.</p>
        <p>president, W. DilOorth, and T. Dilworth; Misses Hunt, Dilworth, Ada Bradshaw, Roddy, and Warren.</p>
        <p>OPENING BY LADY ACTON. The opening ceremony on Thursday was performed by Lady Acton. The chair was occupied bythe Right Rev. the Abbot of Ampleforth, and amongst those on the platform were Lady Acton,Mrs. Courtney Fhrockmorton, the Rev. Fathers Wilson, Dr. Mercer, Whittle, Swarbreck, Baines and Primavesi, Messrs. Henry Rigby, G- M. Vernon, W, Dilworth, M. G. Hearne, and Arthur Vernon (secretary). At the commencement of the pro- ceedings Lady Acton was presented with a lovely bouquet by Miss Doris Lightburn. The CHAIRMAN said he had to perform the very pleasing duty of introducing Lady Acton who, at great personal inconvenience, had been so kind as to offer to come and open the bazaar. They all knew Lady Acton well in Warringtonand,without saying anything farther, he would ask her to declare the bazaar open. (Applause). Lady ACTON, who had a hearty reception, said she need scarcely say what great pleasure it gave her to be present that day after an absence from Warrington and from England of over a year. Although, of course. such a very fleeting glimpse of familiar scenes and friendly faces was not without touching remembrances, still the predominant feeling was purely pleasurable. She most sincerely thanked Father Wilson and the com- mittee for giving her the opportunity of meeting so many Warrington friends. She thought she might say that all Warrington, irrespective of creed, was in sympathy with the (pod object which bad inspired the promoters of that bazaar, who she saw, had conjured up a scene of Oriental fairyland reminiscent of the Arabian Nights. It was an object which appealed to everyone, for the hall which they hoped would be erected would be, after the church and school, a most appropriate centre for the life of the parish. Here their young people would come under the most beneficent influences and the refinement and true civilization which were acquired, they would be able to impart to others in later life. The German Emperor once aroused much comment by saying that in order to make a good soldier a man must first be a good Christian. She thought they might safely paraphrase that remark by saying that a man who was a good Catholic would also be a good citizen ---(applause)—for they knew that respect for authority , and a profound sense of the responsibility of the indi- vidual, which were the two main features of good I citizenship, were essential parts of the teaching of their Church. (Applause.) In present company anything in the shape of a speech from her was inappropriate if not a little bit presumptuous, so she would say no more , except what great pleasure it gave her to declare the I bazaar open and she trusted that it might prove an unqualified success. (Applause). The Rev. Father WlLsos %aid the most pleasant duty of the afternoon had been entrusted to him, and that I was to propose a vote of thanks to Lady Acton for her kindness in coming amongst them and to elicit from the 1 meeting some expression of the very cordial welcome which they gave her ladyship. Lady Acton was very well known in the district. (Applause.) He would like to say a word or two with regard to her ladyship's graciousness in so promptly acceedinr, to their request that she should houour the bazaar by declaring it open. When Lady Acton was asked to perform the cerEmony he believed it was arranged that she would be staying in the neighbourhood but unfortunately circumstances had upset those arrangerapnts. Lady Acton had eme to Warrington at considerable inconvenience and for the purpose solely of helping that object. Bazaars had a great many critics and moralists descanted vfzew largely upon the bad or dark side of bazaars. But he thought they would all admit the bazaars had fi bright side—and that was the inside. (Laughter.) If the emulation of the different promoters did create friction and the friction caused a little warmth he did not think very mach harm was done. On the other hand, he had found from his experience of that bazaar that it served to awaken and bring into evidence what no doubt lay latent before—a vast amount of good feeling. (Hear, hear.) They had experienced great kindness and liberality from all quarters. (Applause.) , The Rev. Father Wm= seconded. They in War- ringtori were, he said, very grateful to Lady Acton for the interest she showed in their work. He knew that her ladyship's interest was not confined to St. Mary's or any particular parish but was extended to the whole of the people of the neighbourhood to whatever denomination they belonged. Her family name was familiar to them all and they appreciated 'her presence very sincerely. He I might add that the old church of St. Alban's was pleased to see the eldest daughter—St. Mary's Church— flourishing so much. (Applause.) The vote was heartily passed and Lady Acton acknow- ledged the compliment. On the motion of Mr. J. W. Mc. Avow seconded by Mr. H. RIGBY the Lord Abbot of Ampleforth was thanked for presiding. In responding the CIIAIRM t.'s7 said it was a pleasure to come to a parish like St. Mary's and to see the enthusiasm and unity displayed on all sides. The object of the bazaar was one they might well take to heart. They couldall unite in promoting social work in the parish whatever their belief might be. (Applause). The business of the day'then commenced. THE ENTERTAINMENTS.</p>
        <p>Theprogramme of entertainments is an exceptionally t interesting oneand already the numerous 'side shows" I have been well patronized. During Thursday afternoon and evening Mr. T. J. Down's orchestra, under the con- ductorship of Mr. Arthur Down, stibmitted choice selec- tions, and they will give additional programmes Friday and Saturday evenings. On Friday and Saturday after- noons Miss Beatrice Turner's orchestta will play selec- tions. A series of entertainments is attracting large audi- ences to the crush room.On Thursday evening the bazaar I dramatic company gave a sketch entitled "Dream faces," the characters being sustained by Mrs. Jennings, I Miss E. Royston, Miss D. Newburn, Mr. H. Clambers, and Mr. Bert Riggald. The appearance of St. Mary's midget Rierrots has been a great success. On Thurs- day evening an enjoyable concert was given by the following artistes :—Miss Rigby, Miss Robertson, Miss Sexton, Mr. de Lacy, and Mr. F. Greenough. Equally interesting items will be given on Friday and Saturday evenings, Other novel attractions are cine- matograph and marionette shows, fish pond, fairy well, Indian Jungle shooting gallery, and an amusing art gallery. FRIDAY'S AND SATURDAY'S OPENING CEREMONIES.</p>
        <p>This (Friday) afternoon the bazaar is being opened by Mr. Robert Pierpoint, M.?. The Very Rsv. Dean Finegan will preside. Oa Stturday afternoon Mr. A. H. Crostleld will perf )rm th opening ceremony and Mr. W. Madden, barrister-at-law, will preside.</p>
        <p>TilE PaOCEEDS. - The proceeds on Thursday a nounted to £230.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>Success of Messrs. Crosflelds' Band.</head>
        <p>Success of Messrs. Crosflelds' Band.</p>
        <p>Warrington people will heartily congratulate Messrs. Crosfields] band upon their latest success at the Crystal Palace. Last year at the great anneal contest which was held at London they secured the premier honours in the junior cup section, while on Saturday they moved a step higher and were placed second in the grand shield section, winning £lO and a set of uniform and caps of the value of 10 guineas. The test piece was La Cenerentola." The band is to be complimented upon its achievement and special praise is due to Mr. W. Halliwell, the conductor, and Mr. J. Taylor, the hon. sec., whose efforts have in a large measure combined to bring the band to its present state of excellence. As for CIINNWGHAII'S HIGH-CLASb NIDTBRAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Warrington Esperanto Society The members of the Warrington Esperanto Society held a meeting at the Old Academy on Monday even- ing. Mr. W. H. Woodcock, who presided over a large attendance, stated that it was proposed that, this session, they should hold an elementary and an advanced class in Esperanto, but they would be guided largely by the number of members who might Join. The language had gradually taken hold of the world, and there were some quarter of a million who could write it, and the majority of whom could speak it. It was resolved about a year ago to hold an inter- national conference this year at Boulogne. This took place the first week in August, and had proved to be a large success. The election of officers then took place, Mr. Woodcock being unanimously elected presi- dent and Mr. S. G. Throssell, secretary. The follow- ing committee were appointed :—Mr. J. W. McAvoy, Mr. H. P. Hughes, Mr. E. G. Burgess, and the Presi- dent and Secretary. On the proposition of Mr. Pendle- bury, seconded by Mr. Hughes, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the President. WASHING AND WRINGING MACHINES try J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55 Bridge-street, Warrington.—</p>
        <p>Boy Billiard Champion at Warrington. There was a large gathering at the Liberal Club on Monday evening on the occasion of a visit from Master T. Tootill, of Bury, the boy champion of England. It had been arranged that he should meet Mr. R. Woods, Dr. Reginald Smith and Mr. E. Pierpoint in a game of 750 up. The first to oppose him was Mr. Woods and play for a time was uninteresting both cueists being for a time unable to guage the run of the table which is exceedingly fast. Totill scored the faster and im- proving in his play ran to his points while Mr. Woods I was compiling 133. Dr. Smith showed better form I and at one time was scoring faster than the champion. Tootill however rose to the occasion and vrassol when Dr. Smith made way for Mr. Pierpoint, the combined scores of the amateurs being 334. Daring the last 250 Tootill gave a splendid exhibition and recorded a break of 52, the highest of the evening, and several of over 20, scored chiefly on the top of the table. He won with ease by a total majority of 309. The best breaks were :—Tootill: 18, 10, 24, 29, 28, 21, 17,31, 42, 19, 29, 21, 52, 39, 25, and 24. Smith: 18,18, 18, and 27. Pierpoint: 14. It should be stated that all players were strangers to the table which was used. After the match' young Tootill gave an ex- hibition of fancy shots and strokes, his cleverness meriting hearty applause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>INDIAN EMPIRE BAZAAR.</head>
        <p>INDIAN EMPIRE BAZAAR.</p>
        <p>BIG CATHOLIC VENTURE IN PARR HALL</p>
        <p>Opening by Lady Acton.</p>
        <p>The Parr Hall has undergone a transformation this week. On entering the body of the hall, • the visitor is surprised to find himself amidst a scene of Indian splendour which pleases the eye by the striking con- trast to commercial England with its smoke and bustle. The centre-piece, at the back of the platform, depicts in typical, glowing colours an Indian City on the banks of a broad river, while on all sides of the hall are effective representations of the dwellings of the people of our great Dependency. In the far back ground rise the domes of stately mosques. The com- plete scene is a brilliant one and will no doubt attract many townspeople to pay a flying visit to India. Why the hall has been thus transformed is soon explained. In order to raise much-needed funds the workers of St. Mary's (R.C.) Church, Buttermarket- street, decided to hold an "Indian Empire "bazaar and the project was entered into with much enthusiasm. The result is the pleasing spectacle at the Parr Hall, and jucfging by the success which has already attended the venture the expectations of the promoters will be fully realized. Opened on Thursday afternoon by Lady Acton the bazaar has been largely attended and will continue until Saturday evening. The object is a very commendable one. Although ' the worshippers at St. Mary's are fortunate in having a beautiful church and fairly adequate school accom- modation, it is felt that much remains to be done 'amongst the parishioners, especially the young. As 1 the appeal in the bazaar handbook puts it : I" There exists an admirable spirit of mutual help,and many are animated with an earnest desire to assist their clergy, but we find from experience that all such social work as is not directly religious and proper for the Church is very much hindered for want of a Parish Hall or room of some kind, where our people could be brought together for purposes of instruction, healthy recreation, or common action in matters of public interest. Sa urgently do we feel the usefulness and indeed the necessity of such accommodation that we have not shrunk from that severe test of earnestness and courage, a bazaar to raise funds towards this object." • With a large band of energetic workers, admirable preparations have been made for the bazaar, and the heavily-laden stalls afford eloquent testimony to the services of the ladies. Gifts have been forthcoming from near and far, and bnutiful specimens of embroidery, &amp;c., have been generally admired, The flower stall is covered with the choicest blooms, and there has been a brisk sale of buttonholes. An excep- tionally attractive programme of entertainments has been arranged, and further particUlars will be found below. It should be stated that the bazaar handbook contains an interesting " Sketch of Catholic develop- ment in Warrington,' and a short history of St. Mary's Church. THE STALL HOLDERS. The following is a list of the stalls and stall committees:— SODALITY OF THE CIIILDREN OE' MARY:—Miss Kiernan, president; Misses Bradshaw, Burrows, J. Corney, Cunningham, Hayes, Kelleher, Mather and Sexton; Mrs. Keating°, treasurer. CONFILITERNITY OP MOUNT CLIIMEL :—Mrs. Henry Houghton, president; Mesdames Hardiman, MeAvoy, B. Massey, Miss Eckersloy ; Mesdames Price and Richardson, treasurers. GUILD OF TICE SACRED HEART I—Miss Dolan, presi- dent; Mesdames Corney, Vernon, Lightbarn, E. Rigby, Talbot, and Miss Corney. LIUGUE OP THE CROSSI—NirS. Henry Rigby president; Mesdames Broadbent, Challoner, Gatley, John Mather, Misses Archer, J. Caldwell, Gleeson,Johnson, Kilgallon, and E. Rigby; Miss Costelloe, treasurer. FLOWER. STALL M. Dolan. president ; Mrs. G. Dolan, Misses Atherton, Bibby, Noakes, Webster, A. Webster and C. Webster.</p>
        <p>TOBACCO AND STATIONERY STILL Kilburn, president ; Miss Kilburn, Miss Mather, Miss E. Mather. SWEETS STALL: Mrs. Byrne, Misses E. Corney, M. Costelloe, and Mather. REFRESHMENT STALL :—Mesdames G. N. Vernon,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>PROVIDENCE CHAPEL, STOCKTON HE kTEI.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival services were held on Sanday when thepreacher was Mr. R. B. Woods. Daring the day special anthems were sung by tha choir, Mister Willie Pearse presiding at the organ. At the evening meating a quartet party of the Apollo Male Voice Choir sang" Rock of Ages" and " Remember me," both of which were mach appreciated. The preacher appealed for a deeper study of the people's needs. He claimed from all Christians a deeper interest in all public ques- tions affecting the moral life. On Monday evening a social gathering was held. Songs were rendered by MISS Ada Grounds, Misses Edith and Ethel Mounfield, and Mr. Grounds, Mr. John Wilde recited, and a quartet was rendered by Miss E. Mounfield, Miss E. Houghton, and Messrs. E. and W. Grounds. Humorous sketches were given by Mr. Mallard. The accompanist was Miss Bouchier. Refreshments were handed round at intervals and a very enjoyable evening was spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>PARISH CHURCH.</head>
        <p>THE PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services were continued on Sunday. when large congregations assembled. The morning preacher was the Rev, Canon Aferley Steven- son, who preached from the words, " Seek ye first the Kingdom of God." The importance of th tnkfulness to Gad for His mercies was emphasized, and the speaker said it was the natural tendency of all of us to dwell more upon our troubles than upon our blessings. It would be a good thing if we were sometimes to try to see how many more blessings we had than trials, to think of the dilly food provided for us, of the calm bight of !deep, of the health and strength which enabled us to meet in God's house that morning, of the employ- ment which enabled us to keep ourselves and our families, of the comfort, brightness, joy. and peace of our English homes, of the paac3 this country was enjoy- ing, of the blessing of living in a well-ordered land of freedom and godly liberty. The plilpit was occupied n the evening by the Rector. Special music was rendereld during the day.</p>
        <p>the evening. Thanks are due to the ladies for their successful efforts in decorating and catering. HOLY TRINITY CHURCH. I VEN, ARCHDEACON MADDEN AND IMPURE LITERATURE. _</p>
        <p>The thanksgiving —se-rri-c-e-s-In-CO;Pection with Holy Trinity Church were commenced on Thursday. The church has been prettily decorated for the occasion with fruit, flowers, etc., and presents a pleasing appear- ance. There was a large congregation. The preacher, the Von. Archdeacon MADDEN, took for his text the words " Herein is the saying true; one soweth and another reapeth." He said we must not forget when we boasted of our empire, our learning, and our religious. life that we were only reaping what others had sown We were reaping but we also had a duty as sowers of what others would reap. Let our patriotism not be the patriotism of shouting and of the streets, but that of men full of righteousness. Temperance, sobriety and parity, were the things which made a nation strong and if we sowed the good seeds of those things we would do our duty and would leave the Empire to our children enriched and strengthened. Recently, in Liverpool, they had bad a NerV serious question engaging their attention—the question of the kind of litera- ture which circulated amongst the children. "I can not understand" said Archdeacon Madden "the kind of creature that can possibly go about doing such devil's work amongst our children.r cannot conceive the kind of creature who can gloat over the evils he works in the child's mind by these things. If we are sowing the good seed there are others sowing seeds of evil. I appeal to Sunday school teachers, to the men of influ- ence in this town. You are doing no good by being silent. It is cowardly to keep your mouth shut. I would that the consecrated motherhood of our cities would rise up and drive these things from our midst— for the men seem to have lost their manhood—and pre- vent these deadly things getting into the minds of our children and doing such terrible ravage with their souls their purity and their morals."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>MERSEY-STREET MISSION.</head>
        <p>MERSEY-STREET MISSION.</p>
        <p>The above Mission held its first harvest thanksgiving services on Sunday last. The Mission had been taste- fully decorated with corn, flowers; fruit, vegetables, etc., and presented a very pleasing appearance. A large congregation assembled at the morning service, when the preacher was Mr. Matthew Llewellyn, who took for his text" I say unto you, Lift your eyes and look on the fields, for they are white already to harvest." John iv., 35. In the afternoon a children's service was held, presided over by Mr. F. Merlin, who gave an address on "The sinking corn ship." Acts xxvii., 38. During the afternoon, solos and recitations were given by Miss Charlotte Littlewood and Master Sam Hughes. A crowded congregation assembled at the evening ser- vice, when Mr. Moores preached from the text "And many Samaritans believed on Him for the saying of the woman who testified. He told me all that ever I did." John iv., 39. During the evening service a harvest piece was rendered by a choir of children, and Mrs. Moores sang a solo.—On Monday a fruit banquet and entertainment were held, presided over by Mr. Harry Walley, who gave an inspiring address. Solos were given by Mrs. Colley, Misses S. Houghton and Rachael Peacock. Recitations by Miss Ada Littlewood, Mr. P. Merlin, and Master Sam II ighes. Mr. Thomas Hughes acted as accompanist. Fruit was banded round during</p>
        <p>CRISP AD CREAMY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>CAIRO STREET CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>CAIRO STREET CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services in connect:on with the above place of worship, were held on Sunday. The sacred building had been tastefully decorated for the occasion with corn, fruit, and flowers the work of the Misses Houghton, D. Monks, Pemberton, Hill and Madeley. The anthems : "Thou visitest the earth," and" Sing asoug of praise" were rendered by the choir the organist being Mr. Muir. The preacher the Rev. C. Harvey -Cook, in the evening said their well-filled barns were the result of their labours plus woat God had added. They gathered not merely what they sowed, but the increase whioh Heaven gave. The bonus that He added to efforts of the farmers and which they gathered, had grown so familiar to them, that the harvest lost its wonder and mean- ing for them. The harvest was more than a church festival, more than a time for church decoration, and more than an opportunity for rejoicing in the fruits of the earth. It was a time of meditation, a time of learn- ing and a time of wonder. " That thou givest unto them, they gather," as applied to harvest, VMS a lesson in agri- cultural co-operation. Man collected the interest God gave to his labours, yet he forgot the Giver. Let that harvest appeal to their thought, and let them think of God's donation. Harvest came yearly because God meant man to be industrious, and things were done decently and in order in His kingdom. Let man show his gratitude to God fot what had been given him. Man gave and perchance God gathered, not that He or they might live on each other, but that they might live in each other's company harmoniously,working together in harmony, and realizing the spiritual bonds which bound them together, and seeking to fulfil their destiny. If some such thoughts as these Associated themselves in their minds with that harvest, their festival would not have been in vain. They would have learnt an old lesson by seeing an old truth in a new dress, " What Thou givest them they gather." The collections were in aid of the Warrington and District Nursing Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>REV. J. G. dtfcCORNIICK AND PUBLIC PURITY. The harvest thanksgiving services in connection with this Church were commeeced on Wednesday evening when, notwithstanding the inclemency of the weather, there was a good congregation. An abundance of flowers, fruit, vegetables and cereals had been forth- coming for decorative purposes and the church was neatly and artistically embellished. The altar, font, and pulpit had received special attention. As usual, the music was of a festal character, processional and recessional hymns being sung. The choir very creditably rendered the anthem "Praise the Lord, 0 &amp;mammies:lt " Miss Armstrong taking the solo. Mr. E. Wilkes presided at. the organ. The preacher was the Rev. J. G. Mc Cormick, of Liverpool, who based a powerful sermon on the words The angels are the reapers." After dealing with the ministry of angels, he said that by holding that thanks- giving service they proclaimed their faith in the father- hood of God. It would be a bad day for England and a disastrous day for the church when they no longer came to the House of God on such occasions as that,— when they only came to pray in their necessities and not to thank Him for their blessings. In addition to the harvest of nature, they had also to thank God for the harvests of thought, invention, and economic conditions. The workingman to-day was sometimes dissatisfied with his lot, and no doubt in many instances petfectly justifiably so. Bat they must rerneinbor that the step from the serf of Flighted—which was the state of the workingman a few hundred years ago—to the man of England to-day was a step as from one pole to the other. It was not by the onward mtrch ofciviliza- tion or the increase of culture that men had been raised, It was by the teaching which had, alas, so slowly per- meated the land—the teaching of the brotherhood of men according as it was brought to the earth by Jesus Christ. Unfortunately even Cnristians had oppressed their brother and trampled upon him but slowly and surely the idea of the brotherhood of men which lay at the very foundation of Christianity was spreading over the world until even those who did not allow them- selves to be ranked as Ctristians had accepted this doctrine in its entirety. In moral and spiritual things there was no less truly a harvest. There was a harvest in the commonwealth and in Society. If our rulers were just and honest, if our municipalities were governed according to the laws of what was right, honouurable and true, the effect would be felt right down through all the ranks of Society. If we might look up to our leaders and say "These men are true and honourable and are setting us a good example "it uplifted the whole tone of society and there was a harvest fillip their actions which we reaped. If we looked to ar leaders and found them corrupt and dishonourable, if we found them involved in every sort of shady transaction, if we found that their names instead of standing high in the rolls of morality stood extremely low, then the result must be felt through all the ranks of society and there would be a harvest of evil. We ought to thank God that there was a harvest bj3cause it made many a man pause to consider when his actions were going to have an effect not only upon himself but upon others. The man must be sunken who said to himself " I don'tcare how I live. don't care whether I ruin innocent lives or not. I am just going to do what I like." The preacher con- cluded by applying the lessons of the harvest individu- ally.—The offertory was on behalf of the endowment fund. The services will be continued on Sunday.</p>
        <p>ST. PETER'S CI-LURCEL</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services in connection with the above church commenced on Thursday, when there , was a large congregation. The church had been specially and artistically decorated for the occasion the work having been done by the following ladies :—Chancel, Mesdames Percival, James, Jno. Taylor and Heaconk ; pulpit and screen, Mesdames Webster, Barnferth and Cowden and Miss D. Percival; font, Mrs. Clayton, Mrs. F. Webster and Miss Stanley ; windows, Mrs. Barlow and the Misses Hughes and L. Percival. The preachei was the Rev. E. W. Whittenbury-Kaye, rector of New- church, who delivered an appropriate address. Diving the evening the music was of a special character, the choir ably rendering the anthem "0. clap your hands."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>APPLETON RIFLE CLU3. -- Medal Competition.</head>
        <p>APPLETON RIFLE CLU3. -- Medal Competition.</p>
        <p>The whole of last Saturday afternoon (the last meeting of the club for this season) was devotod to the final competition for the Bronze Medal, which is offered annually by the National Rifle Association to be competed for by members of the Appleton Rifle Club.</p>
        <p>The conditions of the competition this year were as follows : 10 rounds at 100 yard, Gin bull, and 10 rounds at 100 yards, 310 bull ; the competitor to fire from any position, with the Service rifle. The five best scores were as follows :</p>
        <p>Gin hull Sin hull. Total. Major BrerAton Fair°lough 36 ... 26 ... 62 Sergeant Plinston . 23 62 Mr. Wilcox 40 ... 22 ... 62 Captain H. G. Roberts 99 .. 27 ... 56 Mr. Arthur Down 97 21 ... 48</p>
        <p>The three members who tied for first place were Called Upon by the committee to fire ten rounds at the 3in bull and the scores were returned as fellows :—Major Breretou Fairclough, 28 ; Sergeant Plinston, 26 : Mr. Wilcox, 2-1, Major Brereton Fairclorigh thus carryied oft the medal Sergeant Plinston being second.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S CREAM CRACKERS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>CHARGES AGAINST COUNCILLORS.</head>
        <p>THE CHARGES AGAINST COUNCILLORS.</p>
        <p>THE APPLICATION TO TAKE PROCEEDINGS. Town Clerk and the Investigation Committee.</p>
        <p>A lively discussion on the steps which are Using taken to Institute proceedings against two Councillors for alleged corrupt practices was engendered at Tuesday evening's meeting of the Warrington Town Council. Councillor Lard had given notice of the following questions : . . . .</p>
        <p>1. What steps have been taken with a view to carry- ing out the decision of the Council with reprd to the charges made against two members of this Council? 2. When are the cases likely to be submitted to the Attorney-General? The Havoc replied as follows : —With reference to the first question, the Town Clerk informs me that the necessary documents have beeen prepared, and the memorial to the Attorney-General for leave to proceed will be laid before the Council for sealing at the next meeting. With reference to the second question, the Mayor said the answer was : "At the end of the vacation, which will be in three weeks' time, The Attorney-General will int give his Consent for prose- cutions during vacation except in very exceptional cases, such as, for instance, when the persons accused are about to leave the country." When the minutes of the General Purposes Committee came up for confirmation Councillor CLEGG asked what action the Town Clerk intended to take with regard to the suggestion made at the last Council meeting that he should submit a draft of the case to the Investigation Committee before applying to the Attorney-General. Alderman PIERPOLNT: That will follow. He is bound to submit it.</p>
        <p>The MANOR: The necessary documents hay, been prepared and they will be laid before the Council for sealing at the next meeting. Taen will be the time for discussion.</p>
        <p>Councillor. CLEGG : They will not be laid before the Invest igation Committee then.</p>
        <p>The 'fowN CLEI/1C : Before the Council. The MAYOR : They cannot be sealed unless they come before the Council.</p>
        <p>Alderman PrEneotNT: You promised that the draft should be submitted to the Investigation Committee before anything further was done. I hope that promise will be kept. If there is any reason why it should not, I will be prepared to hear it. Tao rOWN CLERK : There must be no misunder- standing about the matter. I promised to submit to the Investigation Committee my explication to the Attorney-General for leave to proceed. That document will be laid before you at the next meeting. That is the only dOcurnent I intend to lay before you. The MAYOR : It is a matter of law. Alderman PIERPOINT : The matter, as it is in my mind, is that, if you make a promise I hope you will keep it. The ALLYOR : I said distinctly that I did not see ' any objection to the Town Clerk laying it before the sub-committee.</p>
        <p>The TowN CLERK The application to the Attorney- General.</p>
        <p>Alderman PIERPOINT : Very well, you and the Town Clerk must take the responsibility. The M&amp;YOR : I have nothing to do with it. The TOWN Cum: : I arts quite prepared to take the whole reponsibilitv. It is my duty to do so. Councillor AsurcßOFT : The Town Clerk did promise, as Alderman Pierpoint says. The ;Savor:. : The whole thing is out of order. Wo will go on with the hasine:J. Councillor CLEGG I want to know whether the Investigation Committee will be allowed to alter or suggest or make any remarks. The Towx CLERK : No.</p>
        <p>Councillor CIAGG : Yon guarantee that. The Tows CLF.RR. : I certainly do. Councillor WILKINSON: Now, Arr. Alayor,l think— The MAYOR Oa; let us got on. Councillor WILKINSON : But I have something to say about this. The MAYOR : Well, it is out of order. It is not on the minutes.</p>
        <p>Councillor WHAM, SON : Vefl. why did not you call them order before? (Laughter)'. - The INIAYOR : If you really want to make bother you can be in order by discussing Mr. Dodds' letter (this letter had reference to the meeting of ratepayers in the Parr Hal.) Councillor WILICINSON said he took it that the General Purposes Committee ordered the application to the Attorney-General, and it was to the General Pur- poses Committee that any document should be shown, and not to the Investigation Committee. His feeling was that the Investigation Committee ceased immedi- ately it sent in its report. Councillor B•roTr : It is a question of what the Council understood at the last meeting. It makes no difference to ma at all. Certainly, I understood that the statement of the two c ises would bs laid before the Investigation Sub-committee. The Tows CLNAK : I said the application to the Attorney-General. Councillor Sto.rr : Of course you MAY have said that, but I believe thip Council understood that the statement of the case, similar to what was laid before the Barrister, should be submitted. It is a queltion of what the Council understood at the last meeting, and I certainly understood that.</p>
        <p>—Tie Nia.Y.o:l7l will t 7511 you what I understood. I understood that as far as this thing was concerned the Committee ceased its labours directly it made its report. That came b.4ore the General Purposes Committee, aud was adopted. Then someone—l think it was Alderman Pierpoint—got upend asked whether the Town Clerk had any objection to laying the case to be submitted to tne Attorne3 -General before the Investigation Com- snittee. He said he had not, and he says he will do it now. A VOICE does not.</p>
        <p>the) Tows CLERK : I 53,5, the applioltion to the Attornpv-Gooeral.</p>
        <p>The lAssom : And it will come before you in due course.</p>
        <p>Alderman Tissrox : He said he would lay it before them, providing they did not interfere with it—That is what he said distinctly—and very properly so, too. Ths \taxon ; The commit*.ee is not a collection of lawyers•who can advise the Tema Clerk. Councillor STotr : Tee question with rue is what the Council understood. Tie committee saw the case that was submitted to Ar. Eldon 13 takes, [C.C. and I under- stood that this case would be laid before the Investiga- tion Committee before it was sent to the Attorney- General. I think Alderman Pierpoint and the members of the committee understood that too.</p>
        <p>Councillor CLEGG : There vals no resolution,and the whole thing was out of order. Co:incillor Srorr : It is not a question of being out of order. It is a question of what the Council under- stood.</p>
        <p>The NI:11:bn : You cannot alter it. It is only a matter of courtesy to submit it to you. You have no power to interfere with any prosecution or otherwise when you have decided that the case shall go before the Attorney-General.</p>
        <p>Councillor CL,m; G : I am only anxious that the evi- dence shall be submitted to the Attorney-General as it is.</p>
        <p>The Tows Certainly, To MANOR : You must trust your officials to do that. _ .</p>
        <p>Councillor CLEGG : I don't want any alteration in the case. What do these 'men want to do with it ? It ' has cost f 270 already. If they have their way it will not be wolth 70 pence to the ratepayers. Councillor STorr : I object most strongly to that if members of the committee are going to be charged with taking sides. (Rear, hear.) The NIA YOR : They are not. The sub-committee deserve the thanks of the Council for the way they did their work. They are all amateur lawyers,and did their best as far RS their powers went. Alderman SMETHURST : I entirely endorse Coun- cillor Clegg's statement. When we signed the document we finished. Alderman Pierooint said it wanted some colouring putting on. We don't want any colouring. We want it to go as Councillor Clegg says. The MAYOR : Let us not discuss that: _</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : I don't think Alderman Smethurst ought to be allowed to say that Alderman Pierpoint wanted to put colouring on. He never said anything of the kind. Councillor Cr:En G : I deny that. Alderman SMETHURST : Of course he did.</p>
        <p>Alderman PIERPOINT was out of the room during this part of the discussion. The MAYOR : I cannot allow any more of this. You are beginning to get personal. Councillor BcrcroN : Are we to understand that this little Investigation Committee has cost us £7O? Alderman SNIETITURST : Yes, it has.</p>
        <p>Th. TOWN CLERK : I want to make it quite clear that 'cannot allow any interference on the part of the Investigation Committee or the Council The MtVOR : Not so far as the Attorney-General is concerned. Alderman TNNTON : Quite right. It would be encouraging Tammany. Later in the evening Alderman PIERTOTST entered the room and said :—"I must claim the indulgence of the Council. I believe that during my absence my name has been used. whether in a kindly manner, or unkindly. I am utterly at a loss to know. Perhaps the alderman will explain how he used my name and why? Alderman SmsTirrusT : I am qiiite prepared to explain how I used his name. It was by the tantalising— The MAYOR: I think the cap is fitting too many of you. (Laughter.) Alderman SMETIEURST I am quite prepared to explain how I used his name. It was by the— The Maxort: I never heard anything about Alder- man Pierpoint. Councillor PEMBERTON: Alderman Smethurst said</p>
        <p>Alderman Pierpoint said he wanted to colour the report. Why don't you be manly and tell him what you. said about him.</p>
        <p>- The AfAVOR: Discuss the rniuutes of the General Purposes Committee. (Hear, hear.) Alderman SMETHURST : I am telliug him before his face.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: I must have order. Alderman SmEiounsx: lam not afraid to tell him. The discussion on the matter then ended.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. Mr. J. Gould presided over the meeting of this society on Sunday last, when there was a fair attendance. Ho asked a cpiestison, " What good had the P.S.A. meetings done them?" If none,it was time wasted,but if they had received good,they mast go forward.—Mr. T. Gattenby, of Manchester, was the speaker and spoke upon "Happi- ness : How to get it, how to keep it, and how to use it." The Secretary referred to some critics in the pulpits of Warrington of late who said that P.S.A. meetings were only to please and that P.S.A. men were r like children and only went to be pleased, others 'lnd- ia.. fault with its resalts. The Secretary said if bring- " ing men to Christ as sinners and making their lives better and homes brighter was not a rood work,then he failed to see where they must look for good. If the ministers who talked thus in their pulpits would come and see for themselves they would then know something of love, love to God and love to min, service for God and service for man which was carried out in the P.S.A, work. If their work was of man only,it would come to nought, but if of God, it could not he overthrown. For men to speak as these clerical brethren had done,showeil complete ignoranae of the work. Dares new merabars were heartily welcomoi making a total of 519. Dr. Beet at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church. Difficulties of the Christian Belief.</p>
        <p>The anniversary services in connection with the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church were held on Sunday, when the Rev. J. A. Beet, D.D., a former Warringtom minister, who recently resigned a theological chair at. Richmond College, preached morning and evening to large congregations. At the service in the evening Dr. Beet said vengeance had no place in God's dealing and it ought not to be in the English Bible. There was no revenge in the pahishment of the Bible • it meant justice, and the word was vary marked. it was justice, not vengeance. If the congregation would take Christ's hand and follow Him they would be saved. An excellent rendering was given by the choir of the, anthem "Sun of my soul." Mr. Wadsworth was at the organ.</p>
        <p>THE AFTERNOON IIEETING. There was a large congregation in the afternoon. when Dr. Beet answered a number of written questions on the difficulties of the Christian belief. Mr. R. GARNETC presided, and said that if they could exist without doubts life would be better for all of them. They, however, had their doubts, but if they could keep them from protruding and cling to that which they believed, they would have a more satisfac- tory outlook. We wanted something in our daily life, some power which could keep us from over-reaching someone else in the daily conflict with our fellow men. We must endeavour as far as possible to regard the actions of our fellow men a little more charitably, anct give them credit for some good feeling and kindness. We must have a wider, broader, and more general out- look.</p>
        <p>The first question which Dr. Beat had to answer was "What evidence, not hearsay, can you pro- duce to prove that Jesus Christ ever existed." Dr. Beet in replying asked what evidence they had that Alfred ,the Great ever existed. They knew that Christian- ity existed. They knew that Christian nations were tha leading nations of the world. There must have been a cause for the existence of Christianity. He could not think that Christianity had no solid fonnietion. He thought the existence of Christ was proved by the mark which His life had made upon human lire. Replying to the question "Is it not a fact that net. one of the original manuscripts of the New Testament were in existence until a few hundred years after the death of Christ "? Dr. 133 et said it was a fact. There were sever 4 copies of the epistle of St. Paul's and the accuracy of the epistle was proved by the very slight difference in the copies. Another questioner asked " If the theory of evolution is correct there would not he any need for redemption as man could not fall ?" The theory of evolution said Dr. Beet, was the most likely hypothesis of the crea- tion of animals and mon, lie did not see however how that hid anything to do with the fall of man. Man's intellect was created by God. Answering another question Dr. Beet said he could not SOR much in the Bible in favour of the doctrine that every minjwould be saved. On the contrary there was a great deal in the New Testament which was directly against that teaching. Paul saicl " Whose end is des- truction " " Whose end is according to their work." Teat was plain teaching and was against the doctrine of universalism.</p>
        <p>Replying to the question " Ts man's salvation to bet obtained by complying with the law ?" Dr. Beet said salvation could not be won by keeping the law only, though it could not be won without keeping the law.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>ItEt3ON OF THE CEIRESTIAN HOPE.</head>
        <p>THE ItEt3ON OF THE CEIRESTIAN HOPE.</p>
        <p>Oa Monday evening Dr. Nest lectured to a large gathering on "The reason of the Christian hope." Mr. James Dodds had been announced to preside, but was untbla to attend owing to indisposition. "The reason of the Christian hope " said Dr. Beet is such that if it be sound, investigation will reveal that soundness. Christianity is essentially a hope—or, rather, the hope it gives is an essential part of it. On what grounds does our hope of hea yen rest? We get that hope first by tradition—we were taught it. But the reasons we had for believing it in childhood's days are not sufficient for manhood. We begin with tradition, by being told tho old, old story. Next, we get The Book, and read it— more or less. The Book confirms and strengthens that tradition. It is true that people reading the Book through meet with difficulties—things which trouble them—bat there is no doubt as to the great mass of its teaching. Does not all the learning we get of Jesus Christ raise us up in heart and mind ? Is there not that in it which make us better men and women? Undoubtedly there is, and therefore, it must be true or contain a great deal of truth. If a thoughtful man were asked why he believed in Christianity, those, I think, would be the reasons he would ive." Continuing, Dr. Beet said he accepted the Christian belief. He accepted the teaching of the New Testament because it was the only feasible and conceivable explanation of a great multitude of facts—facts written on the page of history and facts written on the inward experience of his own -heart—and there- fore he believed that which he was taught as a boy, because the experience of a fairly long lifetime had given him a mass of facts which could, as he los d said, only be explained by the truth of the Gospel. (Anulanse). Dr. Beet was cordially thanked for his legture, on the motion of the Bev. SIDNEY PITT, seconded by the Rev. WILLIAM IMELLort. A collection was taken for the trust funds and realized a substantial sum.</p>
      </div>
