      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>CHILDREN'S DROLL SAYINGS.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S DROLL SAYINGS.</p>
        <p>£lO for the Most Amusing.</p>
        <p>7 he amcsing remarks of children are always treasured in family circles, and form the most delightful in- stances of humour. Readers now have an opportunity of entering a novel and interesting competition, for the proprietors or Peptolaxa (an idesl medicine for babes and children) offer prizes of £5, £2, £l, and eight others of as. each, for what they consider to be the funniest saying or the most amusing action of a child. , Only actual experiences may be submitted; if not your own child they must relate to one you know. Extracts from books, etc., are not eligible. Write your contri- bution as briefly as possible, after the following style : " Little George-----, age -, who lives at ; ----, on seeing his new sister for the first time, said hopefully, ' Will they be alwsys washing , her now, instead of me ?" Then add your own name land address and mention(Warrington "Examiner "). I Contributions for this competition should be sent in ! not later than November 4th next, to Sayings Dept.," Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., Holborn-viaduct, London, enclosing the label wrapper from a Is. Uri. package of Peptotlaxa ; each w tapper will admit three sayings to the competition. Watch Peptoraxa advertisements for I the names of winners about Nov. 24th. Peptolaxa is a delightful medicine for children, even ! for the tenderest infant, being comforting, cooling and laxative. Teething troubles are overcome without tears while feverishness, cot.atipation, diarrhces, and fretful- ness disappear like magic. Mrs. Strong, 99, St. Geo..ge's- iroad, Barnsley, states : "Through Peptolaxa my Edith ; is quite a different child, so -bright and happy and the , picture of health. Previously she had suffered so from j teething and constipation that I often thought I should i lose her." Peptolaxa, Is. lid., is sold at all Branches of Boots,' ; Cash Chemists.</p>
        <p>TRAVEL BOONS AND FINE ART ENGRAVINGS We have on more than one occasion called attention to the beautiful holiday and health resorts which are to be found throughout the system of the Great Western Railway of England, particularly in the Duchy of Corn- wall and South Wales," The Country of Castles." This line also forms the hest route from places in the South and West of England to the South of Ireland, and the Company will, in to the course of a few months open the shortest channel passage between England and Ireland. . .</p>
