      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Warrington Draw at Halifax.</head>
        <p>Warrington Draw at Halifax.</p>
        <p>The Warrington men were again away from home on Saturday, and in visiting Halifax they faced a severe task. The team was strengthened by the return of Fish, though two e00(1 forwards in Shugnrs and Naylor were absentees. Halifax were quite as severely handicapped by having to find substitutes for Wedgwood and Joe Riley. A new threequarter in the person of D. J. Lewis, late of Oldham played a fair game for them. Fine weather prevailed and there was a gate of 8,000. Teams and officials : Halifax—Little, back ; Drummond, Williams, Lewis, and Hartley, threequarter backs ; Hilton and Atkins, half backs; Bartle, Jack Riley. Morton, Longhorn, Norton, Hammond, Swinbank, and Winskill, forwards. Warrington—Hallam. hack ; Harris, Jordon, Isher- wood and Fish, threequarter backs : Hockenhall and Brooks, half backs ; Preston, Thomas, Boardman, Swift, Helton, Jolly. Harmer sad Cook, forwards. Referee. Mr. F. Kennedy. Broughton Rangers. Touch lodges, Messrs. Scholes, Dewsbury, and G. Fowler, Leeds. Halifax opened strongly, and right from the kick-off the Warrington line had a narrow escape, Jack Riley and Bartle almost dribbling over. The attack continued in the Warrington quarter until a strong effort carried the ball to centre. 'Here the visitors were penalised, and Little kicked a grand goal. The reverse infused more spirit into the Warrington men. and by dint of strong forward work they invaded the home territory. Harris made a long run down touch, hut was over- whelmed by numbers. Warrington persisted, and a smart piece of dribbling by Belton took the ball near the home line. Their stay -here was brief, for Hammond came away finely in a rush, and only Fish's speed prevented him reaching the Warrington goal. Halifax now showed line form in the loose, and Isherwood's pluck alone saved the situation when he dived for the ball. Harmer relieved with a dribble and kick, but Fish missed his main after a fast run. Lewis returned cleverly and Hallam was captured, but fast play saw the ball again at centre. Here Fish took a sharp pass from Brooks and raced down to Little' When challenged he kicked over the 'full hack's head and appeared a certain scorer. Drummond however, came across at top speed and beat the Warrington crack for possession on the line. Halifax attacked again and a long pass to Drummond nearly did the trick, Harris just grassing him in the field of play. Half-time Halifax one goal (2 points), Warrington nil. Warrington resumed with a slight breeze in their favour and were early on the aggressive though Fish twice missed the hall from passes. Hockenhall however broke away grandly and passed to Isherwood. The centre could have given Harris a walk in hot preferred to go on and was captured in front of the posts. War- rington were now playing very strongly, but some magnificent punting by Little quite changed the aspect of affairs. Halifax reached the Warrington line and set up a desperate attack. Drummond and J, Riley were each pulled down on the line, but finally Hilton scrambled over from Atkin's pass. Little missed the goal. Warrington resumed with great dash, only to see the ball kicked past Hallam, and Williams race in unopposed from half-way. Little improved and Warrington were ten points behind with only 10 minutes to go. Preston immediately kicked a Penalty god. Smart passing by Hockenhall, Isherwood, Jordan and Harris resulted in a try to the latter at the corner, and Preston again gonled from a difTicult angle. Play was very exciting and Little only just missed another penalty. Five minutes from time Harmer kicked across to the left wing and Hartley mulling Fish nipped in with a try. The score was now ten points all, and Preston took the kick with great care. His shot was well directed hut the referee signalled no goal, though the kicker apparently thought he had been successful. Final : Halifax, ten points ; Warrington ten points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRTNOTON " A " v. PEMBERTON. Played at Warrington on Saturday. Warrington fared the hreez in the opening half, and Knight was called npon to deal with an invasion led by Broen and Gerrard. Eventually the Warringtan half backs excelled in some quick transfers, and after Davies and Garside had exchanged on opening was made.by Dickenson for Bibby, and the Woolston lad scored after a strong run at the corner. Warrington once mote assumed the aggressive, and Knight scored a goal from a penalty. Pemberton were again forced back, and after Forster had missed an cpening,the Warrington forwards dribbled</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 7, 1905</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>THE DISPOSAL OF SEWAGE.</p>
        <p>The.monthly meeting was held on Tuesday evening, at the Town Hall, when there were present, the Mayor presiding :—Aldermen A. Bennett, John Evans, R. W. Francomb, W. Pierpoint, J. Smethurst, D. Tinnion. Councillors T. H. Ashcroft, T. Barton, W. Benson, W. Brough, T. Burton, W. Clegg, J. G. Craik, James Evans, L. Ellison W. J. Forshaw, T. J. Hulse, Dr. Joseph, J. Lord, P. Peacock, J. Pemberton, J. Sankey' J. Skelland, J. Patton Smith,F. Stott, J. Wilkinson, W. H. Wilson, T. Wright, and Messrs. J. L. Whittle (Town Clerk)„ T. A. Hallaway, (Deputy Town Clerk), J. Fairhurst (Borough Treasurer), T. Longdin (Borough Surveyor), J. Doss (Water Engineer), W. S. Haddock (Gas Engineer), and J. M. Murray (Director of Education). STREET WIDENING. SANKEY-STREET.</p>
        <p>The Street Improvement Committee resolved that Sankey-street be widened from Garven-place to Mr. Birtles' premises, and the gate and palisading in front of the Education office set back, and that the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee be requested to set back the wall in front of the late Miss Oars-en's pre- mises to the prescribed line. The Lancashire and Yorkshire Bank, Ltd., having submitted a plan for the re-building of the front of their premises in Horsemarket-street, and having agreed to set back to the prescribed line when the street is widened without claiming any compensation for the present expenditure on the building, it was resolved that the plans be approved subject to an agreement to be prepared by the Town Clerk. The BOROUGH SURVEYOR reported that the buildings on the lend at Sankey Bridges, formerly chemical works, were in a ruinous condition, and it wee resolved that the buildings be demolished, the road widened, and the surplus land sold. Alderman BENNErr moved the confirmation of the minutes, and said he had no doubt the Council all approved of the prop mai for the further widening of Sankey-street. (Hear, hear.) Councillor EVANS seconded, and the motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>YOUNG CHILDREN AT SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>YOUNG CHILDREN AT SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee resolved "that this com- mittee considers that the exclusion of children under five years of age from school is caknlated to prevent the spread of infectious disease, and consequently to reduce the mortality from the ssone,and that it approves of the action of the Education Committee so far as it has gone in excluding these children." Toe minutes were approved. THE DISPOSAL OF SEWAGE. RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL'S APPLICATION NOT ENTERTAINED.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Paving and Sowemge Committee, held on September Ist, it was resolved that the Borough Surveyor be authorized to make arrangements for obtaining a record (for the week commencing on the 18th inst.) of the quantity of sewage passing ,from oat- falls No.l, 2,3, and 4 into the river. Alderman Tumos, in moving the confirmation of this, said the record would prove of great assist- ance in making their arrangements, when the time came, with the Manchester Ship Canal Company, to carry the sewage to another outran, probably by means of those ejectors. The information would give them an idea as to the power of the ejectors which would be required. The committee bad received an application from the Warrington Rural District Council asking if the Cor- porationi would allow them to turn the sewage from their Orford, Burtonwood, and Winwick district scheme into the Warrington sewers in Orford-lane. The com- mittee had every sympathy with the Rural District Council, but be thought that considering the capacity of the sewers they could not agree to take the other sewage as suggested. Had it been possible, they would have been glad to assist the Rural Council in the matter, but under the circumstances they felt it was for them to provide their own outfall. Alderman Smrrliunsr seconded and the minutes were approved. THE LATE HEAD GARDENER.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the Town Hafl, Parks and Baths Committee were adopted, on the motion of the MAYO 4 seconded by Alderman SMETHURST. The Mayor re- marked that since the last meeting Mr. Parker, the head gardener, a faithful servant had been called away. They all felt very sorry to hear of his death. (Hear, hear).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RIVERS COIIMITrEe.</head>
        <p>RIVERS COIIMITrEe.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Rivers Committee, the Borough Surveyor reported that he was ascertaining the quantity of sewage flowing into the river at the ontfalls at Bank Quay for the Manchester Ship Canal Company's infor- mation, in connection with their proposal to construct a rising main under the powers conferred by the Act of 1904.</p>
        <p>--The minutes were adopted on the • motion of the MAYOR seconded by Coumiillor FORSTIAW. PURcEIASE OF A CLIENIICAL FIRE EXTINGUIWER.</p>
        <p>The Fire Engine Committee met on September 16th, and inspected a model of the Braun Patent Balance Fire Escape, which was fully explained by a representative from Messrs. Henry Simonis and Company, the makers. The Borough Surveyor was authorized to purchase cer- tain small appliances. At a meeting on the 25th Septem- b?r, Councillor Wright reported that he gave a surprise alarm on the 16th instant, and that the turnout was very satisfactory, but that the supply of water in Mill lane was insufficient to keep the engine working with two jets for more than half-a-minnta's duration. It was resolved that the attention of the Water Committee be called to the insufficiency of water for the fire purposes in the Mill-lane dittrict. The committee also resolved that the tenders of Messrs. Merryweather ez Co., at £330 18s., and Messrs. H. Shnonis 34 Co., at £298 for a 63 gallon combination chemical fire extinctenr and escape, be submitted to the Council,and that the Council be recommended to accept the tender of the firm whose engine appears the most satisfactory on inspection by the committee. Alderman SmErnunsr moved the adoption of the minutes. There was found to be a shortage of water in the Mill lane district and steps would have to be taken to put the matter right. The Cornittee were of opinion that a chemical engine should belpurchased. The cost of it would not come out of the rates but out of a fund which the Fire Engine Committee had been accumulat-</p>
        <p>leg. Councillor WirsoN seconded. Councillor WRIGHT referred to the shortage of water. In the event of a serious fire in the Mill-lane diserict it would not be possible for the fire engine to keep a single jet working for half a minute. • At present if a serious fire occurred in the district it would mean the engine having to go to a brook or canal to get sufficient water. He was given to understand that there was only a three-inch main in the district, and probably this had got down to two-inch by corrosion. There were many shops and a big co-operative store in the district, and the attention of the committee should be given to the matter. Alderman Ttwrox inquired regarding the fund the cost of the new engine was to come from. Not many committees had a fund which did not come out of the rates, lie would like to know how much was in the fund and bow it had been accumulated. He would also like to know something about the proposed new engine and as to its special uses. Alderman FRANCOMB said that the engineer had I reported on the 21st November, 1904, that the supply of 1 water to Lovely-lone and neighbourhood was inadequate , andlarger mains would have to be laid below the bridge Ito Liverpool-road. The only reason why they had not carried out the suggestion was that it would cost a great deal of money. There was scarcely anything but three-inch mains throughout the town when they pur- chased the waterworks, and were ,they all replaced by , larger mains at once the ratepayers would feel the I pressure. He could promise the Fire Engine Committee that, as quickly as possible, an eight-inch main would ; he laid down and connected with the eighteen inch main I from Winwick to Warrington. I Alderman SMETHURST explained that the " reserve I fund "was raised from the charge made for attending I outside fires and was devoted to the repair and upkeep fof the engine. The chemical engine was a most efficient 1 machine and acted both as a fire escape and extineteur. The minutes were adopted. THE PURCHASE OF' COAL. ; At a recent meeting of the Water Committee the , Town Clerk reported that the Water Engineer had, in ' order to eopply the Leigh Corporation with water, purchased extra pipes and commenced to lay an additional Gin. maiu, but that while the work,was : proceeding the Leigh Corporation had intimated that : they had made satisfactory arrangements with Man- chester for a temporary supply of water, and that,there- I fore, there was no necessity for any additional mains to be laid down. It Was resolved, "That it be left in the Town Clerk's hands te make a satisfactory arrange- ment with the Leigh Cc -)ration whereby .this Cor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>NEW BOOKS, MAGAZINES</head>
        <p>NEW BOOKS, MAGAZINES</p>
        <p>ETC.</p>
        <p>"The Household of Peter," by Rosa N. Carey, (Macutillian and Co. 65.") Rosa Nouthette Carey's new novel, "The House- , hold of Peter," will, we have no doubt, command a ; ready sale. It is one of these light, cheerful pieces of I fiction which make Miss Carey so popular an authoress I with the girls of the nation. There is a wholesome- ness about all her books which is exceedingly palat- able in these regenerate days and which makes them t eminently suitable as birthday and Christmas presents, " The Household of Peter "is a story which tells of , the hopes and fears, the haps end mishaps, and the ultimate marrying all round of young Dr. Peter Holt' and his three sisters. These four comprise the l orphaned " household " and a happy family they are.! Of course, Peter, being the only male and a I few years the senior of his sisters, is a I miniature god and quite a law unto himself. No ' sister, not even the spirited Ranee, would dream of questioning his decision or opinion on any matter ; under the sun. Peter is, hovrever, no prig, but a I very level-headed, genial young medico with views of his own on most things. It was pardonable of his sisters to build airycastles for his future abode and people them with princesses of similar fitness, but like the arbitrary person he is Peter chooses a wife where no-one—except the always discerning reader— has thought of looking. The rest of the" household" has various small adventures mostly leading up to matrimony, with the exCeptien of Sallie, the youngest, whose bent is art. They are three nice girls, and we can recommend any of our readers to enter the" Hous- I hold of Peter " without the slightest misgivings. They will be warmly welcomed and will enjoy their visit.l "A Waif's Progress "by Rhoda Broughton. (Mac- millan and Co., 65.) After reading" A Waif's Progress" we can only conclude that Miss Rhoda Broughton was in dire straits for an original subject when she penned her latest novel. Originality is about the only merit the story possesses and it has been achieved at consider- able cost. The creation of such a character as Bonny- bell Ransoms is no credit to any writer, and still less so to Miss Broughton who has, in the past, written some good fiction.' "A Waif's Progress is described innocently as depicting " the struggle of a girl, reared amid vicious surroundings, to secure a footing in respectable sodiety." When we learn at the commence- ment that the heroine's parentage is •of doubtful legitimacy and that her associates have entirely consisted of the demi—sonde we are somewhat doubtful of the propriety of the story. Clearly enough the girl is handicapped in life, but even with such a beginning there is room for a redemption which it is plainly not Miss Broughton's intention to allow her to achieve. The young lady's sole aim is to secure a footing in good society, and she cventually succeeds by methods which all but the most worldly must con- demn. Bonnybell has far more personal charm than either morality or principle, and is not averse to un- masking her batteries before the respectable husbands of the two ladies she alternately lives with. One of them she marries a year triter he becomes a widower, and solely because he has an income of some 1,30,000. The sweet yonng heroine's conversation, too, smacks of the "vicious surroundings" aforesaid, and she suspects every man of the most unnameably foul intentiens. What can one say of an authoress whol fills a book with a nauseating suggestiveness and makes her 17-year-old heroine talk glibly of the number of children she deems it advisable to bear' to say nothing of displaying a frequent fondness for cigarettes, arid even whisky? We feel bound to say that "A Waif's Progress" is not the kind of story fit for introduction to the average English household,and we trust its circulation will be limited: Across the Channel it might be appreciated by the j unts fillet whose acquaintance with dnible enemdre and the ways of the 3 drvii-monde is more extensive than is the case with their sisters in England. "The loist Chance by Rolf Boldrewood, (Macmillan and Co., 65.) The well-known author of " Robbery under Arms " tic., remains on f imiliar ground in "The Last Chance," which is a tale of the goldfields of Western Australia and of a mining speculation plich was a triumphant success. As a novel Mr. Bololrewood's latest does not rank high, though well-written. There is no plot, very little incident such as one would expect in a tale of the golden west, and much that is hum-drum and common-place. Arnold Banneret, a goldfields commissioner, stakes all in a claim which is eloquently advocated by a miner whom he encounters"on his last legs." The two become partners, make a somewhat uninteresting trek into the Wild West, find the claim untouched, and forthwith make their fortunes. Mr. 13anneret's home henceforth is Sydney where his family grows up in the way families generally do, the boys are educated in the Old Country, and eventually there is the desir- able marriage connection with the aristocracy. It is all rather ordinary and very lengthy. Really, Mr. Boldrewood might have worked in a little more of the romantic with advantage. With his descriptive powers we have no fault to find, and it is interesting to peruse what he writes of the Western gold-field in full swing, but it is not fiction, and that is what one natur-- elle expects. There never was a safer " spec " than " The Last Chance ", er fortunes made with less difficulty or danger. What with arl absence of the dvpnturous and a , redundancy of the trivial, Mr. Boldrewoocre book borders on the boring. We must confess to a feeling of considerable disappointment in it. We have also received :--</p>
        <p>"The Other Woman," by Mrs. L. T. Meade (The Walter Scott Publishing Co., Ltd., 55.) "The Toll of the Bush," by William Satchel! (Macmillan and Co.. 69.). • " The Diary of Samuel) Pepys " in the" Globe " edition with introduction by G.lGregory Smith, (Macmillan and Co., 800 pages,6a.) Morley's "Life of Gladstone" in sixpenny parts is a venture by Macmillan which should be appre- ciated by the million. Mrs. C. S. Peel's Cook-Books.--Messrs. Archibald Constable and Co. are issuing shilling editions of the above. Two neaelittle volumes have already issued from the press, viz. "Puddings and Sweets" and I "Entrees." 130th are printed in lake, readable type. " Monarch, the Big Bear of Ulan." by Ernest Thompson Seton(Archibald Constableand Co., 55.) This is,we should imagine, Mr. Seton's masterpiece. "A more charming end pathetic animal story was never written," ' says the " Daily Express," and there is no exaggeration in this high praise. " Monarch "is magnificent among bears, and he would have made a fine character among humans. His life-story is full of enthralling intergst.' and the excellent illustrations in line and half tone by the author add to the charm of the book whether read by young or old. . . _</p>
        <p>Tolstoy is a name to conjure with, and the editor )of the " Sunday Strand" has made a hit with the artible on the great Ruasian novelist and philosopher which opens the October number. The photographs are really splendid, and rive a very adequate idea; of the multi- fa rious interests of this great man. Another article which i will be read with great interest is one on the ' unemployed and their belief. The photegraphs, which have been specially taken for this article, are very line. It is not only Welsh readers who will be interested in I the interview with Evan Roberts, which appears in this I number. It is written by Mr. George T. B. Davis, I and he handles his subject remarkably well. There has been much nonsense written about the Welsh Evangelist, , but this interview has a genuine ring, and is intensely I interesting. hfr. T. R. 'Burner and his Bible Class at Sunderland, the usual illuminating Bible Talks by the Rev. W. L. Atkinson, D.D., three splendid serial stories, and as many short stories by first-class writers make up a number of the "Sunday Strand" which probably excels anything which even this high-class magazine has ever done. • It is alwafs a difficult matter to select any particular story or article from such all-round excellence as is i found in the "Strand Magazine" month by month. I The October number is, needless to add, as fresh and I • map as its many forerunners, and the fiction is altogether delightful. We have reason to know that it is a matter of considerable dhliculty nowadays to 'find a really good short story which fulfills the numerous and exacting conditions laid down by an increasingly clis- r criminate Public; we are surprised, therefore, to find I such an excellent all round selection of short tales 'in a single number of a monthly publication. Father Capon continues the narrative of his life, and lin the present instalment he arrives at the incident— the terrible massacre of •January 22nd to which the previous chapters have formed only the, un- eventful but necessary prelude. An interesting article riving the views of well-travelled Celebrities on "Which is the most Beautiful Place in the World " makes interesting reading 4, The Chronicles of the Strand Club" are as amusing as ever, and most humorously illustrated, while "The Effect of Diet and Climate on the Faee" advances most etartingly novel views on the effect of Food v. Physiognomy. One of the curious deductions arrived at by the writer of the article is that a too starchy diet is largely respon- sible for a prominent chin The writer illnstrates his theories by means of ingenious diagrams. I The Portraits of Celebrities, a new series on novel lines, includes such well-known oeople as Sir John A ird, Bart., M.P. Miss Alice Roosevelt (a particularly charm- Linz set), and Mr. Rufus Isaacs, K.C., M.P., while I "Wonders of the World " are startling wonders indeed.</p>
        <p>The reader who does not find much to interest and amuse him in the latest number of the " Grand</p>
        <p>AIIIIINII</p>
        <p>Magazine "must indeed be hard to please. A bevy of the best short stories we ever remember to have seen within the pages of any one issue of any magazine, and something like thirty articles treating of subjects so various that it would require several columns of this paper to do them the barest justice, ought to be enough to satisfy the most omnivorous lover of good litera- ture. This extraordinary migazine, for 43d. contrives to give a budget of facts and fiction never before obtainable at any price. The " Cornhill Magazine" fpr October contains the customary instalments of "Sir John Constantine" by Mr. A. T. Quiller-Couch, and "The King's Revoke" by Mrs. Margaret L. Woods. The centenary of Trafalgar is commemorated in two articles, "The Picturesque Side of Trafalgar " by Dr. W. H. Fitchett, and " Napoleon and Nelson" by Mr. David Hannay, which throws curious light upon the Emperer's opinion of the Admiral. Reminiscences of a Diplomatist" tell of life at St. Petersburg before the Crimea War. In "The Peninsula of Gower " Mr. A. G. Bradley describes a little known corner of Wales with its Saxon population set like an island among the surrounding Welsh. "The Soldan's is a short story by Mr. Arthur H. Henderson. In a third article on" The New Chemistry" Mr. W. A. Shenstone, F.R.S., discusses the interpretation of radio- active changes, while the subject this month viewed "From a College Window ",is the perception of beauty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <p>Corporation for them not to have the prices published.</p>
        <p>COURCiIIOr PEMBERTON said he had LB;Ver heard that argument advanced before. He had never heard of any person tendering objecting to the publishing of the prices. Alderman SMETHURST said the Council had been told of the desire not to publish the price paid half-a-dozen times by the Chairman of the Gas Committee. He had said he would not and dare not publish figures. Councillor WiLicissoN said people had many a time told him that tfiey hoped the prices they quoted the Council would not be published as they could not quote the same rate to anyone else. Councillor Asacuorr said he was a member of the Board of Guardians and they never divulged their quotations. The minutes were adopted. PHYSICAL EXORCISES FOR SCHOOL CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>_ At a meeting of the Gymnasium/Committee the Chair- man submitted a report of the sub-committee appointed to meet a sub-committee of the School Management Committee in reference to physical instruction to school children. This report stated the Joint Committee were unanimously of the opinion that, for a period of at least twelve months, physical exercises should be given, as at present, by the teachers of the schools, but with the advice and assistance of the instructors of the Gymnasium Committee. In order to carry out this suggestion, they recommended that physical exercises be given in accord- lance with the regulations of the Board of Education by the teaching staff of each school, and that the instruc- tors of the GyMnasium Committee visit the schools to assist and advise the teachers in giving instruction in physical exercises; that the Gymnasium Committee provide a class at the Gymnasium for the instruction of teachers who have not got a certificate or qualification for physical exercises ; that the Gymnasium Committee provide a class at the Gymnasium for any teachers already holding certifi- cates who may wish to receive further instruction; that all scholars at the Warrington British and Trinity. National Schools shall use the Gymnasium for the pur- poses of physical exercises and that the exercises be given by the teachers under the direction of the instructor and his assistant. That the Gymnasium be used by the scholars and pupil teachers of the Secondary School on Wednesday afternoon from 2.30 to 4.30 and on Saturday morning from 9.0 to ILO and thatit be left to the headmaster of the Secondary School and the instructor of the Gymnasium to arrange the charges of exercises on the understanding that the mien teachers are to be instruoted in the art of teaching physical exercises; that the instructors of the Gymnasium Com- mittee make a monthly report of their visits to the schools to the School Management Committee; that for these services the Education Committee pay the Gymnasium Committee £lOO for the year commencing 2nd October, 1905.—The report was approved and adopted. Alderman TINNION In moving the confirmation of the minutes said he was pleased that they had arrived at a settlement. It was only right to say that there was probably a misconception and misunderstanding as to the objeets of the two Committees, but after a free and frank discussion the members came to it satisfactory solution. The Alderman explained that the report as printed on the agenda most not betaken literally, as there were several verb il alterations in it which he explained. These alterations would be made for the printed minutes of the proceedings of the Council. These alterations included the following : In the first aragraph after the words "Gymnasium Committee" were added : "Acting under the direec- tion of the School Management Committee." After the words "visit the schools" the words were added "to advise and assist." In the paragraph relating to the Warrington British and Trinity National Schools, after the words "given by the teachers " were added the words in place of "under the directian," "with the advice and assistance." Other similar alterations were made.</p>
        <p>Committor Ammon seconded, and the motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
