      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL Fortnightly Meeting.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL Fortnightly Meeting.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present :—Nfessrs. S. Woodward (presiding G. Pilling, C. Roylance, U. Eyes, P. Mountield, G. Egerton-Warburton,_ C. E. Linker, J. .H. Davies, T. Wright, J. Smith, T. Guost, C. Horton, J. Spruce, J. Hallows, J. Whitley, W. Weir, W. Turner, T. Antwis, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), J. Swinton and W. Diggle (surveyors), J. Farrington (inspector), and Dr. Adams (medical oilicor). THE HUSBY WATER WORKS.</p>
        <p>It was decided to complete the purchase of the under- taking of the Helaby Water Works, and a cheque for £4.257 13a. 3d., the _amount of the purchase was passed for payment.</p>
        <p>. . AN OPEN SEWER AT STOCKTON MATTI. Mr. Farrington reported the receipt of a number of comptaiuts as to the offenSive condition of the brook running from Walton New-road to Ellesmere-road, Stockton Heath. The writers compliined of thb smells arising from the brook, which they said, were dangerous to the health of the district. The brooa was undoubtedly in a very bad condition. Mr. Weir recommended the piping of the brook. Mr. Woodward : Would it not be-satisfactory if it was cleaned out?</p>
        <p>XL Weir: No. The sewage from 25 to 30 houses is turned into the hroo'z.</p>
        <p>Mr. Whitley said the brook was in a v:.ry insanitary condition. It was important to the health of the dis- trict that it Rhould be attended to immediately. It was decided to clean out, the brook pending the acceptance of a system of sewerage for Walton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>PIITBRECAK OF DISEASE.</head>
        <p>PIITBRECAK OF DISEASE.</p>
        <p>The Medical Meer reported thit there bad been an epidemic of measles at aelsby and be had ordered the schools to be closed. Chicken-pox had become an epidemic at Preston-oth-llill and he had ordered the closing of the infanta school. The Medical Officers report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>AN OBTraqCTION:AT STOCKT6N REATH.</head>
        <p>AN OBTraqCTION:AT STOCKT6N REATH.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that an obstruction had been caused in a. street off Fairfield-road, by the erection across the top of the street of a wall. The Clerk was instructed to call upon the owners of the property to remove the obstruction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>WARRINGTON IS NEVER DISAPPOINTED.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON IS NEVER DISAPPOINTED.</p>
        <p>The' r,eg,ular way in which local cases like the follow- ing, appear in our columns, undolbtedly impresses us in Warrington. There's snrceiy a street in our town, or a township in the whole county, that hasa't been represented. Weconfldeutlylook out every week for the local proof, and we're never disappointed. It's splendid encouragement for us. - Mrs. M. J. Andrews, of 63, Gaskell-strpet, Stockton Heath, Warrington, writes :—" Dear Sirs,—For seven- teen months I was unable to wash the floor. boecause my hae:c was so bad. Nothing seemed to help me until I began using Doerfs;llsokache Kidney Pills Now that I have been taking these pills a little while my health is greatly improved, 'as my neighbours have already noticed. ._ _</p>
        <p>—"T.-Certify the above is correct, and give you authority to publish the facts. Yours truly, (Signed) (Mrs.) M. J. ANDREWS." Does every cold affect the back, causing chilliness uneasiness or pain? Are there urinary troubles, sedi- ment, gravel, pains. in the sides or loins? D 6 you safer from dizzine3s. rheutnatimo,headache, neuralgia? , Is the skin sallow, the flesh swollen and flabby, the sight dim? Are you easily worried or tired out? Do you get depressed and hopeless? Doan's Backache kidney pills (see that you get Doan') are composed of pure roots and herbs that supply the worn-out kidney tissues with the constituent parts needed to rebuild their strength, end lit them for their groat work of purifyine the blood and driving out the soluble poisons. Deans' Backache Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ; niuepence). Of all chemists and stores, or post free, on receipt of price, direct from Pester-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Be sure you get , the same kind of pills as Mrs. Andrews had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>SWIAIMIMG GALA.</head>
        <p>SWIAIMIMG GALA.</p>
        <p>Successful Annual Event at Warrington.</p>
        <p>The annual gala in connection with the Warrington Swimming Club was held in the Legh-street baths on Tuesday evening. The event attracted a large attend- ance and keen interest was displayed in the various races. There was an excellent entry of competitors and in every event a close and exciting finish was witnessed. Results : Fifty yards schoolboy championship of Warrington and district : Heat winners, 11. Campbell, J. Eoscow, R. Beaman, R. Atkinson. Final : 1, R. Beaman; 2, R. Atkinson • 3. ElCampell. Won by two yard.. A few Inches only separated the second and third. The prizes were : The" Thornton" perpetual challenge bowl and gold medal, gold centre medal, and silver medal. Final for the Works Sqadron Race: The finalists were Crosflelds and Rylands Bros. and from the start the first named team took the lead winning by six yards Prizes, Sir Gilbert Greenall Challenge shield and gold centre medals for the runners up. , Fifty yards local handicap : het winners, C. Tuner (I),. A. Furness (3), J. Graham (7), G. Monks (11), J. Damtith (6), and H. Woolstencroft (5). Final : H. Woolstenproft,A. Furness,J. Graham sad G. Monks,won by a few inches. Time 35A secs. Prizes : gold medals pre- sented by Mr E. Draper, case of carvers, clock,and biscuit barrel. !he next 50 yards junior handicap open to boys under 17 years of age. The three prizes—silver watch, writing case, and clock—were presented by Councillor Lord. Heat winners: A. Wright, H. Sisson, H. , Howard, C. Taylor, and S. Roberts. Final : 1, Roberts ; 2, Howard ; 3, Taylor. Yards, inches. 150 yards championship of Warrington and district for the licensed victuallers' perpetual challenge trophy and gold medal. The second and third prizes were gold medals. The swimmers were : C. Turner, T. Pemberton (holder), R. Allen, and H. Ince. All got off well and Allen at once commenced to force the pace. At the and of the first 50 yards Turner retired. When 100 yards had been covered Inca was out of the race which lay between Pemberton and Allen. The two kept almost exactly level, but on the fifth length Pemberton gained a couple of yards, which, however, he lost immediately by slow turning. The two kept level until a few yards from home, when by a great effort Allen won by half a yard. Ince was almost half a length behind Pemberton. Time, lmin. 59 3-ssecs. The comic race caused no little amusement. Each competitor was attired in a hat, jacket, and trousers, and carrying a basket, had to dive inand swim to a pole placed in the water. At the far side of the pole he had to gather six corks and the first to do so, and to return home was the winner. The first prize, a set of military brushes, was won by J. Ashton. The second, a gold-mounted umbrella, by C. Woods ; and the third, a penknife, by T. Parkinfon. _</p>
        <p>The concluding item was a polo match "between Warrington and Blackburn. In the first half Warrington defended the deep end and the opening phases were slightly in their favour. Splendid work by the visitors' forwards transferred play in the home goal mouth, and Giltilan, getting clear, defeated Woods from short range. After the restart Blackburn immediately pressed, and the same player notched a second goal, half-time arriving with Blackburn leading by two goals to nil. The second half opened promisingly for Warrington,but Blackburn again assumed the aggressive, and another goal was added. Result :—Blackburn 3 goals, Warring- ton nil.</p>
        <p>During an interval a brief address was delivered by the Mayor who said that personally he thought every- one should learn to swim. Swimming was one of those pleasures which at times came in very useful, and as an athletic exercise was of a character calculated to build up the muscles in a manner which he thought was equal to any other sport— (hear,hear)—in addition to its being one of the most healthy forms of recreation. Corporations, he thought, should do all they possibly could to encourage bathing and he hoped the Warrington Corporation would do its best in that direction. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>COMING JAPANESE ISSUE.</head>
        <p>THE COMING JAPANESE ISSUE.</p>
        <p>With the end of the Russo-Japanese War and the con- clusion of Peace in the Far East, it is not at all surprising to hear of some Japanese and Eastern issues. The Japanese War Loans were greedily absorbed by investors directly the lists opened and very few applioants succeeded in obtaining the amount they wanted. The Hong Kong and Shanghai Banking Corporation, who so successfully issued war loans for Japan, will we under- stand shortly be authorized to receive applicatinns for one million 5i per cent debenture stock of the Japanese and Eastern Corporation, Limited. We have been favoured with a glance at an advanced prospectus of this coming issue and the Board of Control of the Col'- poration immediately strikes one as being one of the strongest features of the concern, most of the men co mprisingithe directorate beingithose who have been and are still associated with the East, and what is still more important we notice that there will be, as must always be the case in companies dealing in foreign parts, an Advisory Board of influential Japanese men appointed. What is perhaps a unique featare of this coming deben- ture issue, which by the way is being issued at; premium per Li stock, is that the whole of the debentures will be guaranteed by one of the largest insurance houses in the country, namely, the Norwich .Union Life Insurance Society, Limited, by means of redemption policies, and after such debenture stock under such policies has been repaid by the Insurance Company the investor will still retain antinterest in theWompany to the amount of his original investment, the debenture stock being a prat sharing perpetual security. In other words, this deben- ture stock, in addition to this regular interest of 5i per I cent. will rank for a share of the Corporation's annual - profits, and what is of more importance to the Investor there will he the guarantee as to the return of the 1 capital and premium by the Insurance Company. That men of the position and eminence, who are associated with this proposed Eastern issue, should ' have insisted upon any debenture stook offered to the 1 public under their auspices being guaranteed in the manner above described and more fully set forth in the prospectus, and giving the debenture stock holder • also a finger in the pie of the Company's profits beyond the original rate of interest, is only what was to be expected of men of such standinta and reputation. Trustees for the debenture stock holders, we observe, are the Right Hon. Lord Sufileld (who is also a director of the Norwich Unita') Insurance Company) and Sir Richard Cameo Temple, Bart. The Chairman of the directors is Sir Alexander. Wilson, who is Chair- ma i of tbo Mercantle Bank of India. Amongst the other directors we notice Sir Thomas D. [ Pyle, Bart., another director of the Northwich Union i Insurance Company, and Sir William Hood Treacher, late President General of the Federated Nfalay States. The solicitors advising the Directors are Messrs Ashurst Morris, Crisp and Co. Of course the applicants cannot expect to get a full allotment in an issue of thia nature, but we understand that the directors will give each applicant consideration. The Cornpiny's business is the taking part in the o.,ening up of the Far East, more particularly with regard to tr ainways, lighting, rail- ways, ac-, &amp;c. The new Anglo-Japanese Treaty recently concluded and published will have a far-reaching effect so far as this country is concerned in Japan and the East.</p>
        <p>iNlMllllik</p>
        <p>RUNGUN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Stockton Heath's Poor.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath's Poor.</p>
        <p>I The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of i Guardians WaS held at Dutton on Tuesday when there I were present :—Messrs. A. R. Norman (presiding) C. I Roylance, H. Eyes, P. Mounfleld, G. Egerton-Wart:m.- 1 ton, C. E. Linaker, J. H. Davies, T. Wright, J. Smith, IT Guest, S. Woodward, G. Piling, C. Horton,J. Spruce 1 W. M. Morris, J. Douglas, J. Hallows. J. Whitley, W I Weir, W. Turner, T. Antwis, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. lE. Hough (assistant clerk), Cook, De Prez and Pearson (relieving officers), and J. Cunnah (Workhouse mastr)- THE VACANCY ON THE ASSESSMENT COMMITTEE. Mr. Piling moved the appointment of Mr. Hallows on the Assessment Committee to fill the vacancy caused by the death of Mr. J. Mounfield. Mr. J. H. Davies seconded. He thought Stockton Heath should be represented on the committee. Mr. ‘Voodward moved and Mr. Morris seconded the appointment of Mr. Smith. On a vote being taken Mr. Smith was elected. Mr. Roylance asked how it was that the Assessment Committee never presented a report. Mr. Morris said he took it that the work of the committee was of a confidential nature and that it would not do for a report to be presented. Mr. Roylance : I give notice that I will move that they present a report. Mr. Ashton: You cannot dictate to the Assessment Committee. You can only, appoint the committee. Mr. Roylance: I wish I had had that information before.</p>
        <p>On the understanding that Mr. Roylance's desire shoulr: be considered by the committee the questioa was allowed to drop.</p>
        <p>• THE EITOCKTON HEATH POOR. lIR, HALLOWS CORRECTS A MISTAKE. Mr. Hallows said he would like to make an explana- tion with regard to some remarks which were made r‘t the previous meeting of the Board. Statements bed been made which created an erronious impression. The out relief paid in Runcorn during the past year was £1,493 10s. 10d., and in Stockton Heath £l4l 14s. Runcorn had 61 inmates in the workhouse, Stockton Heath, 2.</p>
        <p>Mr..l)Ouglas : I rise to a point of order, Mr. Hallow, is referring to a statement made by me. Mr. Hallows explained that owing to a statement which had been made the impression had been created that there was a large amount of pauperism in Stockton Heath. Continuing he said the number of children from Runcorn who were in the Workhouse was 23 from Stockton Heath one. The number fromMuncorn in the County Asylum 31, number from Stockton Heath two, and of these one paid its. a week and the other 7s. a week leaving one-seventh of a man in the Asylum to be Paid for by Stockton Heath. Those figures proved out of proportion altogether. Stockton Heath not only keot no children of its own but it helped to keep other children.</p>
        <p>Mr. Morris : Mr. Hallows should give the popula- tion and the assessable vain., of the two districts. Mr. Douglas rose to speak, but was ruled out of order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Crappenhall Coachman's Sudden Death.</head>
        <p>Crappenhall Coachman's Sudden Death.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at Beech Farm, Grappenhall, on Tuesday by Mr. Yates on the body of Thomas Overbury (51), who lodged with a Mrs. James, Beech Farm, Grappenhall.—Mrs. James said that at half- past twelve on Siturday he came home for his dinner and said he had had a blow on the crown of his head a carriage shaft having fallen on him. He told her that when he was closing the shaft-house door it banged against the shaft of a carriage and the shaft fell on his head.—Annie Edwards, maid to Miss Howson, said that at half-past six on Saturday hearing the dog barking she went into the yard, where she found 0 verbury lying on his face full length.—Dr. Jago said he saw 0 verbary about seven o'clock on Saturday night in the stable. He made a post-mortem examination on Monday night, but there were no marks externally. Internally the centre brain showed htemorrhage and was full of blood. The shock from the blov by the falling shaft might easily have caused a rupture of the blood vessel.—The jury returned a verdict to the effect that Overbury's death was due to hremorrhage on the brain, probably accelerated by the blow from the shaft.</p>
