        <p>THE STORY' OF • SMALL ROOSTER. .Small. Rooster was a very fine bird. He was clresse.d in green and gold feathers, and he wore. a high,' bright-red comb. And oh, how proud he was. He was proud of his green and gold dresi, and his, high, bright-red comb, and he was proud because he could crow so long and loud. :Not one of hie three big brothers or his five big cousins could crow as long and loud. That was all very well, but he should not have always crowed so long and loud just. at the breil of day, when almost every one else was €till-asle.t. "Why will you do it ?" said Pretty Hen to him one morning. Pretty Hen was his mother. "I don't know," said Small Rooster. Well, don't do it again," said his mother. "Yes, • ma'am—l mean no, ma'am," said Small Rooster.</p>
        <p>But the very next morning, as early as ever, Cock-a-doodle-doo-00-oo Cock-a-doodle-doo- oo-oo:" crowed Small Roostel at the top of his voice, waking all the fowls for a mile around and startling his mother so that she fell off the porch. Old Chanticleer ruled the roost; though he ARS too old to fly up to it. At the sound of Small Rooster's crowing, he opened his sleepy eyes and clucked angrily to Pretty Hen: "He's a. boisterous young scamp! Scold him well I" And then Chanticleer went back to his dreams. "Cluck-cluck-cluck-cluck-cluck," called Pretty Hen, as she picked herself up all covered with straw and said: "What did I tell you only yesterday 'morning, Small Rooster?" "Ma'am l" said he. "What did I tell you only yesterday morn- ing?" repeated she, shaking her toe at him. _ . _</p>
        <p>, "Not to crow again at break of day," answered Small Rooster.</p>
        <p>"Then why did you do it?" said his mother. "BecauGe—becau6e—l don't know," said Small Rooster.</p>
        <p>"Well, if you do it again, and don't know, you'll go without your breakfast," said his mother.</p>
        <p>"No, ma'am—l mean—yes, ma'am," eaid Small RooLter, and the very next morning</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>HOME-MADE SCARE.</head>
        <p>A HOME-MADE SCARE.</p>
        <p>Carl was a jolly little fellow, With eyes of blue and curls of yellow, •And rosy cheeks, and just the chin To hold £l, pretty dinaple in. He found himself alone one day, And wondered what 't NVB43 best to _play While hie mamma remained away. Pencil and paper soon he 'saw, And seized them both. Said he, "I'll draw An ogre like the one so grum Poor Jack heard growling ' Fee-fo-f um.' First, here's his forehead full of bumps, And then his nose with three big humps, And then two ears of 'normous size, And then two dreadful staring eyes, And then a mouth from ear to ear, With long, sharp teeth-like tusks." But here 'The artist, with eyes opened wide In fright, gazed on his work and cried, "Mamma, Mamma--:lome, come, please, do, I'm very lonely without you; And ohl Mamma, I'm so afraid Of this old ogrq, that I've made."</p>
