      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 7, 1905.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>[ALL RIORTS RIGBBRTED.I IS THE WORLD IMPROVING?</head>
        <p>[ALL RIORTS RIGBBRTED.I IS THE WORLD IMPROVING?</p>
        <p>SIR LEWIS MORRIS</p>
        <p>(Author of " Songs of Two Worlds," some years ago I remeinner resenting bit- ,1 terly the pedantry, as I then thought it, which postponed the advent of the new cen- tury until the year 1901. I wanted to be' rid at the earliest possible moment of the " fin de siecle " decadent spirit which had then afflicted society for a quarter of a century or more. Very likely the other side were technically right and I was wrong. But lam sure .1 was right on the merits, and that I did well to be impatient. rt may seem strange of course that the coming of a new century, which after all is an arbitrary division of time dating from a remote historical event, should have any such effect. But it in a fact nevertheless. No one could mistake the men or the types of feeling and thought of any of the last five centuries for those of any other. They mark a determinate progression in the history of the race, attended by varying condi- tions and finding expression in new and pecu- liar developments in literature and politics. Nor indeed is a century, so arbitrary a division of time as one might think. It means, of course, three generations of men—the grandfather, the father, and the ; the generation which is passing away, that which is in full possession of the earth, and that which is coming—the Past, the Present, and the Future, and it is natural to date these successive wavis of change from the beginning of the religion under which men live.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>REVIVAL OP LITERATURE.</head>
        <p>THE REVIVAL OP LITERATURE.</p>
        <p>From this point of view it seems Worth while to note whether any considerable change is to be seen in the tendencies of society, of literature, or of art in the century which is now emerging from infancy into childhood. It is too soon perhaps to pronounce a definite opinion, but it is my belief that there is a distinct change, and that it is on the whole for the better. The last quarter of the Nineteenth Century was the era of many disheartening tendencies in litera- ture, and of none worse than the decadent craze—the " fin de siecle " monstrosities which turned to Paris as the Mecca of true believers and saturated us with the obscedities and the unbelief of a handful of middle-aged debaucheres installed at the "Chat Noir." That was the day of Verlaine and his congeners, creatures sunk, not only in crapulous •vice, but in actual crime, as Villon (a much greater name) was centuries before. Many of us who retained enough self-respect not lo go to the full length of, self-abasement, and were still content to be English and inheritors of our great literary traditions, began to pipe in a ludicrous falsetto, foolish little metrical artifices—ballades and rondeaux and triolets and what not, till every monthly magazine was as full of senseless noise as a parrot-house. And now, Heaven be praised, however large these efforts loomed twenty years ago, there is dead silence ; the humblest magazine will have none of them, iind the reign of absinthe and worse things is over. There was another pleasant vice, too—the :fashionable crabe for obscurity. One or two writers of genius Bet up as oracles, by affecting an obscurity of ex- pression as artificial as it was insincere. You had only to leave out the article always or mis- place the verb as often RS mightbe, as if you were composing a sixpenny telegram, and repeat this throughout a long essay in prose or verse, and the thing was done. To this device, if you were in search of notoriety, you might add at the very commencement of your undertaking, so that they could not be overlooked, single sen- tences so long and involved ; with so many .parenthetical observations lugged in by the ears, that the resources of punctuation failed to bring relief and the 'gasping reader subsided long before he had gained the end of the page or more of the performance. Do these exercises allure now ? 1 think the answer must be</p>
        <p>frankly in the negative, and it is the new century, which bas slain them. The obscurantists are dead sine prole, or if any are still alive, they are old and dumb, or seldom use their voices. We who write to-day may or may not hav'e much to say, but we once more try to say it intelligently, and with due respect for the great language to which we have inherited. From all intolerable affectations of obscurity or mock simplicity of Saxon monosyllables, from ,Sartor Resartus, Sordello, or the Well at the World's End, the good Twentieth Century will deliver us forthwith, nay, has practically deliverkLus already.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>DECLINE OF SENSATIONALISM.</head>
        <p>THE DECLINE OF SENSATIONALISM.</p>
        <p>The same thanksgiving may be offered lip with regard to another development—the treat- iuent of the sex problem. For the decedents now happily dead and buried, life seemed a lour, duel between Man and Woman, each striving to rick the other by base intrigues, which could ouly end in dishonour. One had hoped that the last had been heard of this outworn literary . note, in these days of education, and the new view of women, not as possible inmates of the harem but as intelligent partners, with brilliant minds and pure souls. But the brute in- stincts die hard. To the same cause we owe, I sup- pose, thespopularity of sketches of warfare ; all bayonet thrusts and hand to hand fighting, and throats cut from ear to ear_; and good grey alli- gators patiently awaiting the sure fate which shall befall the succulent young workman who will slip from the new bridge into their ready jaws ; and the free use of the clog as a deadly weapon ; and stories of wolves and tigers, and a whole menagerie of cruel beasts; or if a domestic story is attempted,' elaborate descrip- tions of vitriol throwing or of incredible murders in impossible slums. It is a brutal literature this, with hardly a single redeeming moral trait. But it is certainly strong, it is in a certain low sense virile, and this in a time of bloody war and wholesale massacre appeals for a while to a public never attracted by namby pamby • romances in pseudo-Saxon English of an impossible medimval England. Well, all these exercises have had their day, and we are free from them at last, probably for the remainder of the century.</p>
        <p>TEE PURIFICATION OF TILE DRAMA AND OP SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>And there is an even greater mercy to be re l corded. If you go to the Play with your wife and daughter, you are no longer hurried about from one sleeping room to another to find some- body else's husband, who has DO business there, grotesquely dodging behind the bed curtains, or the cupboard in pursuit of objects into which it is better not to inquire. Slowly and tentatively the graceful little story, full of the pathos of youth and honest love ; or poetical adaptations of immortal -classical legends, or the wholesome ringing laughter of comedy touching the foibles of society with kindly satire and wholesome mirth, are raising their heads, and ousting the sordid sex-prob- lems, which have so long afflicted the decent play-gosr, who has long ago ceased to interest himself in breaches of the Seventh Commandment. Even the joys of gutter talk, and unaspirated English are, though this is not quite certain, beginning to pall. It seems possible that before the new century has com- pleted its first quarter all these peatilent affec- tations will be greeted by the new generation with polite incredulity as to their existence in our remote past, and with a genuine sneer of con- tempt. I hope I am not an incurable optimist if I express a hope that we shall soon ble rid, if not of the august Smart Set itself, at any • rate of the literature which depicts its vices, its gambling, guzzling and conjugal mishaps, so lovingly treated by more than one novelist, male or female. It seems to me that there is here, too, some improvement already. There have been fewer flagrantly bad books of this kind within the last few years. Mrs. Aphra Behn is appar- ently less studied than she was. In more than one ease passages too gross for Holywell street of yore, and indeed whole chapters, have been deleted by their authors in deference to a re-, awaliened source of decency. That this should. have to ls• done in ti" vountry of Scott. of Dickens did not regret the loss. To her friend, Lady Susan Strangeways (who had married the Irish actor, William O'Brien) she wrote in 1804, " am one who will 'keep the King's marriage day with unfeigned joy and gratitude to Heaven that I am not in Her Majesty's place .. It's pleasant to have lived to be satisfied of the great advantages of a lot which in those days I might have deemed unlucky. Ideas of 15 and 60, one cannot well assimilate, but • mine began at 11. for If you remember I was not near 15 when my poor head began to be turned by adultation in consequence of my supposed favour . . . ce reste I am delighted to hear the King is so well, for I am excessively partial to him, I always con- sider him as an old friend that has been in the wrong : but does one love one's friend leas for being in the wrong even towards oneself? don't, and I would not value the friendship of those who measure their friendship by my de- serving!. God help me, if all my friends thought thua.'</p>
      </div>
