      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>What Will the Voter Do?</head>
        <p>What Will the Voter Do?</p>
        <p>feel obliged to Higher Critic ' for his I criticism of last week, and also thank him for the courteous tone he adopted. L'erhaps he will kindly excuse me repeating the phrase "that a workingman who votes Conservative is some- what of a Curiosity." Why? Because it is the Conser- vative Party which stands in the way of the improve- ment of the condition of the masses of the people, of which the working class comprise the greater part. Because it is the Conservative party which bolsters up the House of Lords which has the power and generally adopts it, of blocking or altering wiseßills which have been passed by the House of Commons. Because it is the Conservative Party which upholds the Land Laws which educated and enlightened people are now beginning to see, are the chief source of poverty, +unemployment,and the high rents which the working class have to pay. Surely" Higher Critic" has had more than enough of the present Government. Well he will pardon me when I say ,he is somewhat of a "Curiosity "jibe has not. One would imagine he has had more than enough of a Government which has reduced his purchasing power by 3s. in the .0 by increased taxation and expense of living. If he hasn't, I think his wife has—i.e.—if he</p>
        <p>possesses one. And what about the South African war? Was it not , distinctly understood that we went to war in order that the workingman out there was to be made a free and indertendentvoter ? And was it not to be also an outlet for the surplus white labour of our own land ? And has not the Government scandalously betrayed its trust and treacherously broken its promises. Have we White or Yellow Labour? And is it not now unfortun- ately a matter of history that the White workingman is not wanted in South Africa, for fear of his vote. In 'fact the surprising and most curious thing is that after the extraordinary performances of the present Govern- ment there is to be found a single workingman who calls himself a Conservative. But as the bye-elections have shown they are becoming beautifully less. And -surely the Warrington workingman is quite as wise a man as is to be found in other towns.—Yours, etc.. FREE CRITIC.</p>
        <p>NEW CATHOLIC CLUB ROOMS. Opening Ceremony. This week the members of the Warrington Catholic 'Young Men's Society have entered into occupation of their new club rooms in Jubilee Buildings, Horsemarket street. The rooms are in every way suitable of the purposes for which they will be used,and are fitted with electric light. There are admirable billiard tables and other games are also provided. The Society is in a very flourishing condition and has bard work- ing officers in Mr. Owen Toole, president, and Messrs. J. Fagan and Austin M. Crowe, secretaries. The Rev. Father Swarbreck is the Chaplain. Any Catholic over sixteen years of age resident in the borough or its vicinity is eligible for membership, and persons of other denominations or non-residents may, at the pleasure of the committee, be elected as associates, being at the same time, entitled to all the privileges of members. The Rev. Father SWARBRECK formally opened the club on Friday evening. There was a large gathering over which Mr. Owen Toole presided. He was sup- ported by the Revs. Father Swarbreck and McGmthl Messrs. Charles Quinn (general secretary of the Centra Council), Charles Roddy, H. Rigby, N. 'Cunningham, .1' Sexton. Austin M. Crowe, and others. The PRESIDENT ably reviewed the year's work of the Society, and impressed upon the members the necessity for united effort in order to build up a strong Society, independent of any political party. He congratulated the members upon having acquired such comfortable rooms and trusted the Society would make its presence felt in the town. He called particular attention to the desira- bility of such an organization securing Catholic repre- sentation on public bodies and instanced a town where a Young Men's Society had accomplished a great deal in that direction. The Rev. Father McGill tvi in the course of an instructive speech gave the members sound and practical advice as to the working of a Young Men's Society gained by personal experience. Mr. Charles QUINN (secretary of the General Council) expressed the pleasure it afforded him to come from Liverpool to assist them, especially as the organization had made such gratifying progress. He proceeded to speak on the object of Catholic Young Men's Socities and laid special stress on the speech made by Arch- bishop Bourne at the general conference of Young Men's Societies at Leeds. He was glad the Warrington Society had joined the General Council Debating League as debating societies were a good means of culture for -young men. Ho appealed to the Catholic young men of Warrington to rally round the standard and join the I -Society and by so doing they would be fulfilling the desires of the present Pontiff and every eminent ecclesiastic of the Church, as well as of the late illustrious Pope. The Rev. Father SWARBRECK spoke on the religious side of the society's work. He eulogised the services of the President on behalf of the Society. On the motion of Mr. H. RIGBY seconded by Mr. Charles Roddy a vote of thanks was accorded to the Rev. Father Swarbreck and Mr. Quinn. A vote of thanks was accorded to the President for his services in the chair and his special efforts in the acquiring of the new premises, on tha proposition of Mr. A. M. Crowe seconded by Mr. N. Cunningham. The billiard tables were then opened with a match between the Rev. Father Swarbreck and the Rev. Father McGrath, which resulted in a victory for Father Swarbreck.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL. -- Alleged Nuisance at Penketh.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural Dis- otrict Council was held at Bewsev Chambers on Wednes- day when there were present—Mr. Brooks (presiding), Dr. Murray, Messrs. J. Ball, G. Mather, J. Backhouse, W. Houghton, J. C. Sutton (clerk), B. S. Newcombe (surveyor). F. W. Kerfoot (inspector), J. D. Sephton (medical officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>'CLOSING OF KILN-LANE.</head>
        <p>'CLOSING OF KILN-LANE.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Clerk to the Lancashire County Council in which it Was stated that the County Council had held an inquiry in referenco to the com- plaint of the Burtonwood Parish Council that the Dig- trict Council had not taken any action with regard to the alleged closing of Kiln-lane footpath, Burtonwood. The Main Roads and Bridges Committee of the County Council had considered the report of the inquiry and bad come to the conclusion that no further action be taken in the matter. The CIIAIRMA,N : That is very satisfactory to this Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>ALLEG ED NUISANCE AT.PENKETIL</head>
        <p>ALLEG ED NUISANCE AT.PENKETIL</p>
        <p>The CLELK reported that he had received a letter from the Periketh Parish Council, asking the District Council to take immediate steps to abate the nuisance created by the Fleetwood Chemical Company at Fiddler's Ferry. They were of opinion that a continu- ance of the nuisance would be a serious injury to the -district. The inspecter reported that during the month the had received 'letters from several prominent residents calling his attention to the abominable nuisance arising irom the worles, and asking him to take steps to have the nuisance abated. He bad also received a petition signed by 32 residents of Penketh, asking the Council to take steps to have the offensive trade stopped. On September 11th be visited the works, and found a .c nantity of animal matter being boiled in two open pans. The stench was very bad. He called the at- tention of the Secretary to the fact that the trade was being carried on without the consent .of the Council. On the 21st nit., ha visited the works and found offensive matter being boiled. There were also several tons of offensive matter. He called the manager's attention to the trade being carried on vad:said thatlegal proceedings would bo taken against them. On September 29th in company with the medical officer of health he visited the works and found the trade.still being carried on. There were several tons of offensive matter on the premises some of it being in a state of putrifaction. On Tuesday, of this week, he again visited the works in company with the Surveyor and found the fires banked up on account of the wind blowing in the direction of Penketh Lett the material in the pans was warm. He might add that there were several tons of offensive matter on the premises such as he had chiefly mentioned, together with tinned meat, fish, rabbit, and fruit. The following notice was posted up in the works :—" Important notice. No operations causing a disagreeable smell are to be carried on while the wind is blowing towards Penketh." Mr. S. F. FnANcomu, solicitor, Manchester, and Mr. Miller, principal shareholder in the company, attended the meeting and applied fora license. It was decided to decline to grant the license and the company were informed that a prosecution would be entered into if the nuisance was allowed to continue.</p>
        <p>THE HEAT TIT OF ME DISTRICT. Dr. SENT rozi reported that during the month there had been 19 births and 31 deaths. The death rate was equal to 36•4 per 1,000 as against :32. the average of the past three months and 231 for thecorresponding period of last year. Eighteen of the deaths occurred at the County Asylum exclusive of which the death rate for the district was equal to 173 per 1,000. WAaN LNG MOIORISTS. Mr. NEWCOMBE reported that he had received the following letter from the County Council. Your appli- cation for motor symbols was submitted to the last meeting of the Main Roads and Bridges Committee and the following resolution was passed. That 3 motor car danger symbols be erected near the Winwick church and crae et Hermitage Green.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>WATERWORKS' CONTRACT.</head>
        <p>THE WATERWORKS' CONTRACT.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER ACRIM9NIOUS COUNCIL DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>Alderman Francomb and the Non-Traders. -- -- LIVELY SCENES.</p>
        <p>Another heated discussion on the Waterworks Con- tract took place at the meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday evening, The following letter and resolution had been read at a meetin., of the General Purposes Committee and appeared on the agenda : Bewsey Forge, Warrington, September 15th, 1905. William Bolton, Esq.,</p>
        <p>Mayor of Warrington. Dear Sir,—l am desired as Chairman of a very largely attended public meeting, held in Parr Hall last night, to forward you the annexed copy of a resolution unani- mously passed thereat in reference to the Lawson Water Contract, and to request you to lay it before the next meeting of the Council at which the subject is dealt with. _</p>
        <p>I am, faithfully yours, JAMES DODDS. (RESOLUTION.) It was moved by the Chairman, seconded by Mr. Councillor Lord supported by Mr. m. Norbury Williams, and carried unanimously : That this meeting learns with alarm that a claim of .£57,288 19s. 4d. as against the contract amount of £2.1,400 has bean sent in to the Water Committee by Mr. George Lawson. and is astonished to find from a statement of the Chairman of that Committee at a I recent Council meeting that neither he nor any mem- ber of his committee had up to a few days ago seen the contract specification and accounts rplative to Mr. Lawson's claim. In these circumstances, this meeting strongly disapproves the action of the Town Clerk, the Water Engineer, and the Water Committee, in severally refusing to produce to Mr. Pemberton, a member of the Council and of the Water Committee, the papers con- nected with the Lawsott contract, such refusal being in direct and distinct contravention of the standing orders of the Council, No. 37 of which reads :—" That every member of a committee shall be entitled at any time to inspect all books, papers, and documents of such commatee, and shall at all times have access to all the 'works and undertakings of such committee." I This meeting further calls upon every member of the Council to support the Special Committee appointed on Friday last, in their endeavour to secure a full and I complete investigation of Mr. Lawson's account and claim. . _ _</p>
        <p>Alderman FIANCOMI3 said he objected to a repeated statement, which was incorrect, going on the vninntes. The MAYOrt : Is it worth while going into the matter ? _</p>
        <p>Alderman FRANCOMD : We must not have untruths going forth. I cannot understand how a gentleman of Mr. Dodds' position, of profassed religious convictions, should allow such statements to be made in an open meeting in a public hall, mostly filled, no doubt, with ratepayers of the town. Mr. Dodds knows perfectly well that there is a great difference between a claim of £57,58 and a claim after it has been altered and got ready for payment. Mr. Deas has reduced the amount I believe, to something like £35,000, or even less. Does it follow that because a man sends you in a large bill that you are going to pay it ? Alderman SMETHURST :</p>
        <p>Alderman FRANCOMB : The contract was arranged beautifully, and as clearly as any business man could arrange it—that Mr. Dees and Mr. Lawson together with their solicitors should put everything right in the end so far as they could. If they could not do that then it is arranged that an arbitrator shall be appointed who shall have full power to settle the matter. If there is a claim by the Corporation for say £5,000, which the arbitrator says is wrong he has the power to wipe it out. On the other hand, if the other man puts in an extra claim or the Corporation claim something they had no right to, he has the power to wipe that out. I cannot see anything plainer than that. We have been accused of being almost children, but I have no hesitation in saying that from the day the Water Committee was formed we have had some of the very best and ablest men in this Cor- poration on that committee. Alderman TINNION : I have never been on. (Laughter.) Alderman FRANCOMB : I am sorry for you. (Renewed laughter.) We have had such men as Alderman John Burgess, Alderman Joseph Harrison, and others, who helped with the purchase. Seventeen names were balloted for, and ten were put on the committee. We never had a bit of trouble until, unfortunately, Alderman Pier- point handed us over someone who knows everything. Never before had we any trouble. Every month we had a statement from the engineer and clerk of the works, and every day information was taken, and at the present moment, with all the noise that has been made, there is not a single charge made against anybody. Councillor Loup: Oh, yes there is. Alderman FRANCOMB : Insinuations, not charges. There is not one of you man enough to do that. Councillor Lorto : I made one against you. A Voicr, : Oh,_get off. You are not worth twopence. The Mavon : I must have order.</p>
        <p>Alderman FRANCOMB : We want nothing but fair play. We have never asked for anything else. Councillor PEMBERTON : Hear, hear; we do want fair play. Alderman PRANCOMB : You would say "Hear, hear," to a dog bark. (Laughter.) We have had no fair play. Councillor PEMBERTON : Shame.</p>
        <p>The Mayon : Don't interrupt. Alderman FRANCOMB: I have asked engineers, architects, and all who know anything about it, and they say our proper course was to pay. on account of work done, by the certificate of our engineers. What do we know about the yards of work done underground? (Hear, hear.) If a builder sent in a claim for a great sum of money he would get what the engineer thought he had legally earned. We could not go beyond that, but the one great thing I object to is this sum of 1E24,400 being stated time after time as being the com- plete contract sum. Everyone knows it is wrong. The £28,400 was for four wells and the tunnelling. Then we had to pay for the pumping,and hire of plant. If any- one has read the papers during the last two or three Necks he will see that it has been clearly pit what the contract is, and I am certain that the £57,288, as put in here will not be paid, but that it will be very much reduced, or else we shall all be very much disappointed. Councillor PEMBERTON : I must speak. Alderman Francomb says Mr. Dens has brought Lawson's charges down to £35,000. ft is like all the rest of the ridicu- lous statements Alderman Francomb has made in this Town Council.</p>
        <p>Alderman FRANCOMI3: They are truthful, and that is what you cannot say. _ .</p>
        <p>Councillor PEmiiEwro:v : There is no one in this Council who is capable of telling the truth but Alder- man Francomb. _</p>
        <p>_ The MA YOtt : Let ns have one speech at once—not half a dozen. _ _</p>
        <p>I Councillor PEMBERTON : Alderman Francomb, if he did his duty as chairman of the Water Caminittee, should acquaint himself with the bare facts of the case, and with what has taken place in his own committee, and if he did so he would never bo so foolish as to come before the Town Council and the public and tell us such a childish tale as he has told us to-night. It is appalling that the important business of Warrington— the work of a committee which has a capital of some- thing like oe 100,000—is managed by a gentleman who tells such fairy tales as have been told to-night. Our own engineer on his printed certificate, there is no getting away from it. has actually stated that Mr. Lawson has done .£39,000 of work for the Corporation. You produce that certificate if it is not trne. The MAYOR : You mean a series of certificates. Conncillor PENttummoN : No. I mean the last certificate. It says that work to the amount of £39,000 has been executed, and £15,000 has been actually paid on it. (Interruption). The MAYoR : Let him go on. Councillor PEMBERTON : I know what lam talking about. I have taken the trouble to ascertain the facts 1 but unfortunately some of the Coancillors won't take I trouble. It is not because the Council have not the capacity or brain power or are ignorant of business 1 but because they won't take the trouble to ascertain the facts of the case for themselves. They come here and say "It is not so," because some official tells thorn so.lf you go to the Borough Treasurer's Office you can see the thing in black and white, but they won't take the trouble to go there. It is simply wonderful that the town's busi- ness is conducted in such a manner. Councillor WILXINSON : Come to the point. Councillor PEMBERTON : lam coming to the point. Councillor WILKINSON: It is getting near Novem- ber. (Laughter). Councillor PEMBERTON: Nossmber has no terrors for me. It is a matter of indifference to me. The Mayon : Go on.</p>
        <p>Councillor : The difference in the amount certified and the amount paid is the retention money. The reason the contractor has not been paid £39,000 is because it is agreed in the contract that the Corpora- tion shall retain a certain amount. Councillor Wri.KnzsoN : We know that.</p>
        <p>Councillor Asiimorr : I hope the Mayor will stop Councillor Wilkinson int Irrupting. The MAYtut : I cannot stop either of them. I cannot tell what they are speaking about. (Laughter and in- terruption.) Order, gentlemen. Tell us something about what wo don't know. -</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : I am trying very hard to tell you There are a great many businessmen outside who understand it and T am trying to make a few in- side understand. The difference between the £35,000 and the £39,000 is the retention money and our engineer L has given Mr. Lawson his certificate that he ha.s done</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Exanii]ier, Saturday, October 7, 1905. NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>The Exanii]ier, Saturday, October 7, 1905. NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>£39,000 worth of work. There is no doubt about that At this point the Councillor ceased to speak. The MAYOR: Go on.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEmßuirrom : When you and the Town Clerk have done I will go on. The MAYOR: The Town Clerk is not interrupting you. Go on. lam listening. Councillor PEMBERTON : There is no good listening to me and the Town Clerk at once.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: I have capacity enough to listen to half a dozen. (Laughter.) Councillor PEMBERTON : This is a serious matter. I say deliberately that the contract was let to Lawson for £24,400, Alderman FRANCOMB : Plus—</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: Don't interrupt. Councillor PEMBERTON : Plus nothing, only contin- gencies which are put in every contract, not only Lawson's, but every contract you have here. Lawson's contract is no different to any other contract. It is 'true there may have been more pumping. I have said so many times. There may be extras on every job it is true. There may be legitimate extras, I don't question that, but I do question when it comes to the amount of £57,000 and no one is allowed to see the documents. There is no one in the Corporation from the time the work was commenced five years ago who has ever seen , Lawson's bill or checked it, except the water engineer and during some of this time he was not in a fit state to check the job. There was a resolution passed in this Council Chamber that if he did not alter his ways he would not be allowed to stop in this Corporation. I say without fear of contradiction that Mr. Deas was not in a position to superintend— The Mayon : I think I would leave that out' Councillor PEMBERTON : lam not going to leave it out. It is important. Alderman Tflozros : I think it is cowardly.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON :It is cowardly. Councillor PEMBERTON : Oh, yes! yerything is cowardly. The MAYOR: He knows perfectly well that Mr. Deas cannot answer him. Alderman TniNtoxi : It is not fair to make these statements publicly when Mr. Deas has not the power to reply. Councillor PEMBERTON : Let Alderman Tinnion reply for him.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION : If he had been an officer of my committej I should have defended him long ago and defended the action of the committee.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : He has a very able defender in Alderman Francomb. _</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON: lam talking about what the public of Warrington have to put up with. I uay deliberately that Mr. Deas was not in a position for months to look after the job and he does not know what state it was in. The MAYOR: That is not on the minutes. Go on with something that is on the minutes. COUnOillo2 PEMBERTON: It is difficult for any Man to go on and keep the thread of his argument. This is all done for a purpose. Certain Aldermen will get down and lick the boots of the officials of this Town Hall. You ought to see that I have fair play. \ The MAYOR: I will see you have fair play. Only I want yon to stick to the subject. Conncillor PEMBERTON: I say this: This is the biggest "job" ever perpetrated in this town. I say that this job should have been completed in 52 weeks. It has been admitted in this Council that £so—on the Town Clerk's assertion—would cover the extras on the adits. Then, there was no difficulty with the adits or driving the wells, and the contract ought to have been completed in fifty-two weeks. Eventually the contractor put pnmps down and completed the work. Why was he allowed to pump for three and a half years? Where was the engineer—the man who gave the certificate and was looking after the interests of the Corporation? What was he doing then? When the famous slack con- tract was on the Chairman of the committee came to the meetings of the Council and did not know a single thing that was going on in his own committee. On his own admissions here time after time he never knew a word about the contract.</p>
        <p>COURCiIIOr PE:MBERTON spoke with considerable heat and repeatedly struck the table before him. Alderman FRANCOMB remarked that Councillor Pembert,on's conduct was " Quite theatrical." Councillor PEMBERTON : It is preposterous when a man comes and lies like you lie here. The MaYort : It is all personalities. Councillor PEMBERTON : It is not personalities. It is facts. _</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN: You are only attacking the Chair- man. You are not giving the Corporation anything fresh.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : How rainy gentlemen in the Council knew what I have told you—that the con- tract had to be completed in 52 weeks? The MAYOR: No one believes it who is in the Connell. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : It is in writing in the contract. Instead of pumping one year he has been pumping four and a half years and we are asked to pay for it. It would pay him better to pump than to bore his edits. One bill came before the committee and I say deliberately that it came before them accidentally. That was the only bill of Lawson's I have seen during, the whole of the time I have been on the Council. The bill for driving and concreting the edits was £306, while the cost of the extra pumping during that time was £l,2oo—four times as much. Four times the price and you let him go on for four and a half years ! Alderman FRANCONIB : He could not get rid of the water.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON: But he did get rid of it. He put down pumps and finished the job. It was for our engineer, if he knew his business, to see that these pumps were put down at first. Why wasn't the water dealt with at the proper time? Alderman FRANCOMB said that Councillor Pemberton had had three years in which to bring the matter up. Councillor Pemberton : I have had three years on the Water Committee and my influence on the Com- mittee was worth that much (a snap of the fingers). You have admitted time after time that you knew nothing about it. Alderman FRANCOMB : I don't tell everything like you do. Councillor PEMBERTON But you are very fond of denying any assertions made. Alderman FRANCOMB : When lies are made in them. The Mum.: I must have a little fair play some way or other. Councillor PEMBERTON : Hear, hear.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON: What lime is your train, John? (Laughter) Councillor PEMBERTON : Nine-thirty ! (Laughter.) I had not intended to speak on the question if the resolution had been allowed to pass. But attacks are made on people who have gone to a tremendous amount of trouble to know the facts and men who are working for the public benefit. Alderman vrancomb drags in Mr. Dodds' name—a man whose shoes he is not fit to wipe. Then he talks about me telling lies. Time will prove who is:right, if we have fair play and the Investi- gation is a proper one. I have great faith in the .Com- mittee that has the investigation in hand. The MAYor: : With reference to that Parr Hall busi- ness there is no one with more cause for complaint than myself, but lam satisfied. They want to teach me my duty, and lam perfectly willing to be taught. Let us go on. Councillor EVANS said there was another point in the matter. This letter was submitted to the Council for confirmation or acknowledgment. The Chairman of the Water Committee had raised the whole of this question afresh at the start, and the Council could not be blamed for the discussion which had followed. It would have been better to have left the matter alone. They had appointed a special committee and should have left the matter to it to investigate and report. It was not fair to repeatedly say that a certain statement was abso- lutely untrue if only a statement to the contrary was given in support. Not the slightest evidence was brought forward.</p>
        <p>Alderman FRANCOMB : It is about this £24,000. If they say that was not a complete contract lam satisfied. Councillor EVANS : I know what the amount is ; and I know the difference between the money paid and the retention money. You even contradict that statement now, and I cannot listen, without saying anything, to the thing being gone into again and again here when there has been a special committee appointed to get at the facts. I hold you (Alderman Francomb) responsible for the scene to-night by having opened the question again. Another thing Ido not like to hear is to hear members continually ejactulating I" November," November." I abominably hate that kind of thing. It is unfair and unmanly. (Hear, hear.) I don't care who it is that says it. I don't always agree with Coun- cillor Pemberton, and I don't agree with some of the things he has said to night, but the elnditions are absolutely provoking. Such a condition of things ought not to obtain in the Council. The Mayor has not been able to control that element I complain of, and I say the time has come when we ought to thrash this matter out without falling out. Bytand by the ratepayers ought to know what is the real truth of the matter.</p>
        <p>The limit said Mr. Garside (the exeprt appointed 'to go into the contract), had all the papers and had signed the contract. The thing was well in hand and going on as fast as it possibly could. Re (the Mayor) would like the members to understand that the Council ought not to be brought into every squabbling match that could be raised by one or two outsiders. Also they did not want the town to understand that it was a ease of the Town Council versus the Non-traders. They wished it understood that they wanted the Non-traders to be at one with them and to carry on the business for the best interests of the town. They did not want the ratepayers to imagine there was nobody excepting one section to look after their interests. He defied any section that had come into the council lately to say that they bad done more to reduce the rates than the other members, lie hoped that all would sink their differences and do what they could together for the benefit of the town generally. It was time they got rid of this squabbling and series of investigations which did not seem to do any good. The minutes were then confirmed and the meeting ended.</p>
        <p>ELASTIC STOCKINGS. KNEK CAP-5, &amp;c.—For Varicos Veins and all cases of Weakness and Swelling of the Lag- Sprains, Sr:. They are porous, light in texture, and in- expensive, and are drawn on like an ordinary stocking. —Price is. Gd., is. Gd. and 10s. Gd. each. Postage free. WHITK &amp; WRIGHT, Surgical Instrument and Truss Makers, 96, Renshaw Street, Liverpool.—Advt.</p>
        <p>scrum but there is evidently a desire to try the new man, Garside, in better company. If the selection is exactly as given, Dickinson and Jordan had better change places, or there will be some queer three- quarter play.</p>
        <p>The Junior Rugby League starts to-day. The Clubs that have joinedare : Walkers Brewery 2nd, Victoria Park Rangers, Warrington Rovers, Warrington Rangers, Orford Hornets, Orford, Woolston, Padgate, and Rylancls Juniors.</p>
        <p>Fixtures for to-day, on ground of Ist namod : Orford v. Warrington Rangers, Victoria Park v. Walkers 2nd Warrington Rovers v. Orford Hornets, Woolston v. Padgate.</p>
        <p>Last year's cup-finalists renew acquaintance on Monday at Wilderspool. The Warrington team to oppose the Rovers will be chosen to-night. Kick-off 3.30.</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath Rugby Unionists played their first match at Kersal on Saturday and, of course, pro- vided easy victims for Kersal " A." It was not to be expected that a raw team would commence with any- thing but a defeat, and the score of 20 points to three points was not out of the way, especially considering that 14 of the points were scored almost before the Heathens had had time to look round. Afterwards they improved and were credited with one try against two by their opponents. It was better than being nilled and the young enthusiasts who sport the red and black colours may ho expected to improve rapidly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Rugby Football.</head>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovallst."l</p>
        <p>• Warrington gave a distinctly improved performance on Saturday in halving the points at Halifax. Not many clubs profit by their visit to Hanson lane, but Warrington have generally put up a very good fight and last January they won by ten points to nil, almost their best performance on Halifax soil. On Saturday, however, Halifax appeared likely to obtain ample revenge for at threequarter time the score was ten points to nil in their favour. Warrington had been trying hard all the afternoon with no luck to aid them, but now the fortunes of the game suddenly veered round and in ten minutes the leeway was made up by the aid of two goals from Preston and tries by Harris and Fish. In fact Warrington came very near to securing a victory for Preston's final shot at goal was only inches off the mark. The Halifax folk trembled while the burly forward was taking aim from such a convenient range, for defeat stared them in the face with the suddenness of a catastrophe. A. draw was the fairest result, for under the circumstances either side would have been unlucky to have lost,</p>
        <p>The following are the results of Warrington and Halifax engagements for the last eleven years. It will be seen that, while wins and losses balance, War- rington have been the better scoring team.</p>
        <p>Where Halifax Warrington. Year. Date. played. score. score. Result.</p>
        <p>_ G. T. P. G. T. P. 1894—Nov. 17 home 1 0 5 3 1 14 won. 1896—Jan. 1 away 0 2 6 0 0 0 lost. ISM—April. 25 home 2 0 S 00 0 lost. 1897—April. 10 home 1 1 8 1 210 won, 1900—April. 13 home 1 1 5 0 1 3 lost. 1900—April. 17 away 0 1 3 23 13 won. 1901—Nov. 23 away 1 2 8 t 1 5 lost. 1902—Jan. 11 home 0 2 G 1 0 2 lost. 1902—00 t 18 away 1 0 2 1 0 2 drawn. 1903—April. 10 home 0 0 0 1 0 2 won. 1904—Feb. 16 away 1 0 2 0 0 0 lost. 1904—April. 1 home 2. 0 4 22 10 won. 1904—April. 3 * 1 2 8 0 1, 3 lost. 1904—Sept. 10 home 0 0 0 0 0 0 drawn. 1905—Jan. 7 away 0 0 0 2 210 won. 1905—Sept. 3 away 2 210 2 210 drawn. *Cup Final at Salford.</p>
        <p>Halifax always play a vigorous, determined sort of game, and are one of thoso teams who rarely receive a severe beating even, under the worst circum- stances, They possess a very strong and clever set of forwards, and though the Warrington men have been considerably strengthened since that cup-final at Salford, they are not quite the equal of the blue and white scrummagers even yet. In the packs War- rington could hold their own ,on Saturday, but when it came to loose work they were clearly the inferior lot. Some of the running and dribbling by the speedy Halifax forwards was very flue, and it directly led up to the second try and goal against Warrington. At half-back and three-quarter Warring- ton could claim some superiority, but at full back Little reigned supreme. He was in great form, and kicked with the power of the proverbial mule. Hallam did not play at all a bad grime, but he was over- shadowed by a man, who, at his best is without his equal in the Union. Little kicked half the length of the field against the wind, and what work this entailed , on the Warrington forwards can be imagined. Once they "got there" and Boardman gave the Cumberland man a shock which he will remember for a while. -</p>
        <p>' The game was a partrnularly fast one and there was plenty of running and passing, though not of a high order. Warrington's combination has deteriorated considerably and just now they are not playing the game which has made them famous. I think much of the cause is at half back. We have two very good halves here in Hockenhall and Brooks, but they have the unusual fault of being too much alike in style. They play the same game, and the result is a want of variety which makes the Warrington attack less effective than it should be considering the quality of threequarter line. I should not like to suggest that either be permanently dropped, but I am certainly of opinion that J. Davies should be tried with these two Alternatively. He possesses nitich of the Welsh clever- ness and resource, and these are the qualities essentially lacking at the base of the Warrington scrumman.e. For instance I have not seen a cross-kick by a alf back this season, and there is no more dangerous move , when properly executed! Brooks and Hockenhall have plenty of energy, but they requiro judicious mixing with a stylist to be of the best service.</p>
        <p>Isherwood was the best of the Warrington three quarters and Fish the most disappointing. He by no- means looked for work and left a great deal to his centre, of course when a chance presented itself Jack was "on his toes," but if he had been as watchful and kpen as usual I think he would have overhauled Williams before the latttr ran over from half way, Isherwood and Jordan continued the charge alone and they were scarcely speedy enough to give the Halifax man a yard and catch him. However, Boardman and Belton were prominent among the forwards, while Preston's goal-kicking was" A 1," On the home side after Little, the best man was the little Leighite, J. Hilton, whose display at half back opened the eyes of the Warrington men. He has come on a lot, and the fact that he is first reserve for Yorkshire will make Mather-lane people feel rather sore. They could well do with him now, but he is a hot favourite at Halifax and likely to remain so. Jack Riley, Hammond, and Bartle are three old-stagers who also played a great game for Halifax.</p>
        <p>Runcorn, actually head the League table at present and are the only undefeated club. This is very good indeed for the " Linnets " who were somewhat despised a season ago. Still, I don't think they wilb he champions, and a few clubs including Warrington should head them before May next. We have seen the last of the 100. per cent. for a year. All points possible is too much for any club to achieve—unless it be the New Zealanders !</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows , For. Agst. Per-</p>
        <p>P. W. 1,. D. P. G. T. P. G. E. P. centage Runcorn 4 3 0 1 7 2 8 28 0 1 3 8750 Leeds Salford 5 4 1 0 810 17 71 5 4 22 80•00 Wigan 5 4 1 0 8 914 GO 4 7 20 80.00 Hull K.ll 5 4 1 0 8 5 6 28 3 4 18 80.00 Bro'ton R 5 4 1 0 810 18 71 5 9 37 80.00 Keighley 5 4 1 0 810 12 56 4 3 17 8000 York 5 4 1 0 8 7 8 38 3 4 18 8000 Oldham 7 5 1 11110 91 83 7 3 23 7857 Bradford St. Helens 3 3 2 0 612 17 75 610 42 60.00 Halifax 5 2 1 2 6 510 40 G 4 24 60.00 Hull Hunslet ‘Varrington 5 2 2 1 5 5 4 22 510 40 5000 IVak'flold 5 2 2 1 5 4 4 20 7 9 41 5000 Dewsbury 5 2 3 0 4 2 4 16 712 50 4000 Leigh Swinton 5 2 3 0 4 1 4 14 4 7 29 40•00 Bnamley 4 1 2 1 3 0 3 9 3 7 27 37.50 111illom 4 1 2 1 3 3 4 13 613 51 37.50 Normanton 4 130220 4 610 42 2300 Widnes 4 1 3 0 2 0 2 6 3 3. 15 2500 Cnstleford 4 1 3 0 2 3 1 9 815 61 2500 Hunleld 4 1 3 0 2 511 43 4 8 32 2500 Rochdale LI 5 0 3 2 2 1 2 8 3 7 27 2000 Pontefract. 3 0 2 1 1 0 1 3 3 3 13 1666 Morecambe 5 0 4 1 1 3 5 21 918 72 10.00 Batley 5 0 4 1 1 6 1 15 7 8 38 10.00 Brighouseß 4 0 4 0 0 2 2 10 4 6 26-10000 Barrow 4 0 4 0 0 2 3 13 8 17 67-10000 The points are two for awin and one for a draw. The percentage is calculated upon the basis of the total points possible.</p>
        <p>The first county match of the seasln takes place to- day at Wigan, where Cumberland and I.ancashire will be in opposition. Fish aud Boardman are down to play for the Palatines, who should secure a fairly easy vlctory•</p>
        <p>Warrington's match is with Barrow, who have yet to secure their firit point. They will n'A have much hope of obtaining it at Wihierspool, bat with the somewhat experimental side Warrington are placing in the field, I don't think there will ha much to give away. harrow are not quite so bad a team as their record would suggest. They however, so far, played none but clubs unmistakably more powerful than themselves.</p>
        <p>The Warrington teamwiUbe : Hallam, full back ; Dickinson, Isherwood, Jordan, and Harris, three- quarters ; Hockenhall and Garside, half backs ; Preston, Thomas, Jolley, Belton, Harmer, Cook, Swift and Ernest Lunt, forwards I thought J. Davies would have been given an opportunity behind the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Hockey.</head>
        <p>Hockey.</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath club opened the season in brilliant style, for not only did the first team make Alderley Edge look like a very second-rate eleven, winning by no loss than 6 goals to 2, but the second eleven journeyed to Alderley, and beat the home side by 4 goals to 1, and this on a ground much in favour of the home side, owing to its pecultr formation.</p>
        <p>The first eleven must thank the forwards for their brilliant victory, the passing was excellent, and the speed and dash of the home quintette proved altogether too much for the visitors' defence. The right wing was especially effective, Richardson's shooting and Day's rushes being one of the main features of the game. The goals were scored by W. Richardson 5, C. H.-Bond 1.</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath defence did not give one that feeling of confidence, which was the case last season, and although it is hardly fair to pass adverse criticisms upon the first match of the season, it is to be hoped there will be a general tightning up, otherwise all the brilliant runs the forwards may make in future matches, will not save the club from defeat, when opposed by a club pos- sessing an equally good quintette.</p>
        <p>The second eleven match was of a fairly even descrip- tion, but in the finer points of the game the visitors were much bette, than the home side. Stockton Heath are fortunate in having such a good forward as J. Baxter in theii second team, and although he did not actually score, he was the chief factor in seqeral of the goals. Hawtin and Fairclough played well at back,anl if the present side will only keep together, a record for the club ought to be established this season. The goals wore scored by W. Howard 2. 11. Dolan 1. W. Linnel 1.</p>
        <p>' This week the club play Huyton, the first team play- ing away by arrangement. Last reason both first and second teams were defeated, the first by 2-1 and the second by 4-1, but still I shall although it may be ever- bold. look for both elevens to reverse last season's results.</p>
        <p>The following temns 'have been chosen to represent Stockton Heath.—lst v. Huyton at Huyton: E.G. Pierpoint, goal ; E. Pierpoint and E. E. Woods, backs ; G. Hatton, Eric Fairclough and L. Fairclough, halves; C. H, Bond, R. Pierpoint, W. H. Banks, W. Richardson, and T. Day, forwards. Referee, N. W. Bellamy, Esq. Train leaves Central 1.50. 2nd v. Huvton at the cricket ground: J. Gray, goal ; P. B. Hawtin and E. Fairclough, backs ; J. T. Oliff, J. Houghton and A. Hodgkinson, halves: L.Plerpoint.J. Baxter, W.Howard, H. Dolan and W. Lionel, forwards.</p>
        <p>Latchford opened their season with a home match against Sale Moor, and after a hard game they secured the verdict by two goals to one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>JUNIOR LIBERAL ASSOCIATION. Opening Meeting. ADDRESS BY MR. J. HOWES.</head>
        <p>JUNIOR LIBERAL ASSOCIATION. Opening Meeting. ADDRESS BY MR. J. HOWES.</p>
        <p>The recently formed Warrington Junior Liberal Association is alreuds giving signs of groat prosperity. The membership which is steadily increasing, is considerable, and the Association gives promise of taking, in the near future, a by no means unimportant part in the political life of the town. The inaugural meeting was held on Monday evening at the Liberal Club when Mr. J. Howes delivered the presidential address, taking for his subject "The origin of the two political parties." Mr. HowES at the outset of his speech illustrated the origin of the constitutional Parliament of England from the time of the early :Saxons down to the present day. We heard nothing said Mr. Howes of the two political parties until after the sign ing of the Magna Charter when Simon do Monfort, one of the barons, undertook to call Parliament together but in a short time the barons protested against sitting with the repre- sentatives of the common people and they were allowed to sit in a separate chamber, and for the first time, in English history there came into existence the two schools of political life, the House of Lords and the House of Commons. In the reign of Charles I. there came the first great division of parties—the court party and the anti-court party. In the reign of Charles 11. the two parties became thoroughly divided into two organized camps—the Tories and the Whigs. Ta a short time the strife between the two be- came acute and great battles began, battles with far- reaching interest for the nation. The land question was at the bottom of the mischief as it was at the present time and as it would continue to be until we had a more rational and sensible system of land tenure. Class 11. was compelled to accept any legislation which the nobles provided and they changed the whole system of land tenure and from land-holders became land-owners The revenue the nation had previously enjoyed from the land ceased, and the system of customs and interal taxes came into existence. That heavy taxation caused misery and poverty ani it soon became evident to the Whig leaders that the first battle which they would have to fight would ben battle upon the franchise. The battle commenced early in the 18th century, but it was until 1830 that the first bill for an extended franchise was passed, and rejected by the House of Lords. There was another election and a Liberal party was again returned. The Franchise Bill was again passed through the House of Commons and again rejected by the House of Lords. A revolution followed, and the result was that when a Liberal administration was again elected, Earl Grey got permission from the King to create a sufficient number of Peers to enable the Franchise Bill to become law. It was not necessary for him to take that step as the Tory Lords walked out of the House and the Bill, which enfranchised half a million of the middle classes of the country became law. Immediately that concession was granted to the people, a number of earnest reformers began an agitation for a further extension of the fran- chise. That was continued until 1867 when another Bill r.as passed which gave a million votes to the workingmen of the towns. in 1886, with a Liberal Government in power, the franchise was extended to the counties. That great question was not however settled, and at the present time an agitation was being carried on for manhood suffrage and woman's suffrage. In 1836 the Tory party took one side and the Liberal party the other as they had done in the great battles for the abolition of the corn law and of tariffs and in the struggle for education and for the removal of slavery. in conclusion Mr. Howes urged his hearers to a more close and thorough study of political history. (Applause). Questions and discussion were invited. Answering Mr. Guest, Mr. HOWES said the question as to which party carried the Franchise Bill of 1867 was avery complicated one. in 1880 there was a Liberal adnunistration with Mr. Gladstone at the head and a Reform Bill was introduced. The Government was defeated and Mr. Gladstone at once resigned. The Queen sent for Earl Derby and asked him to form an adminis- tration. This he consented to do without appealing to the country,with the result that he was in the minority. The agitation in the country for a reform Bill was continued with such vigour that the Tories took alarm and Mr. Disraeli brought in a number of resolutions on the franchise question. The Liberal party, who though out of Wilco were in a majority, decided to reject those resolutions and they did so. Two days afterwards the Ten Minutes Bill " was introduced but not a single clause of that Bill ever became law. The Act which was passed was altogether different from the Bill which was introduced. The original Bill gave the vote to schoolmasters of the Church of England, to men having a certain amount of money invested in Government securities, and to men who had passed through the universities and to workingmen householeers who paid their own rates. The Liberal party wrecked that Bill and out of the wreck made household suffrage. At the election which followed immediately the Liberal party was returned to power. (Applause.) M. Font): How many progressive measures have been rejected by the House of Lords during the past 30 years? Mr. Hoxvgs : Practically every one. Continuing, he said the House of Lords did not openly reject any peasnre but by unnecessary delay they prevented their being passed. In answer to another question Mr. HOWQ9 said all the reforms which he had mentinoned,the abolition of salary the abolition of the tax pon newspapers, the granting</p>
        <p>of the franchise and all such measures had been the result of Liberal activity. The question was asked as to whether it would not be better to have no king,as said the questioner they always stood in the way of reform. Mr.HowEs : We had got some reforms from monarchs so it is not correct to say they always stand in the way of reform. Continuing he said the present King had shown more radical tendencies than any monarch. Although they might not be much enamoured of Kings they must not forget that it was an exceedingly wise natiod which could govern itself. It was not the kings who stood in the way but the circle of hereditary aristocracy. In answer to Mr. Ford, Mr. Howes said it was a mistake to suppose that tithes had been abolished. Mr. Porto : Will a Liberal Government sweep away all these obnoxious things? Mr. HOWES pointed out that a Liberal Government would first of all have to turn its alteration to o thor things such as Chinese labour and education. Mr. FORD moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Howes for his address. In Mr. Howes, he said, they had a worthy president. He hoped the youth of Warrington would take advantage of membership of the Association and endeavour to educate themselves in progressive principles. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>- Mr.- GuEs): Vecondeil and the vote was heartily accorded.</p>
        <p>Mr. HoWES announced that at some future date he would address them on the origin of tithes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>NOVEMBER ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE NOVEMBER ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>BEWSEY WARD LIBERAL COMMITTEE MEETS.</p>
        <p>A well attended meeting of the above was held on Saturday last, to consider the November elections, Mr. Joseph Owen presiding. Among those present were Councillors A. Peacock and P. Peacock,Messrs. William Furness, J. Morton, J. Critchley, P. O'Brien and .1. Howes, jun.—Councillor P. Peacock gave a rough out- line of his work on the council, and invited those present to offer any suggestions or criticisms. An interesting discussion took place principally on the Gas and Tramways Quostions,and it was unanimously decided that Councillor P. Peacock be heartily thanked for his valuable services, as a friend of the working classes on the Town Council, and that he again be asked to allow himself to be nominated as a candidate at the forthcoming election. Mr. Peacock thanked them for their kind and enthusiastic offer of support, and said that if he were nominated and placed at the head of the poll, he would do his utmost to serve the working men of Bewsey Ward to the best of his ability. In reply to a vote of thanks the Chairman (Mr. Joseph Owen). said he had now been connected with the Bowsey Ward Committee for 20 years, and he was sure the com- mittee was stronger now than it had ever been, and the great interest exhibited at that meeting was very encouraging. He hoped they would all do their utmost to secure the return of their ,:andidate, and by so doing to show that Bowsey Ward would not be found wanting, at the greater fight,which would shortly be upon them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>WHITECROSS WARD.</head>
        <p>WHITECROSS WARD.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Liberal Committee for this ward was held in the Workingmen's Mission Schools,Thewlis- street, on Monday evening. Councillor James Evans presided and the attendance included Councillors Brough and Barton, Messrs. Henry Roberts, Nathan Cook, and J. HONVeg, jun. (registration gent). The Chairman briefly introduced the business •of the meeting, and as he was the retiring member, vacated the chair in order that there might be full and free discussion. Councillor Brough then took the chair, and the meeting unanimously decided to ask Councillor Evans to again stand as the Liberal candidate. A vote of thanks was also accorded I to him for his past services.—Councillor Evans thanked the meeting and accepted their invitation, promising that if elected he would continue to do his best in the interests of that Ward particularly, and of the town in general.—A vote of thanks to the Chairman concluded the meeting. FAIRFIELD WARD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Councillor Craili-S-anii-o-11-nce.dh-i-s-Intention to again seek re-election as the representative of Fairfield Ward. Mr. Craik will tight as an independent. ,</head>
        <p>Councillor Craili-S-anii-o-11-nce.dh-i-s-Intention to again seek re-election as the representative of Fairfield Ward. Mr. Craik will tight as an independent. ,</p>
        <p>.ORFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Liberal Committee of this ward was held in Silver-street schools on Wednesday evening. Councillor A. Peacock presided and there were also pre- sent :—Councillors P. Peacock and John Wilkinson, Messrs. A. H. Sanders (ward secretary), and J. Howes, jun. (registration agent). After some discussion the meeting was adjourned with a view to finding a suitable candidate to contest the ward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>COUNCILLOR STOTT AND:ORFORD WARD.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR STOTT AND:ORFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>Connelltor Stott will again seek re-election as a Labour candidate in Orford Ward. Resolutions in sup- port of his candidature have been passed by the War- rington branches of the Amalgamated Society of Engineers and the Amalgamated Union of Cabinet- makers. CONSERVATIVE WARD ISIEETINO.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Pilling presided over a meeting of the Latch- ford Conservative Committee hold at the club on Tuesday night- Councillor Pemberton was thanked for his past services and again selected as candidate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>WARRINGTON CHAMBER OF COMMERe E</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHAMBER OF COMMERe E</p>
        <p>The Meeting at Liege.</p>
        <p>At the last meeting of the Council, the President (Councillor J. Fulton Smith) in, the chair, the Secretary (Alderman Bennett) submitted his report on the Autumnal Meeting of the Associated Chambers at Liege, and particularised the various matters discussed.</p>
        <p>There were four resolutions upon the canal question, sent in respectively by Bristol, Belfast, Worcester and Warrington. The latter, it will be remembered, was adopted by the Special General Meeting of the Chamber held at the Town Hall. A very interesting discussion on the subject took place, and the four resolutions were ultimately withdrawn in favour of the following resolution moved by Sir J. T. Brunner, Bart., M.P. : "That this meeting thank the members of Parliament who supported the two Bills for the co-ordination, improvement and extension of English and Welsh Canals, introduced into the House of Commons last Session, and bogs them to continue their efforts with the object of bringing the United Kingdom into line with the progressive countries of Europe, and that the Executive Council be requested to take such steps as they may consider desirable in support of the policy of the Association."</p>
        <p>This was carried with the following addition, moved by Mr. E. PARKES, M.P. :—" That a deputation be appointed consisting of representatives of the Associ- ated Chambers of Commerce to wait upon the Board of Trade urging the necessity of taking prompt action to promote legislation upon the Canal Question, in accord- ance with resolutions passed from time to time by this Association. And that Members of Parliament who have taken a leading part in the House of Commons in this subject be asked to join in the deputation." An International Congress of Chambers of Commerce followed, at which many important resolutions bearing upon the powers and duties of Chambers of Commerce were adopted.</p>
        <p>"The Belgian Chambers treated us" (adds Alderman Bennett) " with the greatest hospitality. At Ostend, Li4e, Brussells and Antwerp we were received by the respective Burgomasters in their beautiful Town Halls; at Brusssels and Antwerp the Chambers of Commerce, magnificent institutions, whose offices adjoin the Exchanges, likewise hospitably entertained us. At Brussels, we were honoured by a visit from the King of the Belgians, who spent nearly an hour in con- versation with the English delegates. A banquet and special performance at the principal theatre were also provided at Li4ge, and the delegates had opportun- ties of visiting either the splendid zinc works at the Vielle-Montamne, the National Manufactory of Arms, the Ougree-Mbarihaye Iron and Steel Works, and the fine establishment of the John Cockerill Company at Seraing. Picnics were also organized to Spa, one of the oldest watering-places in Europe; to the great Dam of the Gileppe; and to the wonderful grotto of Han at Rochefort. I visited the zinc works and the grotto, and was immensely pleased with all I saw. At Antwerp an excursion on the Scheldt gave all of us insight into the amazing progress of that noble port and a lesson in enterprise which I wish that we could learn in Warring- ton. The Belgian Government provided us with special trains between the leading centres on the splendid Government railways, and everybody appeared to vie with everybody else in giving us a good time. The exhibition ikelf was highly interesting, although the United Kingdom was most inadequately represented; and Licige the centre of the iron trade, was an object- lesson as to how the two ideals of beauty and utility may be combined, a lesson emphasized by nearly every continental town I saw." The Secretary was thanked for his report. IMPERIAL DEFENCE.</p>
        <p>The recent address by Lord Roberts on this subject to the London Chamber of Commerce was considered carefully, and the important bearing of the question on Imperial Commerce fully recognized. It was decided to arrange an early meeting at the Town Hall (subject to the Mayor's consent) to consider the matter, and to invite Admiral Mann and Sir E. Wolfe Barry to address the same.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>NEW MEMBER.</head>
        <p>NEW MEMBER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Owen Toole was elected a member of the Chamber. CDMMERCIAL INTELLIGENCE DEPARTMENT. The Secretary has since received a letter from the Commercial Intelligence Department of the Board of Trade, which will no doubt be interesting to local manu- facturers. The letter points out that it is desirable that persons wishing to obtain any information relating Ito trade in foreign countries should, before addressing his Majesty's Consular Officers abroad, make enquiry at the Commercial Intelligence Branch, 73, Basinghall- ; street, London, B.C. if this course is adopted the I enquirer would in many cases avoid considerable delay, and his Majesty's Consular Officers would be relieved of much unnecessary labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. New Assistant Master Appointed. THE UNEMPLOYED QUESTION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. New Assistant Master Appointed. THE UNEMPLOYED QUESTION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held on Wednesday, Mr. J. Bißinge presiding. There were also present :—Mrs. Clay, Mrs. Wilkinson, Miss Broadbent, Rev. Dr. Mercer, Dr. Patterson, Messrs. W. fiickman (vice-chairman), A. J. Jolley, R. Forshaw, S. Davies, J. Payton, T. H. Ashcroft, G. Thompson, i‘r. W. Seton, A. Brooks, R. Flannagan, G. Mather, J. C. Sutton (clerk), J. F. Awbery (master), and A. Wright (superin- tendent Padgate schools).</p>
        <p>THE ASSISTANT MAS'TRII. At a meeting of the House Committee held on Sep- tember 29th, from forty applications received for the.: position of assistant master the following four were selected to appear and their reasonable travelling expenses were directed to be paid out of tbe Master's petty cash :—George William Ball, Chesterfield; Herbert Todd, Prestwich; Percy Winter, Gainsborough; Horace Golby, Darlington. The Rev. Dr. Mercer, Messrs. Forsbaw, Thompson, Brooks, Jolley and the , Master were appointed to interview these candidates on ,~ Wednesday, prior to the Board meeting. Mr. FORSHA.W announced that toa sub-committee unanimously decided to recommend the appointment of. Nlr. Todd. He moved that their action be confirmed. The CHAIRMAN pointed out that only two candidateS —Messrs. Ball and Todd—appeared before the sub- committee. Mr. Todd, who was unanimously selected-, was twenty-four years of age. He had been in the Poor Law service for four years. Private inquiries fully con- firmed the excellent testimonials which he submitted. It was unanimously agreed to appoint Mr. Todd at o Wary of £35 with the usual allowances.</p>
        <p>COMPLIMENT TO THE OFFICERS. Mr. Moonsom, ono of the inspectors of the Local Government Board, wrote regretting the severance of his official connection with the Poor Law. Few spheres of activity were so fascinating and the nineteen year. spent as inspector had been some of the happiest ot his life. He conveyed to the Guardians his deep sans') of the courtesy and goodwill which had always been shown towards hira when he attended their meetings and took the opportunity of saying " farewell" to all the officers of the Union with whom he had been brought into contact. Their unwearied assiduity in the discharge of onerous duties and their much-enduring kindness towards the unfortunate and oftentimes undeserving people under their care had been forcibly brought to his notice in the course of his work. He left them with the greatest respect for the noble ser- vice to which they belonged. THE VAGRANCY QUESTION. The CLERK read a letter from the hon. secretary a the North-Western Poor Law Conference enclosing the following resolution on the question of vagrancy "That it is desirable the committee constituting the conference recently held at Lancaster to consider the subject of vagrancy should lose no time in considering the resolutions approved at that conference and further that the North-Western Conference urges the Boards of Guardians who have not yet appointed representatives to serve upon that conference to do so." Mr. MATHER : Is our representative on that com- mittee allowed to pledge us to any expense? The CLERK : I take it that no definite course will be taken without first submitting it to the Boards corn- pi ising the Conference. THE UNEMPLOYED ACT. A letter was read from the Women's Local Govern- ment Society for the United Kingdom expressing the hope that the Board would nominate one or more womel7 for election to the local Distress Committee with a view to rendering adequate the co-operation of women in the work.</p>
        <p>Councillor: F. Stott, secretary of the Trades all ' Labour Council, wrote with reference to the election? Board of Guardians representatives to act on the Dis- tress Committee which would be formed by the Torn, Council, and said that what the Trades Council desire° was that the Board should select Mrs. Wilkinson as one 'of the eight representatives. She was almost con; stantly in touch with the poorer working classes au" moreover, was a direct labour representative. Mr. Stott also enclosed a copy of a resolution adopted at the last, meeting of the Trades Council, to the effect that it wee desirable that as many meetings as possible of the Tool Council and the Board of Guardians, be held in the evening, so as to give an opportunity for direct labour representatives to become members of the two public bodies. Mr. MATTIER : How many Guardians rdpresent the Borough of Warrington. The CLERK : Ten. While the Act of Parliament doeS not limit you to the Warrington Guardians it seems tr: me that the Borough of Warrington only is concernet? with the committee on which the Guardians woule sit.</p>
        <p>Mr. MATHER : I understand that if any money is tc. be found it will have to come out of the borough rates. The CLERK : I cannot advise you on that point. , Mr. THOMPSON : I think it states in the Act that in the £or not more than Id. can be levied on the borough rate. The CLERK : That will have reference to their work- ing proceedings. At present there is no arrangement 55 to how the money is to be found. Mr. TttomrsoN : If the borough rate is to find 06 money let us elect eight Warrington Guardians. If the' Union is to find the money the whole Union should have representation on the committee. Eventually it was decided to elect eight of the Warrington Guardians to serve on the committee- Messrs. Hickman and Ashcroft declined to act and the representatives were appointed as follows :—Mrs. Clay' Mrs. Wilkinson, Miss Broadbent, Rev. Dr. Mercer. Messrs. A. J. Jolley, W. Payton, G. Davies and Bolton. FREE CHURCH co mic in AND WORKHOUSE SERV ICES. A letter was road at a meeting of the House Coo" mitten from the Rev. J. F. Matthews thanking tile Guardians for their vote of thanks foe the volUntati religious services given at the NVorkhouse.</p>
        <p>ComiwzßetAr. FAILLTRES.—According to " Kerriq Mercantile Gazette," the total number of Commercia he Failures Irecorded in England and Wales during t: week ending Friday, September 29th, was 171, viz•Lqi New bankruptcy proceedings published in the Gazette, 9I ; deeds of arrangement registered, respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were: Bankruptcies, 82; deeds of arrangernBel 88—total 170; being an increase of 1. The tot!,'„, number of commercial failures recorded during the weeks of the present year 156.815 : the total numb€2.l recorded in the corresponding 39 weeks of last year 6,816 showing a decrease of I.</p>
